      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>2 Gtaza, mcDouGALL's Pateo SELF-RAISING FLOUR -WOMAN'S REALM,</head>
        <p>2 Gtaza, mcDouGALL's Pateo SELF-RAISING FLOUR -WOMAN'S REALM,</p>
        <p>DRESS-FASHION-HOME.</p>
        <p>AGIt OF GREATEST MENTAL STRENGTH. Professor W. A.. Newman !Dorland s has</p>
        <p>HOLIDAY DRESSING-GOWN. gone into the records of achievements of the The ,fihmssing-gown 'depicted was made to take world's chieS, workers and thinkers, and finds sway br the holidays. It was fashioned in some that the average age for the performance of pretty green-and-white-spotted cambric, and the master work is 50. For the workers the was. quite dainty, enough to use as a breakfast- average is 4--*iftl gam at home. The skirt and bodice were made Chemists and physicists average the youngest for the thinkers 52.- separately. end joined in a narrow band at the waist, covered by a fixed belt ef soft green at 41; .poets and inventors at 44; novelists at ' 46.. explorers and warriors. 47; composers ribbon. The collar was cut' like a fichu, and and actors, 48; artists and clergymen, 50; essayists and reformers, 51; physicians and statesmen, 52; philosophers, 54; mathema- ticians and humorists, 56; historians, 57; naturalists' and jurists,. 58. Professor Dor- land concludes that if health and optimism remain "the man of 50 can command suc- cess as readily as the man of 30." _</p>
        <p>SOUNDING BY -SIGHT.</p>
        <p>erc.y3ed at the back as. well as the front, and was bordered with a bend of plain green zephyr, cut with-little pointed tabs at intervals. A band of the same trimming was carried down the front of the skirt, the fastenings being arranged beneath it. The opening at the neck is filled in with l,ac3 insertion, drawn up by a narrow rib- bon. The sleeves are full, and gathered into curial of the plain zephyr, cut with a long narrow tab up the inside seam of the sleeve.</p>
        <p>An ingenious device by which the depth of a swift river above a high fall in Ontario was ascertained has been described by Mr. H. W. Hixon in the Engineering and Min- ing Journal. It was too dangerous to make soundings from a boat, so Mr. Hixon planted a transit on one bank, and from it carried a strong wire, with a heavy_ lead weight at the end, to the oppceite shore. The wire was drawn taut, and the weight was gradually "pulled across on the bottom of. the river. At stated intervals a sight along the wire from the transit to the point where the wire dipped into the water gave the inclination of the straight line. ,* The length -of submerged wire and its angle with .the water surface being known, it was easy to calculate the depth at the various points.</p>
