      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>COLLARS AND RUFFLES.</head>
        <p>COLLARS AND RUFFLES.</p>
        <p>Neck-wear is important, not only at the sea-, Bide ant in the ecfuntry, but at all times, and the ingenuity of many minds is brought to bear upon producing an abundant variety. The little ruche round the neck is now considered almost indispensable to these, declares a 'Globe .writer. A turn-down collar in pleated fine batiste has rising.from beneath it a high collar with three pleats to match, headed with the little ruche and finished with a neat black 'satin bow and ends. Another pretty Mt of neck-wear has an Irish lace hind with high tucker of the same and a frill below the collar, pointed and longer in front than at, the back and sides. This "does up" beautifully, a groeft recommendation, for, fairly inexpensive as these small article are, they swcely pay for themselves unless they are of conisitution sufficiently robust to be washed at least four or five times. A very elaborate one is made of folded ribbon with feather trimming sewn into the under-side. This fastens at -the back w -th a bow of the ribbon. A very simple little collar has five tucks of white batiste in tho cenlre, with a band of lace top and bottom. From ttle middle fall four leaf-shaped pieces of the tucked batiste, each surrounded with an edging "A lace Matching that on sthe collar.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>SMART AFTEILNOOIP DRESS.</head>
        <p>SMART AFTEILNOOIP DRESS.</p>
        <p>Th tart afternoon dress depicted below is carried out in fine souple cloth in a delicate and pale shade of grey. The long clinging skirt is quite plain, and falls in folds at the feet. The sleeveless corsage is also of the cloth,_ and is quite pLain-fitting across the shoulders, the little square opening being turned back with revers and a small pointed tab in front, edged with ball fringe to match. The wide armholes are turned back and the edge cut out in scallops, the points being finished with a taffetas-covered button. The guimpe and sleeves are of tucked not, the latter having a band of insertion at the edge, and finished with long mitten cuffs of</p>
        <p>net. The collar is of sort grey ribbon with an up-and-down frill at the edges. The twisted ends of ribbon that appear from under the/ revers are finished with a small gold tassel. A sash of wider ribbokt is swathed round the waist, thi? knotted ends being finished with fringe. REST HOLIDAYS. Holidays spent entirely in the open air are to be the holidays par excellence of ISO 3, writes "Ilebe " in the Gentlewoman. The pace at which we live now is responsible for the really irritat- ing iteration of the word "rest:" I really do</p>
