      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>WIGAN F.C. AND THOMAS THOMAS.</head>
        <p>WIGAN F.C. AND THOMAS THOMAS.</p>
        <p>At -Liverpool Assizes on Wednesday, Mr. Justice Pickford awarded the Wigan Football Club ICO damages against Thomas Thomas, one of their playerg, for breach of egreercent.—The club sent a deputation to'Wales to see Memos ploy, and finding him a very promising foothaPer, he was raid £l2O to sign on for Wigan.—He played for a soason aud a half, and then failed to appear at certain matches or to 'explain his atsence. The action was undefended.</p>
        <p>EIFFEL TOWER LEMONADE. This article is undoubtedly the most delicious, cool, refreshing drink obtainable, so healthful, so convenient, so cheap-40. per bottle.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>The King when Visiting.—When the King elects to visit his st.hjects, a private telegraph wire has always to be available in tie house, this convenience being indispensable for State business. Then beside the Royal set cf looms, vest rccornracdation lies to ea forthcoming for the gentlemen in attendance, and Eeryant 5, taken by his Majesty. The King is always attended by his own erants. even at table his own footmen stand behind his chair and waits upon his Majesty exclusively. 'The Fairfax Peerage.—Lerd Fairfax. the American peer, is seeking to have his claim to the Fairfax Barony in the Peerage of Scotland, recognised by the Com- mittee of Privileges of the P.ouse of Lords. That would not enable him to sit in the Upper Chamber unless elected one of the Scottish Representative Peers for whom he would then be entitled to vote. He is a naturalised British subject, but for many generations the descendants of the fancons Parliamentary General of the civil war, hrve been citizens of the United States. The first Lord Fairfax, the Victor of Marston Moor, wished only to depose Charles T., and tried to prevent his execution, he was one of those who proceeded to Holland to ask °lath-a 11. to assume the Crown, and was allowed to keep his estates, but some of the family disgusted with the immorality of the Ccutt, withdrew to America. The wheels of the Committee of Privi- leges, like those of the Law Courts, move slowly, and it will probably take Lord Fairfax some time to establish his claim to the Barony. The'Earl of Lauderdale whose ancester we.s a prisoner of Cromwell for nine years in the Tower of London, and was afterwards a besern I friend of Charles H., has been occupied for fire years 1 in establishing his claim to the office of Hereditary 1 Standard Bearer for Scotland. That, having at last been decided in his favour, the Earl is entitled to carry the King's Standard, in the unlikely event of his Majesty havint, occasion and electing to lead his troops into I</p>
        <p>battle: A Boy's idea of the Sea.—Nowadays much is done I by the Sunday schools, and carious philanthropic organisations, in order to give, the slum children of the great cities a day by the sea. There are nevertheless many who never get as far, and have but shadowy notions as to what the sea is like. One small boy told a London masistrate the other day that he wanted to be a sailor, and that he had seen the sea. It turned out he had seen it from the unlovely scenic point of view cf the Thames Einbaraiment, and the dirty, evil smell- ing, and factory choked river was his conception of the sea. it would well repay some kind-hearted perEcn to show him the real thing, n erely to hear his first impressicns. Possibly they would be much the same as those of the ivorkhouse boy, of anecdote, Wee, when first shown the sea, said that it was the first time he had seen enough of anything. The Medical View of Tea and Tobncco.e-Tri the seventeenth century, tea and tobacco were both extolled as possessing many curative properties, and for keeping the world in "sprightly life and health." Boss eta school had to smoke as a safeguard against the plague, ' and there was scarcely any ailment, trifling or other- wise, for which either tea or tobacce was not said to be a remedy. To-day however, both herbs have fallen tinder the ban of medical opinion, as a fertile cause of neurosis, nervous breakdown, insomnia, and insanity. Physicians are constantly inweighing against the after- noon tea party as a fruitful cause of these evils, but it ! is not so much in the fashionable drawing rooms as in the houses of the pcorer classes that the deadly strong black tea is in favour. Our ancestors who had to pay six or eight times as much for their tea, had some excuse for " getting all the goodness out of it," but as a rule the educated classes have now learnt to make tea properly ; and to pour it off !TOM the leaves into another teapot before pllclug it on the table. Where that course is adopted there is no occasion for the physician's diatribes, and afternoon tea is such an indispensable social institution that it is useless for him him to ask us to give it up. Online of Beads.—The craze for chains of heads to match each toilette goes merrily on, lied leading dress- makers are now keeping cases tilled with all sorts and conditions of heads, frcra which their customers may choose, and necklaces be built up. Very large herds are utilised, and not infrequently a chain to correspond is wound round the lower part of the crown of the hat. The neck chain, to be correct, should be hang, reaching well to the waist in front. A Touch of Black.—Simple little coats for early autumn wear, are appearing in the showrooms, those carried out in beige, and pale grey satin, with black satin roll collars, and large pearl buttons, being especi- ally desirable.- The note of black makes considerably for smartness and it is a matter for rejoicing that tailors dressmakers, sad milliners alike, rice showing signs Of deeming it indispensable. A Test for Colours.—A capital plan whereby to find out whether a fabric is likely to fade or not is as follows :—Take a sheet of thick notepaper, and cut out several rings, squares, or oblongs. Then pin the paper en to a piece of the material it is desired to test and lay it in the sun for several hours. If, when the paper is removed there are ,to rings, squares, or oblongs, visible upon the fabric, then one may safely rely upon the coleus being permanent. Uncomplimentary Proverbs.—A recent lecture on "Old sayings and beliefs" recalls the fact that proverbs as a rule do not flatter the fair sex. They are meetly of masculine authorship and not a fem speak of women with contempt. There are scores and hundreds of pro- verbs which reflect on the feminine tongue, and ignore the fact that, whilst there is some excuse for wcmen, there is little or none for the vast numbers of men who are given to "tittle tattle," and are as arrant gossips as any wearers of petticoats. Women's tears form another subject of which not a few proverbs regard with unfeeling contempt, but it is in the matrimonial proverbs that the abuse of women is carried to its greatest lengths. " Blame, blame, but never praise " may be said to be their keynote, and in country places where old modes of thought as well as old forms of speech still linger, there is much belief en the part of the lords of creation, in the assumed wisdom of these old sayings, such us, for instance the ore which refers to a dog, a woman, and a walnut tree, and ecelares "...The more you beat 'em, the better they be." Housekeeping Problems.—There are said to be 50,000 empty houses in London, and the inference is that' people will net live in hous.es that require a good deal of work such as those with basements and several nights of stairs. Everywhere the tendency is tcetry eral uinrni he diliic'alties of modern heosetesping, 11114 altiiough there are objections to living in flats., as a I rule they are more easily managed than a house. Sooner or later we shall come to more or less dispense with carpets, hangings, and rooms overcrowded with furniture, and ornaments all requiring constant work and attention if they are to he kept in a state of cleanliness. The test way of meeting the ever- increasing difficulty of obtsining satisfactory servants is to redece needless work and be as far as possible independent of them. Devotion to household duties Mote often than not simply means domestic drucleery rather than so-called Weal life for women, and it is therefore not surprising that there is a general tendency en the part of housewives to avoid houses that require a lot of work to keep in order.</p>
        <p>MAD GE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>DON'T BE TOO FAT-BE COMFORTABLE.</head>
        <p>DON'T BE TOO FAT-BE COMFORTABLE.</p>
        <p>If you are tco fat jest go to the chemist and find ort ally so many yeopte are talking about Mairaoki. Take some yourself and watch your figure getting slighter and slighter daily until you are comfortably thin. Then tell other fat folks about it. Remember the full prescription, Loa-ever :—One half-ounce of Marmola,one onnce of fluid extract of Glycyrrhiza D.P. one our.ce of pure Gi3cerine 8.?., and Peppermint Water to make six cunce3 in all ; dose, coo dessert- spoonful after meals and before bedtime. While you are taking 111armokt 3on can eat and drink what you like and do es little es convenient in the way of work or exercise, but you will keep en getting thin until your normal figure is recovered and you step taking the medicine.</p>
