this ease was guessing. • Boddington's Breweries, Ltd., Strangeways Brewery, of 1 She got no further. Mr. Oxenham grew__ What they were no one really knew. which his father was formerly proprietor. He was alarmingly apoplectic. He tore the Times in I In the week's itinerary they took in The Iborn in 1853, and was admitted as a solicitor when half and danced on the frogments. And he 'Laurels, Mr.. Oaenham's hired house, at nine twenty-two years of age. In July of last year Mr. strunled for b'reath and articulation, . and o'clock. They gave their little entertainment , Boddington was elected president of the Manchester' looked for all the world as excited and per- on Monday and Wednesday, in the first week, Law Associatipn, of which he had been a member ever' turbed as a trout suddenly tugged out of its to the ) delight of the whole household, and ! since his admission as a solicitor, and he was one of the , accustomed haunts in the c Ishadows fM Oxenham, co o the r. pleased to ,give his wife and ' patrons and a consistent supporter of the Manchester _,___. - ~ - Law Clerks' Friendly Society. Mr. Boddington was ''''`,';"",,' daughter some little entertainment, liberally ' one of the directors of the" Manchester Coinier," Ltd. I Go to your room," he said, sternly, subscribed. ' , and of the British Law Fire Insurance Company. , when at length he recovered his voice. , 1 . Still there was no appearance of Fred, and Mr. Boddington went to live at Thelwall earl', in the I Mrs. Oxenham came in at that moment. no- letter. Kate began to droop and grow present year. Prior to -that he lived at Eccles, where, With characteristic impetuosity, he ex- despondent. Her mother grew more end he was closely identified as a Conservative. with plained. , . molt irritable, and more inclined to revolt. political life and work in the division. 1 "Kate says that young idiot, Fred Free- Stephen gl•ew slightly more complacent, and Mr. Boddington married a daughter of Mr. Joseph ride, proposed to her last night. I won't the discipline relaxed a little as' suspicious Walker, a prominent business man. They celebrated have it-I tell you I won't have it!" conditions ceased to present themselves. '.hei- -elver wedding five years ago, and Mrs.Boddington , -- - • eked sd. • I,• On the Saturday. the ',mysterious minstrels</p>
        <p>" this is</p>
        <p>Mrs. Oxenhari looked surprised. . —n the Sa.,...day, ___ys, "After all," she said, does it matter? appeared on the lawn about 8.30. Their only Fred is in every way desirable." audience was Kate; 'Mr. and Mrs. Oxenham "I won't have it ! ' the old man shouted, had dared an entertainment on the pier:// "I won't hate it!" he added, banging his The three masked figures took up their list on the table till the cups rattled; "I places and trolled the opening chorus. Then won't have it!" he literally screamed as he the tenor began a popular ballad. Kate roughly put his. hat on in the hall; "I won't watched 'them with interest. The baritone have it!" he said, as. he banged the door to came towards the open windotv with a collect- and strode across the lawn on his way to the ing-plate, office. . ! Will you patronise our entertainment ? " "It seems to mo,". thought Mrs. Oxenham, he asked, his eyes twinkling through the es she looked into her daughter's eyes half- mask. an-hour later, "Stephen will have to accept Kate put a few coppers on the plate. ", I one Freeside at least." But Mrs. Oxenham am sorry it is not more; papa is out," she discreetly took no sides, though the girl and added, in explanation. . her mother drew closer together in the "Is he ? " said the baritone. Quickly he shadow of the lore interest. . slipped off the slouched hat, which draNed Oxenham, at the office, spent the whole away the mask as well. morning composing an insolent letter to his - "Fred!" Kate screamed, delightedly.</p>
        <p>rival Freesicle. He; denounced his enemy, he "Let me in," he said. denounced his son, he denounced everythirig "How jolly !" Kate exclaimed, as they that Was theirs. , He added that nothing on exchanged greetings in, the hall. Outside the. earth would persuade him- to sanctim such a tenor was singing rapturously to the still union, and that he would do . everything that evening. .. - was possible under heaven to .prevent it cm-- "He will keel) it up until I get back," lag about.. He further explained that if ~be Fred said. , saw young Freeside anywhere near ' his "Mother is tired 'of this foolishness," said premises or his girl, lie would consider hiM-,. Kate, after their greetings were over; "she I self justified in shooting or horsewhipping 'is on our side." him on sight. Moreover, he sent the trucu- . "All the better," Fred said; "we'll end lent letter to his business enemy. - it at once. We might elope next Friday iFred sals he never heard is father, laugh evening." so much at one time in his lifd. He received "Oh ! I couldn't," she said. the note the next morning with his other letters, as he sat at breakfast. Fred heard "Of course you could; I'll shew . you. There's the cart and my. friends. We can the contents with misgiving, and it precipi• 1 Icome as singers about 9.30, in this make-up tated tiatters. . You pack your boxes, and give the signal "I've seen the lass," Freesia. explained, " anc I think she'll do. If she has-half the about 9.40. Over goes the piano, on go the boxes and ourselves, and hey! for the pertinacity of her father in caring for you station and the 9.50, which just catches the that lie has in hating me, she -will have cha- mail at Preston for town." 1.- ,ac tor, at all events. And, after all, as wo Kate pouted. "It seems *very easy." she 1 'lorkshiremen say, 'it's dogged as does it.' " mu ; " nut you are thinking only of your- F-ed saw the trend of the old man's mind, a I.dvantsqe of it, . tru , " Don't you e 4 me, sweetheart I