      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>PHOTOGRAPHIC NOTES.</head>
        <p>PHOTOGRAPHIC NOTES.</p>
        <p>By Veronica.</p>
        <p>DEATH OF MR. W. S. BODDINGTON.</p>
        <p>town, and get her to' write to your mater: Then she can meet you, and we will b 3. married by special licence the next day." "I think mother might not object so much if Ohe had such an assurance. She and you aunt in town were .great friends." " It is funny to elope with the consent of your mother-in-law," Fred -added, with a - grin. "Will you ?'" "Yes," she said, softly, after a tong pause.</p>
        <p>. - The slouched hat and the mask went on once more, and Fred went back to his com- pany, treating Kate to a parting serenade, a rollicking baritone song of a lover ' and his lass ' who ran away to Gretna Green, when post-chaises nem fashionable. They were finishing a liort entertainment when Mr. and Mrs. Oxenham returned.. The three men saluted them, and Fred proffered the plate, to which old Stephen made generous contribution. _</p>
        <p>Then they disappeared. The plot thickened very quickly. Fred wrote to his aunt and explained. His aunt entered eagerly into the spirit of the adven- ture, and wrote to Fred and to Mrs. Oxen- ham by return, promising just the assistance that would remove the last scruple from the mind of Mrs. Grundy. The mysterious musicians performed on the lawn on Monday night,. and the :note from Fred's aunt, with full explanatory sug- gestions, was smuggled to Kate during the entertainment. ,The directions were that if the elopement were to go on there should a vase of' red roses in the window overlook- ing the lawn on Wednesday. - Mrs. Oxenham, after nutch misgiving, ap- proved. When the mysterious minstrels again appeared on Wednesday evening, there was a bowl of red roses in the creeper- covered window overlooking the lawn. "1' have never heard those fellows in such, fine form," Stephen said 'to his wife, when they had gone. "That baritone fellow seemed in great spirits. Mrs. Oxenham bent over her sewing, and blushed like the roses in the bowl. "Didn't he " she said. . __</p>
        <p>_ Friday came. Mr. Oxenham was in great feather. Another week had gone—no letter —no Fred. He had won, after all. Perhaps, now that be had vindicated himself, he might: consider the suggestion for Kate's sake, ho was beginning to think. . At 9r30 the mysterious musicians al:rived,. and struck up their Bohemian chorus. ' "Well, good-bye, darling," the mother whispered upstalrs. "It's 7 a strange wedding for an Oxenham p but it will be all for the best, I hope." "Of Course it will, mother dear,' she. answered. "Hurry to town, and you'll be in time for the wedding breakfast. Don't for, get—the Had Russell, 2.30, to-morrow." They kissed. Dora fluttered a handker. chief through the window upstairs. Mrs. Oxenham tripped down, aud called her hus- band into the dining-room. ' It was the work of seconds. Two light dress-baskets dropped from the Window. One of the mysterious entertainers caught them. The other upset the piano on to the lawn. • A third stood waiting at the door, from. which a breathless, bright,eyed girl quickly tripped.</p>
        <p>4'l3race little girl ! " he said, as he hugged her to him. "At once, dearie'." She took her seat in the trap. One of the men climbed on behind. The, .other, taking off his hat and mask, threw a note on to the table at the window. Fred took the reins,</p>
        <p>"Let her go,' he said, grimly,, flicking the horse. They set off at fult speed down the quiei road, and five minutes later were in the train for Preston.</p>
        <p>Stephen 'Oxenham wondered at the disap- pearance, of the musicians so hurriedly with- out a call for a contribution. He vvondeved still more when he saw the overturned piano; the cloak, the mask, and the hat on the lawn. And then he jumped to the conclusion. There was no anger in his heart. Only bitterness that his daughter should have been driven to this underhand method of leaving him by his own spleen. "Margaret—Margaret ! " be cried. His wife came running down the hall.- " They've gone—they've gone ! "Surely—not without a line or a word? " she said.</p>
        <p>"Yes—yes." In his pain he stumbled into the drawing-room. It v,:as empty. He sat wearily on a chair. His wife noticed the, letter;</p>
        <p>"See—she must have gone 1." he said. "Kate is with me," the note briefly ran. "If you can forgive us, come to the Hotel Russell for ,breakfast-2 30. Shi, is safe, but • , we shall be married before you can find 1 us. Take it pleasantly, as a good move, at your avn game, by the youngsters.-=Fred." Ozenham fretted and fumed, inquired and wired, but all to no purpose. They caught the train, and he did not. - At eleven ho Made up his mind to the inevitable. His wife was anxiously awaiting him when he returned. There was a changed, softened look on his grim. Yorkshire face. "These lovers—these lovers ! " he said.</p>
        <p>"Heaven bless them !" his wife her eyes shining to his. "We, too, were young, deari and lgrers, too." "Yet," he corivected, gently—" besides, L might have known Fred would be as obsti- aate as his father.'? "Or you, dear ? " she suggested, with a smile.</p>
        <p>They travelled at an uncannily early hour to town, and reached the Hotel Rtraseil at one. Jacob Freeside greeted them 'in the</p>
        <p>"I've -retired from the quarrel," he said, putting out his hand. "Fred has taken my share in the game, and he is sure to win—he is younger." . Stephen shook the offered hand. - "It's peace," he said. "If he takes my daughter in the first round, I had better give in." . . .</p>
        <p>The breakfast-party at 2.30 was a merry one.. There were the bride and bridegroom, one very proud, one very shy, and both very happy. There were the parents of the bride and bridegroom, and there were the aunt and a young daughter, who had ,filled the breach as bridesmaid.</p>
        <p>Stephen and Jacob toasted everyone gene- . rously, and each other. - "'lO love and lovers," Stephen proposed. ‘. And the- mysterious music that makes U 3 young again." Jacob clinked his glass with Stephen's, and they looked with frank kindness int ) eash other's .eyes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>THEFT FROM A COLLIERY.</head>
        <p>THEFT FROM A COLLIERY.</p>
        <p>At St. Helens, on Monday, a labourer named John Summers, of Zittleis-court, was charged with stealing two pieces of timter from Ashton's Green Colliery, the property of Messrs. Bromilow, Foster, and Co.—Police- constable Trani met the prisoner coming from the colliery that morning carrying two pieces of timber. Knowing he had teen working in the colliery, he allowed' him to pass, after which he went to the colliery and found the titnber had been stolen, lie then went to the prisoners house, and on questioning him he admitted having stolen the timber. It was stated that a great deal of coal and timber had been taken from the colliery lately. It was stated that tha prisoner had teen con- victed three times for theft, and he was fined 10e. 63.</p>
      </div>
