      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>s THE MAN REVEALED.</head>
        <p>s THE MAN REVEALED.</p>
        <p>BY ANNIE 0. TIBBITS, ,Author of "False Heart and True," " Tho Übon. Swindle," "The Shadow Between," "Her Last Adventure," "Under bus- picion,"</p>
        <p>1. Ii thc small room at the top of a house .in one of the most squalid parts of the east of London three men • stood facing. each other. Two of them had cunning, •treacherots eyes, and rough, reddened faces, blotched with drink. The third looked different. He was dark and determined, with a keen, •Unpleasant face, and thin lips. Unlike the 'others, who were scantily clothed, he wore a -shabby overcoat, buttoned up to the chin, and at the edge of the sleeves could be seen the gleaming white cuffs of his shirt. He Was not,an old man, and the other two were near. his _own age, but he was master of them in spite of their . threatening attitudes. One of then' leant forward with a snarl.</p>
        <p>"It won't do, Dick 'Sledger," he said, savagely. "You're not going to leave us in I the lurch. You've got to stick to us." ' "Dick Sledger" lifted his head. His thin lips were oddly set, and neither of them had ever seen him look quite so determined. . " You'll do as I say," he said, quietly. ,"Everything is ready and in order, and ifi three days I'll meet you here for the last time, and divide the jewels. The next coup You can arrange yourselves." . The two men hesitated. There was some- thing in Dick Sledger's face they did noelike -to rouse, and his eyes made cowards of them, As they had done for years before. Dick Sledger was master—always would be master as long as he chose, but now that he wanted them no longer their tiger blood was roused. "I tell you you can't get out of us like :That," one of them said, hoarsely. "If you want to get rid of us you must buy us off." Dick Sledger laughed. "I shall do nothing of the kind," he said. 'You've had your fair share of the spoil. You've had your chance. I've arranged - everything, planned everything, and taken all the risk of disposing of the jewels, and you must be content." - The man crept forward. "Yes, that's all very well," he said. "But Who's done the work, I should like to know? Why, me and Bill. Who is it as has crept into the houses and done the jobs? Why, me and Bill; and it's easy enough to talk o' -"akin' risk when you've bin hanging about outside doing nothing, only waiting for me and Bill; and we might have been 'ad up at any time and handed over to the Coppers. A lot o' risk in that. And you're not goin' to 'give it up now." lie jerked his head with an ugly look at 'the man who still stood by the little grimy table, resolute and unmoved. One hand rested upon it, and on it a diamond gleamed fiercely in the dim candle-light. Bill pointed . -to it savagely. "Oh, yes, there's a lot o' risk," he cried, 'when you can wear that as brazen as brass. Why, it's one o' them we took from West- 'vood Park, I'll bet ! " Dick Sledger moved it a little. It shot out st sharp, ugly light. 1 "It is, he said. "People never see things that are directly under their, nose, as I told you over and over again—the safest I.place for this is on my finger. Now, are yesii ready to go?" The man hesitated again. " I'v given you instructions," Dick Sledger went on calmly, "and the robbery Will be perfectly easy, and five thousand 'Pounds' worth of jewels and plate at the end. I'll meet you here on Thursday and divide everything equally, as it is the last time, and still take the risk of disposing of - them. Now, are you ready to go, Jim ? ' "It ain't the last time," burst out t. 'man, angrily. "It's all very well for you. 'You've done well out of it; but 'look at us! Why, I heard that you'd got a big house somewhere in the West End, and a horse ; and carriage and servants, and a yacht, and g, l'm end of money. You're all right." ' , q 'lndeed ? " Dick Sledger lifted his head: .1 ‘,„‘ But that has nothing to do with' it. We 1 nave worked together for ten years' and you might have been rich enougo if you had , saved your money instead of gambling it r -away. You can't complain that I have been unfair to you or that- I have got you into any i, [ scrape, We've scarcely had half-a-dozen rob- beries that- failed; and, then, you weren't caught—l took care of that; I prepared for everything, and arranged everithi.Sg—" Re broke off suddenly and impatiently. " But I am not here. to argue," he added, quickly. "I can't stop any longer. And 1 licw, before you go,' you understand-,.-nextThurSday will be the last time I meet you." He waved his hand tov:ards the door, and irretolutely the men., moved towards it. Even now they could not disobey him. ).;• Jim hesitated, however. "We don't even ' ow your address," he -began. nick Sledger cut him short. "It's not , . necessary," he said, sternly. "Go ! " •./ They stumbled forward, slouching to the •' - cloor—beirt, ungitinly, evil men. Jim turned ' 48 he twisted the handle in his hand, and .-- looked back. His face was savage and Malignant, and "Dick Sledger" shuddered . 48 he met his eyes. - 'Oh, we shall meet on Thursday," Jim snarled ; "but if you mean to kick us off , .. You'll be sorry for it." Ile flung him a last savage look, and • liaturned the door. . Sledger listened to his footsteps dragging ,- d• own the dark stairs, with his face liet and bard, and then he began pulling off his over- coat. -- , The last time," he muttered to himself. " 4, Yes, it will be the last time. It's getting 'd• angerous ; and besides—" " His thoughts broke e, and something odd flnd hard crept into his throat. Before him, ' Ili the dust of the garret, with its packing- , 'eases and broken stools and chairs, he leen:Led to see a girl's bright face. He turned away. "I'll end it all," he muttered. " She must never know, poor little girl—poor little . He tore off his shabby overcoat, and 'Pushed it hurriedly into one of the packing- ' '4BeS. taking from it another—of good ~' broadcloth—and a shiny top - hat. ` When he put them on he looked . ' kiifferent man—he looked a "swell." Re opened the door, and listened :autiously. Then he took out a key, locked 4t, and crept downstairs. Outside was a little narrow street, known 43 Stoney-lane. He looked -up, at the rows 4f sordid houses, at the one gas lamp , flickering a yard or two away, and then , tarted back. ' Two figures were slouching up the dismal street—Bill and Jim—and on the opposite ' side, in the shadow, was another figure, evi- dently following them. , "Dick Sledger" peered out, and 'his face grown a little white. it 1 A detective ! " he muttered; "and good :. leavens ! Geoffrey Grey! It is time I left e. 4a—it is time I left off ! " ! Ile drew back into the shadow and waited. 44(1 then, when the three figures had rounded , the corner, be crept out cautiously . Itlad disappeared. 11. , ~ zt was a charming room—warm and luxn- -414118, with rich hangings and curtains, and bright rose -pink lamp burning in thc rc,;:intre. The room was in strange contrast to I' gle sordid glow of the public-house Geoffrey \pre I ~, , uy, the 'detective, had just left, and he Aed with content as his feet touched the to-oft carnet and hurried forward towards a 841 who rose up to meet him. 1.. Their hands met, and tne light in his eyes ^clalitened. ' . ht,,, 1- ant very sorry lam late," he said. • 1 Pe e Yonill forgiVe me, hui I thought I had II sort of clue to sue Westwood robbery, and "• co °Quid not resist following it up. It didn't • horrlr c. to anything exactly; and it led me to a ,- rible hole of a place called Stoney-lane, it made me late here. Please excuse mo ' t °nee Miss Marsham." it - , raihe smiled in his face. She was a slip of a a white evening frock—the premeas to Geoffrey Grey—and RS ho across the room to the fire he</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>1</p>
        <p>bad no eyes for the man who sat in an easy.; chair close beside it. He was too busy 100 k-1 ing at Eva Marshain to notice, but the man was watching him curiously. His uharp, keen face put on an alert expression. "You are a bit late, my boy," he said, as they shook hands, ' but if you've made it right with Eva I don't count. NOvv sit down I and tell me the 'news."</p>
        <p>Geoffrey dropped into a chair at his side. "'here isn't any in pacticular," he said; "but I'm late to-night because I have beea spending the afternoon in following up a clue to the Westwood robbery, and it made mo late in dressing; and it didn't come to any- thing after all.' Richard Marsham leant back in his chair and joined the tops of his fingers. A big diamond glistened uneasily on one of them. "You'll never get them," he said. "The robbery was well planned and well carried out, and you'll never catch the thieves. Be- sides, three months has passed, and they have had time to get away." Geoffrey starcd at the fire. his face, eager and boyish, . scarcely seemed the face of a detective, and Mr. Marsham puckered his brows as he looked at him. •</p>
        <p>"Yes, but you know," Geoffrey said, "a criminal always fails somewhere, and you have only got to watch long enough to catch him. He may go on for years, but his sin will find him out at last. No man it clever enough to fight Fate. He .will find that Fate will put him into the hands of the police sooner or later. Dishonesty doesn't pay for long. Oh, no; a thief comes to the end of his tether some time or other, and he gene- rally has a bad time of it when he does. And these Westwood thieves will be caught yet. I think I can lay my band on one of them. I'm not sure, but I think I can.'! _ _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. Marsham looked up suddenly, and, with an abrupt movement, clasped his hand over . the diamond on his little finger. , His face was strangely grey. "Oh ? " he said. "The leader of the gang is a man named Sledger," GeofNey went on—" Dibk Sledger they call him—and if I can only lay my hands on him I shall get the :whole lot. It was be- cause I thought I had a clue to his where- abouts that 1 went to Stoney-lane to-night. I didn't get him, though." Mr. Marsham sat upright in his chair. He looked suddenly old , and haggard in the shaded candle - light, and he was staring at Geoffrey with 'startled eyes. If Geoffrey had been (looking he might have noticed that his lips were twitching, and his hands trembling; but he was looking at Eva, - and he 'was scarcely thinking of his ,case, but of her. • Mr. Marsham looked at him oddly for a moment, and there got up hurriedly. "You won't 'catch them," he said, stand- ing with his back to the light. ." You can't catch them. They've covered their tracks by this, and it will impossible to convict them.' Geoffrey looked tip. I'll bet you I do, ir you like!" he said. "I know where they are—l'm pretty nearly certain of catching them, and then when I do—"</p>
        <p>11-o-oked back at Eva, and then turned again suddenly and faced Mr. Marsham. When I do," he added, "it will mean pro- motion, and a road to success;' and—and— I'm not penniless, as you know, and Mr. Marsham, if succeed in catching the Westwood thieves, will you give me your daughter for my wife? We've waited the six , months you stipulated for, and I—l want her very badly, Mr. Marsham ! " He put out his hand to the girl, .and she slipped hers into it. Richard Marsham stared for a moment, with a strange, grey, twitching face. The luxu- rious room, the bright lamp and the soft ' lounges, the ornaments and tho flowers and the smiling face of the • girl grew blurred for a Moment. Then he burst out suddenly into fierce,- irregular laughter. • " " I—l, good heavens! Give y.ou—my girl—for catching--' The strange laughter • seemed to choke him. His voice, which had risen almost-to a ' scream, died away again, and he turned / abruptly and hurried from the room. Eva turned, pale and trembling, to Geoffrey. I "Geoff, Geoff! what is the matter ' with him ? " she cried. " I don't know what tc make of him lately—he has been so , strange and odd. He looks worried and ill, and he is changing so. Oh! Geoffrey, he isn't happy —not as happy as he was years ago, when we were poor, and had to do, all sorts of things to make two- ends meet. He was jolly then— father . was, and we had good times, even though he was sometimes nearly starving. But noW'--" She broke off. "Oh! what is the matter With him now " she added. Geoffrey looked down at her. He didn't know. Richard Marsnain puzzled him, and there was a curious, uneasy shadow in his eyes as he turned his face away from her. "I'll "and and see what is the matter," she added, and bring him back. I don't be- lieve he can be well. He frightens me. She slipped from tile room—a dainty little figure in her soft white dress, and Geoffrey leant back in his chair, staring undornfort- ably at the red coals. Later on, after dinner, the two men faced each .other over wine and cigars. Richard Marsh= had been strange and moody all through dinner; and now he sat, ashy pale, with his lips set and his eyes dark. Once their hands touched, anti Geoffrey was' startled to find Mr. Marsham's ' icy cold. Now he lit a cigar, and drew, a chair close to the dining-room fire. "I want to talk to 'ou," he e said, "just for a minute before we go to Eva. I want to ask you—do you really believe thieves and People like that get found out, and have a bad time of it, after all 1" Geoffrey drank off his wine without look- ing at Mr. Marsham's face. I'm certain of it," •he said; "every crime is found out sooner or later, however clever the criminal may be." Mr. Marsham fidgeted with his cigar. "And you think you are going to catch, the Westwood thieves ? " he asked. Geoffrey nodded. "I've got a good clue, and I think next week I shall be able to lay my hands on this Dick Sledger. It is 'he who is the ringleader—a gentleman, ap- parently, living in style, and arranging things for other men ,to carry out. By next week I hope to have discovered who he is, and then—" "And then " Mr. Marsham's voice, odd' and shrill, broke in. "And then," he cried— "what then? Ah ! au ! au ! my boy, you won't get him. You won't catch him! But look here."</p>
        <p>His manner changed suddenly and strangely, and he sat upright, leaning for- ward a little to touch Geoffroy on the arm. "I wanted to speak to you about Eva," he said: "When you first asked me for her I bade- you wait, didn't I? Well, now, arc you , sure you love her—above everything— beyond everything? Are you sure you love her so much that you care for nothing be- sides her 1"</p>
        <p>Geoffrey was. "Supposing sham asked. money 7"</p>
        <p>she was poor " Mr. Mar- " Suppose I could ,give her no</p>
        <p>"I don't care how poor</p>
        <p>„ Geoffrey laughed. she is,” he cried. "But if there was some disgrace attaching to her, what then " Marsham asked. ^ Geoffrey hesitated for al bare second, and then lifted his head. "It would not touch her," he said, proudly. Mr. Marsham locked at him—an odd look, and. then turned. "Well, I have something to tell you about her," he said, slowly. "I sin afraid it will startle you, and yet some day you. may be thankful. She—she is not my daughter—not my child, after all." Not your child ' ,Geoffrey fell back, and stared with open eyes at Mr. Marsham. He was sitting upright, his white hand out- stretched, and the great diamond upon his finger gleaming sharply. He stared straight before him. "No," he said, slowly, in a quiet little voice, "not my child, after all, but the child of a women I knew. I adopted.' her years ago. We were very poor—it was a horrible struggle. You don't know what it is, Geoff- rey, to struggle. against poverty. .You don't know what it means not to be able to find bread for your child; to watch her day by day, and see her getting thinner and whiter, and all the time people round yoir wasting money—oh! heaven knows it's a bitterness a man never rorgets. And you are young, Geoffrey, and you don't know the temptation —the hideous temptation that -assails. a mau then—when his 'child is !star v g, I "</p>
        <p>... 1 i,r .. 3 t , . . . , .. , ',' . . ' . , , ,1 t. 1. I/.</p>
