      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <p>J.</p>
        <p>His voice broke and stopped, and he sat staring into the fire in slier ,e for a moment. . . .. • _ _ .</p>
        <p>:" I san understand," Geoffrey said, quietly. He waited; fingering his wineglass and staring down at the shining white table- cloth. and the glittering silver upon it. He wanted to ask questions about Eva, but some- how he could not just then. "You've never experienced •it," Mr. /Tar- sham cried almost • fiercely. " You don't .know what it is to want bread as I did—brend for the child—not my . child, but the child- I loved and would have cUed for at any time. You don't know what it is; and now—"</p>
        <p>He broke - off again !disconnectedly, and passed his hand acro..s his face. "Now, of course, I am rich„!' he added, almost bitterly. "Rich—and as miserable as though I didn't know where my next meal was earning from." He laughed harshly. "Bat as long as she is all right," he went on, " I don't care. I want her to be happy, Geeffrey, and you'll see that she is, won't you? '.' Geoffrey. held out his hand. "I'd die to save her -a moment's Unhappiness," he! cried. Mr. Marsham looked steadily into his face. "I believe you," he said. • "1 believe you! shall feel her safe in your hands. And if ever you learn my secret, Geoffrey, will you kce? it?"</p>
        <p>He looked with strange, dim eyes into the young man's face. Geoffrey looked puzzled. "A secret, sir?" he asked. "I don't understand; but- I'd do anything for her sake !" *Mr. Marsham grasped his hand again. "Yes, yes," he criecl • "for her sake--for her sake, Geoffrey. She has made life worth living for me—keep my secret for her sake. And—and—marry her, Geoffrey—Marry ber soon, and keep her safe and happy. It is ray lust request.' -</p>
        <p>Geoffrey got his clue at last, and on Thurs- day he was stationed outside tl:e dismal house in Stoney-lane. Entering, he and his mon crept quietly up the narrow stairs and waited outside a dpor on the top landing. No sound of voiees came from within, , ancl Geoffrey's heart began to beat uncomfort- ably. It looked as if he had been mistaken. The room, was still, and, he thought .at first, empty. The packing - eases stood round the walls, and under the grimy table in the centre. On it was a .guttering candle. Geof- frey took it in his fingers and held •it up and looked round.</p>
        <p>The shadows danced as the light flickered over the ,dirty walls and up to the blackened ceiling: A breath Of wind blowing through from a hole above their heads beat back the flame, and he lowered it quickly and peered round. The 'room was about as filthy as it could be. It smqlt foul, and on the floor, in a corner, was a dark heap of rubbish. , He went forward, holding the candle so that the light flickered over it, and then he drew back with a quick indrawing .of his breath. It was no rubbish—no ,bundle- which which -lay there,:but the huddled body of, a man.' He started forward and leant over if, stooping to his knees and locking down at the grey face. He lowered the candle to the floor,, and, as he did so, the light flickered hp, sending moving shadows across the silent figure. • 1 It was the body of a man in shabby, M- atting clothes, apparently 'dead but, as Geoffrey touched him, he groaned, and tried to move. He opened his eyes, and then Geof- frey started back. "Mi. Marsham t" he cried.</p>
        <p>Mr. Marsham stretched out his hand feebly. On his finger the diamond still glit- tered—the Westwood diamond!</p>
        <p>"Are we alone ? " he asked, in a breath- less voice. ." I want to speak to you—alone." Geoffrey waved his men back. Mr. 31arsbani waited a moment, then drew a deep breath. "I want to speak," he said. haven't many minut,..t. . I seem to have lain hero hours; but I shOuld , have waited for you to come however long it was. I could not die until I had seen you—a.nd I knew —I knew you would come." , • "But what on earth does this mean ? " cried Geoffrey. "It msans," said Mr. Marcham, slowly and painfully, "than you have caught. the West Wood thief.. lam the Westwood thief. Geoffrey, I want you to 'understand—to understand—"</p>
        <p>His voice trailed away weakly. The ebbing Life was taking with it his power of speech. "You love Eva," he Went on after a 'minute, "and it can't make any difference— she is not my daughter—she—she can't —in- herit my sins; but I want you to knew, be- fore I die,' what I am—a thief, Geoffrey—a big thief! I have grown •rich, and I thought I should never be caught. I thought that I nould- leave off when I had had enough. B 4 I couldn't, after all, Geoffrey; and to-night I have been shot by bne Of my own men. told them you were on our track, and they thought I had betrayed them; but I had And I want you to promise, Geoffrey, that you will drop the Westwood 1.-hatter. Let my men go. The jewels you can recover. I have Left .11 paper telling you,-where they are, and you will find my will at home. Do. with it what you like. Perhaps, after. all, my money is ill-gotten, and will not bless her—my little sue. But you will let my men pi, Geoffrey— you will give them .a chance. Perhaps they. may be honest—after I ,have gone. Perhaps they. will learn that you were right. shistide tloes overtake; dishonesty doesn't pay. But, Geoffreylet them o." Geoffrey - promised. He was dazed and horror•struck at the confession—dazed, al- though a certain haunting fear had _been with him of late. That Mr. Marsham . had known something about the West Wood robbery he had guessed—but this— He was sick at heart. • _</p>
        <p>"I must get a doctor," he said, suddenly, and turned to rise to his feet. Mr. Marsham held him back.</p>
        <p>• "Wait," he said, feebly. "Just a inoraent —promise me this, too—that Eva 8611 not know—who I am "</p>
        <p>" I promise " Geoffrey's hand went slowly towards the )ne cn which the Westwocd diamond glittered evilly—a shining eye. mocking the squalor. of the plate. Mr. Marsham's fingers closed eagerly. over his young ones. • Thank you, kleoffrey. Heaven bless you—bless you both—my little Eva—" His voice died, then rose again in rambling incoherence. "No one knows the bitterness of poverty," .he cried. "No one know's what it is to see her .starvo—so hard —other children—too much foed—but she —starving—l can't stand it—l am going mad, but I must do something—Steal? Ali! No one will know . it is for Eva—my little Eva— Kate!"</p>
        <p>He raised himself slirhlenly en his elbow. The candle was, gntiering on the floor. Mk eyes were fixed eagerly, hungrily, at sorneone beyond it—someone Geoffrey could not see— someone who was not there.</p>
        <p>Kate!" 'he whispered again.. eagerly. "You have come to me at last. Don't, turn away from me—l havb done my best. I—l --it was for Eva's sake—to save her—for. give!" He fell back heavily, and Geoffrey bent hurriedly over him. He snatched up the candle, with the wax running in burning lumps over his fingers, and looked down. Mr. Marsham was still, and scored on his face Geoffrey saw the agony of years. Would he be forgiven? It was hard to say; but he had suffered for his crime, anyhow, and Geoffrey covered his face gently before he got up.</p>
        <p>• • Later. when the newspaper -Tele full of the description of the murderea thief, it was hard to keep it away from Eva.; but Geoffrey managed it somehow, and all that she ever understood was that her father had been killed, and that she must not question. "Trust me, darling—trust me," Geoffrey cried. "It is your father's wish—that you sio not see him now, and I want you to come away—to my mother. It will be best, darling." She looked up, white and worn, into his face, that had grown strangely. hs,s,-gard in those few hours, and, put her hand in his. "I'll do whatever you say. Geoff," she whispered. And in a short time she was safely deposited with his 'mother, and knew nothing of the trampling feet in her father's 'house, of the curious faces of detectives, who stared at the luxury Marsham had heaped together for her. " She knew nothing of . any- thing, except that all Geoffrey MO her ratner had wished; and, though he explained who she was, she never knew the dead man's secret that he had promised to keep.</p>
