        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, August 13 1908. . i A ROMANCE OF RODEN I hands and coyeringthem with kisses. " Tell me now, • I here, before your friend returns, if you love me. I --- - will keep it a secret as long as you desire, but tell me, By Thomas Saunders. , only tell me if you 1 rye me.' l " Well, if you must know," said the woman in a Frank Failsworth lay on his bed thinking. It was louder key, " I will tell you." But before she could / , a beautiful moonlight night towards the end of Juno, ' give further utterance Frank Failsworth had detected lend as he gazed through the window he could see that the voice Ile heard now was not the voice which !a large horse chesnut tree swaviag in the breeze, and had spoken the dozen words or so a few days could hear its swish as it caugbe thelaburnuna which ' previously, and looking up cptickly 'jest as the moon stood next it iu the delightful grounds of Roden Hall. : burst from behind a cloud, ha discov.ered to his horror ' Thanks to the, kindly thought of Lord Sunfield, he, ' that the woman before himevas the elder one. ; like many others, was recuperating after a long ill- I "Gold heavens !" he exclaimed, as he stepped • ness at the Convalescent Home which at one time had hastily back. "I thought I was addressing Miss , e , been the ancestral ball of this beneficent earl. !Marlowe." , I But while Frank's thoughts were divided- between I " seed you are tiering se," said the woman before the brotherliness of his fellow men and things at him. "I am Mile Merlowe, -Miss Madge Marl rw. i home, ever and anon there would come the thought of . " But never mied," she continued, as she saw his look a charming girl who had arrived at the Horne only 'of dejection. "I saw from the first you hed made a the week before.. She was a tall, frs.il creature, with mistake, bat it was so nice to be told I was loved to large;' luminous eyes, and a manner sufficiently distraction, and have my bands covered with kisses ! reserved, to be fascineting. sAt meals she sat at the that I let you go on. But do not trouble, there are next table to Fragle, . and, thanks to a large palm in worse accidents at sea, and so long as the whole house , ' the centre of the table, he bad a good view of her doesn't know of the affair we shall,--" ; face without attracting ndtice to himself. Only a "Auntie!" cal:el a voice coming through the trees day or so after she arrived he had decided that she to their right, "the matron is cross. She thinks you ! was the most beautiful woman be had ever seen, and are running a great risk by staying mat in the---" i now, as he lay thinking in bed, he had come to the Suddenly she saw Frank, and stopped. ' conclusion that if only she could get back the bloom , ," Come along, Evelyn, Mr. Faileworth has been iof health to her cheeks, and beceme more sociable, telling me the funniest story I have over heard. Sit . and,---welle-'--at any rate, if she gave him half a by me, here, and perhaps lie will repeat it to you, - chance he would tell her how much he admired her. 'no ! not to night, Mr. Failsworth ? Well, perhaps 1 But how the trees did sway -in the breeze ! It some other time, Evelyn. But the matron is right ' seemed as if they were doing their best to keep him after all. I think I must go indoors." , awake by their noise. Right through the other , And with that she took the arm of Evelyn Mayeton window at the farther side of the bed, he ' could see and Frank, and together they all ' entered the hall, • the tall firs on the front , lawn bowing gracefully while th ese banging around the bottom of the stairs towards his window as if to do him homage. How he wondered and wondered, kut for all that got no nearer did imagine things ! And yet how much nobler he the truth. Rumour says now, that Frank and Evelyn I felt since he came in contact, eith the frail creature are going to lie married and spend their honeymoon lof the luminous eyes. True, she had not spoken more at Roden Hall. than a dozen words with hint yet, but that very after- , noon in the grounds, as he had howled his last wood ! and won in the excitinglinal of a bowling handicap, ilea seen her watching from behind a thick growth of ; rhododendrons, and quietly clap her bajada as she ; passed out of sight. . And the prize ! Yes, the prize. What must be do with it ? Had it been a pipe he might have used it, - but it wasn't. It was a chastely designed, and delicately moulded china vase. ,„On the one side was a pretty pasture of the Hoene, onAho other was the ever youthful Cupid in the very net of despatching an lam w. Of couyse he could,present it to the Home, : but then he would like to give it to someone else. 1 Yes, it was no use shirking the truth,, be wbuld ,like Ito give it to,-ewell,-tb Madge. There, it was out at last. He had pronounced her , name aloud for the first time, and how *ell it sounded. , He found himself repeating it several times after that t for the mere pleasure of hebring it. Madge Marlowe! 1 Yes, that was her name, for he Lad found it out one , morning as he went into the ladies' sitting room to look for a stud he had lost when dancing at the concert Ithe night before. On the mantelshell was e letter , addressed to Miss Madge Marlowe, end of course it !must be for her, for, excepting the other tall, elderly Ilady who-had arrived with her, he k-elew the name of every rerson in the Home. And how sweet a name it was. It just suited her; Madge Marlowe ! And what &amp; good `girl she was, too. Full of sympathy, yea, she was kindness itself, That little 'Clap of ‘ the hands when he won, how kind and womanly. Unless it was that she was fond,-- no, how could she be fond of 'him yet, for he had not 'exchanged more than a dozen words with her, and not eytn called her by name. ' But the more he thought of her, the more he grew to like her. Of comae she had not' seemed to be so free as the others, but then that always denoted good breeding. Ote thing in particular he Lad noticed, and that was her solicitude for the comfort of the other tall lady, who was evidently a relative. How strange that he should not have beard their names distinctly. That very night as he.bad passed out of the supper room with a bad headache, and turned up the broad stairs to go to bed quite an hour before anyone else, he had peen them standing en the top of the stone steps in the hall, with the girl's arm around the elder woman's waist, and her woollen shawl thrown around her cetnpanion's shoulders. Bow considerate she was. He had heard that they often stood thus after supper, and sometimes they had even walked around the avenue and sai down on the lawn in spite of it being against the matron's wishes. Frank's headache vould not allow him to go to sleep. He had turned the electric light off, and done his best to snatch a doze before the others came to bed, but it was no use. The noise of the swaying horse•chesnut, and the tormenting obeisance of those tall firs, and his own thoughts Made his headache worse, and be decided- to get up, again and dress. Slipping on his readiest suit. and noiselessly pushing back the bolt of the corridor door which opened on to the top of the steel staircase outside, in another minute he was passing beneath' the archway 'of rose trees at the bottom. - , But what was tinit noise ? Footeps to be sure, in spite of the fact thet'the matron forbade anyone to walk in the grounds after supper. He stepped hastily back into the shade, and watched the forms of two women pass, affectionately linking arms. Twenty-one and fifty-one," he muttered to hint 'self. "And how sweet she is, too," he continued- illogically forgetting to inform himself which one he meant was sweet. Some of the coarser men had named these tsvo "Beauty and the Beast," but then sometimes men are sarcastic, as he knew to his pain at the close cf the bowling match. He stood and watched them turn a corner, and would have retreated up the staircase, but in a moment be had perceived their intention was to sit in a maid corner of the grcuuds before retiring indoors, for they were passing on to the fu\rthermost lawn. From his position 'beneath the archway of rose trees be could see their forms on the wicker seat, but ho dared not go near for fear of detection. He 'could sea their bare heads outlined against the sky, hut at such a &amp;stance he could not distinguish which was the younger woman. . Oh, to be near, and unseen, that he might hear that sweet girl's voice! Quietly he crept from beneath the. rose tree, and making a detour of the grounds he came out at a spot where 'he could see and yet be unseen himself. As he locked towards the seat he was surprised to see only one woman there. The elder one must have gone indoors, afraid of the cool night air. What a, splendid opportunity was this. All the scheming of his full three weeks' residence would not give him such a chance again of speaking with this sweet, frail creature But of what did he want to speak? Ali, well,-- there was only one subject which could be spoken of between two young souls on a moonlight night, beneath the trees in a secluded spot of some romantic grounds, and that was love. Yes, love! Wes not his soul consumed within him as lie stood gazing at the figure on the seat a little distance from hint? But why delay? Perhaps the elder woman intended to return for the younger. If only he could plead his cause before her return, all might he well. Suddenly he resolved upon action. Taking a nervous glance around, he strode quickly across, and flinging himself in his knees before the surprised woman,, poured forth his whole soul in eloquent appeal. ..., "I humbly ask yoer pardon for my intrusion,, but my dear 14Iiss Marlowe, for ' days- I have been con- Slimed withlove for you. Ever sinee,you came I have lived only for you. Even now when I had gone to bed with a splitting headache I could not sleep for thinking of ycu. I catne out into the open air to cool my throbbing brain, when ho! the gnds have sent you to me. Tell me, oh ! tell me if I may daro hope!" " This is very strange indeed." said the faint voice of the woman befrxe him. So faint was it, that he could hardly distinguish it from the chirping of the birds in the trees around. . , "Not strange at all my dear Miss Marlowe. Not strange at all that I should fall so deeply in love with the fairest creature on God's earth." :" But I cannot understand it at all," she responded in a voice even fainter still. "It seems all so sudden and bewildering." "I know, I know ; it may seem all that to you," said Frank impatiently. ' But to me it is life or death. Yea, tell me jolt tobe life, or death for me. Another minute and your friend may return, 'and I shall not have another chance. Speak, quickly,-- do yeti, s.-- i ?newt - -may I go on loving you with the hope--" " My dear Mr. Failsworth, I don't think you have given ;his sufficient thought. Believe me, if you were to wait till to-morrow,--" " No, no no. ..I cannot wait. In another second your friend will return. I love you -to distraction. If you refuse me I shit die, I know I shall. Fray be merciful. Quickly T.- If you love .me not, say, so, and put me out of my misery." " It is all so sudden," repeated the voice of the woman before him. " Give me till to-morrow, then perhaps,- -,-" " I cannot, oh, I cannct !" said Frank, seizing 'her</p>
