      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>PEN K ETH.</head>
        <p>PEN K ETH.</p>
        <p>TENT SERVICE.—On Sunday the Wesleyan Men's Bible Class held tbeir animal service in the tent which had been erected for the Penketh and Slnkey flower show which was held on &amp;tut lay. There was a very good attendance, ahont 200 people Tteing present. Mr. C.E.Parker the president occupied the chair.An excellent address was given by the Rev. P.Vanghan,of the Emmanuel Church, IVarringtou. Special hymns were sung and the Bible class choir rendered th 3 anthems " Daughter of Zion" and "Jerusalem, my happy home" in grand style, and grest oredit is due to Mr. Herbert Plumpton, who has the choir in hand. Mrs. C. E. Parker was the accompanist. The Panketh Tannery Prize Band also played two selections with good effect, Mr. George Carman being their conductor.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>ANNUAL FLOWER SHOW.</head>
        <p>THE ANNUAL FLOWER SHOW.</p>
        <p>The annual show of fruit, flowers, vearetreiles and bulbs in connection with the Penketh, Sankey and District Girden and Cage Bird Society was held on Saturaay, and proved a great success. Heavy rain in the morning affected the attendance but there was a good company when Miss Tinsley opened the pro- ceedings in the afternoon.. _</p>
        <p>Mr. -J. G. Withinshaw (president), presiding at tho 'opening ceremony, briefly traoed the origin and growth of the society and made the satiiractory announcement that this year there as a rocord number of entries for flowers, fruit and birds. Miss Tinsley then declared the exhitition open, and wished the society continued prosperity. Forth's little duty she was thanked on the proposition of Mrs. Dodds, seconded by Mr. J,13„ Aitken. _</p>
        <p>The exhi.bits, which totalled 3'59, were staged in a large marpnee' and formed quite an interesting collec- tion. While there was no outstanding feature, the vegetables generAllv were in a forward state, but the fruit called for little qomment. Of the flowers there were some exceptionally good dahlias, sweet peas and violas, while amone a moderate lot of roses the Jennie Dickson, a nicely-shaped pink, of Mr. J. Goulding, won the championship. The appearance of the marquee was greatly enhanced by two fine groups of plants oelonging to Nirs. Garnett and Mrs. Aitken. There was also a table of well-grown stocks sent ,by Mr. C. B. Parker; and Mr. Upton of Penketh, and Mr. Byrom, of Sankey were represented by stands.. .</p>
        <p>*The birds bad a little department to themselvas, Mr. I. Hampson (assisted by Mr. A. Howson), being in charge as secretary. Thole was a good collection, especially of ycnng birds, and competition was keen. Mr. J. Frangleton won the prize for the best young canary with a four-pointed Yorkshire which was rather heavily marked on the eyes. The best old bird was a variegatedliorwich of good shape, with a flab chest. belonging to Mr. \V. Murray. The best foreign small bird was a beautiful green budyard, owned by Mr. C. H. Cook, and the best foreign lam was a blue-faced Amazon parrot, the property of Mr. J. Swift. As a side show the Crewe Royal Morris Dancers 'proved entertaining, and several performances were watched with great interest. They were accompanied by the Penketh Tannery Prize Band, who played for dancing in the evening. The.officials who carried out the arranrements were: —Committee, Messrs. J. Backbones, J. Knowles, R. Long, T. Bell, G. Faulkner W. Moores, S. Andreas, W. Woods, J. Moores, E. liorster, J. Ditchfleld, and E. Houghton ; general secretary, Mr. John J. Foden ; assistant secretary, Mr. R. Aldcroft ; and treasurer, Dr. Murray. Mr. G. Ferguson, Norton Priory, Runcorn ; and Mr. R. Whitfield, Bank Park, Warrington, judged the flowers and vegetables, Mr. J. Watson the children's essays (a new feature), and Mr. A Wellings, of .Prescet, the birds.</p>
        <p>It should be stated that special prizes were given by Miss Bolton, Mrs. Garnett, MIS. Seed, Messrs. .1.13. Aitken R. Aldcroft, Carter and Son, .7. W. Baker,. Cs!dwells Limited, Price's Stores, J. Knowles, J. G.. Wlthinchaw' G. Starkey, 0. Shepherd. H. Irwin, W. Afoston, J. Fisher I. Hampson and Billington, and Owen.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>PRIZE LIST.</head>
