        <p>GARDENS, &amp;c.—Large cottage flower garden : I, J Goulding ; 2, S Mose. Small cottage garden with greenhouse : 1, A Birch. Medinro cottage garden without greenhouse : l, Hampton. Small cottage garden withont greenhouse : 1, W Scott ;2, J. Lees Kitchen garden : I, G Lee ;2, J Carmen. Allotments:- I,G lee; 2, J earnvin. Climbling Rose : 1, IP Duruhill 2, J Cerrington. Ampelopsis : 1, A Bowyer ; 2, T Bell. Creeper (except ivy) planted within the last two years : 1,3 Woodward ; 2' Skelhorn. PLANTS.—Window plant foliage variety not grown for its flowers: 1, W Lightfoor. 2, 3 %%heeler. Window plant (flowering): I, W Jones; 2, J Lee. Pour cottage greenhouse plants: 1, 1 Hampson. Pot Lilium Aumtum : 1,1 Hampson. Cottage garden green- house: 1, B Moss; 2,1 Ilampson. Stand of plants and cut flowers from cottage garden and greenhouse: 1, I Ilampson.</p>
        <p>CUT FLOWERS.-Six cottage greenhouse roses: 1, J Goulding; 2,5 Moss. Six roses (hybrid perpetuals), I,J Goulding. Collection of sc‘eet peas: 1, J Gould- ing; 2, D Boueh:11. Three distinct sweet, peas, not less than six sprays of each: 1, J Davies; 2, D. Bonehill. Six roses (hybrid teas): 1, F Dnmbill; 2, J Goulding. Three roses (hybrid perpetuals): I, J Carrington 2: A Seed. Three roses (hybrid teas) : I.F Diunbill ; 2, J Davies. Six spikes antirrhinums (three or more colours) : 1, H Bingham; 2, J Goulding. Three spikes of stocks : 1, C Ilickling ; 2, I Ilampson. Six asters : 1, P Sparks. Three lilies, any variety (outside grown): 1, J Goulding ' • 2, D Bonchill. Three carnations : 2, J Davies. Three picotees : 1, H Bingham. Three spikes of phlox : 1, .1 Carman; 2, J Carrington. Three violas, self colour : I,D Bouchill ;2, J Davies. Three violas, any variety : 1, .1 Carman • 2. D Bonehill. Violas, three varieties, six of each : 51, H Bingham ; 2, J Davies. Six pansies, distinct : 1, 1' Durnhill ;2, 11 Bingham. Six dahlias, cactus; 1, C 2,-D Bone- hill. Six dahlias,pompone: I,D Bonebill. Three dahlias, cactus: I.H Bingham; 2,C Hickling. Three dghlias,pom- pone: 1, C trickling; 2,S Moss. Three flowers,any variety ' (not mentioned in any other class) : 1, S Moss • 2, J Goulding. Twelve everlasting flowers : 2, S kor.s. Three sunflowers (any variety) : 1, S Moss. Bouquet of wild flowers (made up by children under 12 yeats) : 1, F Ray :2, Winnie Foden ; :3, W Rater. Best named tray of wild flowers (20 varieties botanical and common names, for children under 12 years): 1, It Ray -2, E Hobson ; 3, S Richardson. Best named tray of wild flowers, 25 varieties (botanican and common names for children over /2 and under 34 oars) : 2, A Cheetham. Best essay on wild flowers of the district for boys only under 13 years : 1 J Irvin ; 2, C Tarhotton : 3, J Midwood. Pest brush work illustration of plants, and essay on same for children of Standard 5 and under : 1, A Cheetham ; 2, J Faulkner ;i, Gertie Ray ; 4, J Irwin.</p>
        <p>-- -- VEGETABLES AND FftrlT.-Six potatoes, red 1, J Carman; 2, P Sparks. Six potatoes, white : 1, J Carman • 2,, S Moss. Box of six different named vegetables : 1, J Carman ;2, G Lee. Celery : 1, 0 Carman ; 2, J Garman. Lettuces: 2, LI James. Parsley: 1, J Goulding ;2, S Moss. Ppas, one dozen : 1, H Bingham ; 2, J Carman. French beans : 1, J Turner ; 2, T Walker. Broad beans : 1, J Isherwood ; 2. J Carman. Vegetable marrows, white : 1, J Gonldine. Vegetable marrows, green : 2, J Goulding. Shallots : 1, J Goulding ; 2, T Jackson. Cauliflowers : 1, J Goulding ;2, I Hampson. Leeks: 2, II Bingham. Onions : 1, J Goulding; 2, J Bighorn. Apples, culinary : 1, J Goulding ; 2, G W Clare. Apples, dessert : 2, ./ Gonlding. Pears : 1, ve chirp ;2, J Goulding. Any other variety of fruit: T, S Moss; 2, A Watson. Tomatoes : 3, S. Moss. Beetroots, tore on : 1, J Goulding : 2, J Carrington. Yellow or white turnips : 1, P Sharkes. GARDENERS OF LOCAL GENTRY.-Stand of vegetables fruit and (towels (sft. square): 1, S. Moss (gardener to the Friend's School)._</p>
        <p>- -------- OPEN CLASS.—Roses (six varieties hybrid teas; six rieties perpetuals) : 1, D., Bonchill ; 2,J. Carrington. TABLE DECORATION.—Decorated table by ladies: 1, Miss Shaw:_ 2, Mrs. Cook ; 3, bliss Bell. Ec; GS.—Eggs (white) : I,J. Jackson; 2, J. Goulding. Eggs (brown) : 1, Miss Shaw; 2, J. Gonlding. BREAD BAKING.—Loaf (home-baked) not exceeding 21b.: 1, Mrs Cook; 2, Mrs Hobson. Loaf (borne-baked in 21b. tins) : 1, Mrs. Phoenix ; 2, Miss Booth ; 3, Mrs Cook.</p>
