      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>The programme at this popular place of amuse- ment has this week been noteworthy for its musical excellence. The genius shown in the composition and the orchestration of some of the songs is unmistakable there being nothing brighter or richer in tone colour, nor anything more ear-catching in melody before the vandeville public at the present moment, than the songs which Ryder Slone and Norah Stockelle vocalise so successfully to delighted audiences. Ryder Slone is very self-restrained in his style of presenting such songs as " The Night Watchman" and "Sing sing" and herein, we think, lies his strong point. The vivacity of Norah Stockelle is, on the other hand, a most fitting chgr- acteristic of her singing, and when, the lilt of her song's refrain having crept into the ears of her audience, she breaks away into the dance, the grace- fulness of her abangon, is as refreshing as it is remarkable. The Vyponds, too, are entitled to no small measure of the credit for the specially musical feature of the present week's programme. Mr. Vypond's extraordinary pianoforte accompaniments to Mrs. Vypond's songs and the clever way in which some Scotch national melodies were here requisitioned as underlying themes against the principal melody of the song deserves noting whether the composition is that of the pianist or an outsider. Giving agreeable variety to the vocal music the Darktown Entertainers, Hampton and Bauman, present a pretty little sketch " Down in Kentucky," bringing in some well-known plantation songs with fine breaks into two-part vocal harmony. The eccentricities of Ferguson and Mack, the very talented exhibition of ventriloquism by W. E. Whittle and several other " turns " make up a very interesting entertainment, to which a visit this evening can be confidently recommended. NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME.</p>
        <p>_ The following items are included in the bill for next week :—Jack Pleasants, a comedian with a style of his own ; Ethel Sheffer, with illustrated songs; Dunn and Reano, eccentric parody acrobats; Mdlle. Bartenelli, the famous contortionist ; Harry Nelson, quaint comedian, in the skit "My wife's a suffragette"; Matthews and Brown, comedians and dancers; Hanid Alexander, in novel piano entertainment ; Margot and Lener, continental eccentrics; Ramond's bio-tableaux.</p>
        <p>Aa the greatest British OUR GREATEST interest is peace, general INTEREST, attention will naturally concentrate itself on the International Peace Congress, which was opened in London this week. The differences of party vanish on occasions like this:, and it is pleasant to find that among the letters read at the commence- ment of the proceedings was one from Mr. BALFoun, who Extends the famous saying which we have just quoted into the larger formula that peace is the greatest interest of the civilised world. Everything which promotes peace, observes A the leader of the Opposition, whether by 411 educating public opinion or by the conclusion of aebitration treaties,or by the efficient maintenance of defensive agreements,should have the sympathY and support of all who have the welfare of humanity at heart. There are sentiments that are not peculiar to our own statesmen or to our own statesmen or to our own country. It is interesting to note that among the objects exhibited by the French Government at the Franco-British Exhibition there is a map which displays, by means of lines drawn between the different States of the world, the number and the character of the Arbitration Treaties which are already in Existence. It is one of the many agree- able proofs that the negotiation of Treaties of Arbitration is becoming a part of the policy of all civilised Governments. The speech of Mr. LLOYD GEORGE was a most eloquent and convincing plea for the abolition of all causes of war. Earlier in the day, the powerful mind of Lord COURTNEY of Penwith had started the arguments for peace in an inaugural address He asked his audience to look back on the wars of the past. How many of them can be justi- fied by subsequent reflection ? Most of there as Lord COURTNEY said, it is now generally admitted, ought not to have taken place, for they could have been prevenied. Wars are, in act, in most instances monstrous examples of human error. The exceptions are, as the opener of the Peace Congress said, those which have been waged against open injustice and for the ex tension of liberty. If we could get rid of injustice, Lord COURTNEY suggests, we should dispose of all legitimate occasions for war. But here an immense problem presents itself. The President of the Con- gress thinks that the desirable consummation may be brought about, in large part at least, by the strengthening, developing, and purifying of international law. This is the especial work of The Hague Conferences:, two out of three of which, however, have been followed by war on a great scale. Never- theless, there is now a tribunal to which it ig possible to appeal. There would, in all probebility, have been no Boer War if we had per- mitted the Transvaal to be represented at The Hague. Assuming the existence of any desire between States at variance for an amicable settle- ment of their differences, the machinery exist:, for securing the continuance of peace: That is, at least, a great advance. In ordinary affairs, the law is substituted for private violence. If the same thing could take place among the nations, says Lord COURTNEY, there might be an end also of national violence. The Peace Congress, by a happy coincidence, follows closely on the speech in which Sir EDWARD GREY explained to the House of Commons our policy in regard to almost all those portions of the world in which differences affeeting ourselves might be expected to arise. It was a speech of remark- able weight, of great plainness of phrase, Of singular clear-sightedness, and it has probabli done greater service to the cause ef Etropean peace than any speech delivered, either here or abroad, for many yeare past. It must have been read with peculiar satisfaction in Germany—outside those circles which, like certain similar circles in our owe country, hold fatalistic ideas as to th 3 " inevitable- ness " of a conflict between ourselves and the the German Empire. There can now exist 0° further excuse for believing that the isolation of Germany is a part of British policy. Germany, as Sir EDWARD GREY reminds us, has had maul proofs of our friendliness. The idea that these are delusive and insincere and that they mask a° unslurnbering enmity may still persist in some cankered minds, but should no longer influence German statesmanship. The Congress, it seems to us, cannot possibly do more for peace, however successful it may be, than the frank, and at the same time, the cautious speech of our Foreign Secretary.</p>
        <p>j</p>
        <p>1 I 1</p>
        <p>If the growing desire for SMOKELESS CITIES. pure air and cleanliness ill our great cities is ever to find fullest expression, governing bodies will hay° to deal almost first of all and very energeticallY with the imperfect combustion of fuel. It III! been shewn often enough that smoke is the resuo merely of a foolish waste of very valuable material. It needs no proof that its destructive effects urill buildings and stone work involve an expenditul annually for necessary cleaning, repairing 6% refacing of many thousands of pounds. If 13" method could be devised to prevent this troublesora: nuisance some excuse might be made for the clouds that rush all day long from myriads I:3' chimneys into the air we breathe. Since, howeveri it has been demonstrated not only in Eoglioll but on a very large scale in Chicago, that sourb; may bo entirely consumed in the fire box waste of coal avoided, there seems no reason ; the determined effort to abate the nui13613</p>
        <p>,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
