      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <p>ebould net already have begun. The means of obtaining perfect combustion is only an arrange-i rnent of space enough in the grate for the volatilel matter to have become gas before it touches the cool surface of the boiler. An interesting demon- stration has recently been made in Cbicago con- sequent on a compaien by municipal authorities there. At one very large factory some 2,000 tons of coal are burnt every day. A space of 141 t. has been left between grate and boiler, and no smoke is ever seen coming from the chimneys. Nine feet, however, has been found to be the minimum distance at which satisfactory restilts are attainable. ,The saving ot coal qoickly recoups the expense of rearrangement, while the public advantage is immense. Reform of this kind would, unfortunately, only concern the nuisance of factory smoke. It must be confessed that very little has been contributed as yet to a .successful scheme of dealing with the waste pro- ducts,of household fires. A system subterranean tunnels into which the smoke could be drawn and taken to central destructors sounds rather empractible. So, too, however, would have seemed to Hannibal a tunnel railway through the Alps.</p>
        <p>-- - We have all been told at AT..wess THE 5A3114. - school that the history of these islands begins with 'the arrival of Julius Omar in the year 50 B.C. Of course, most people have learnt since that Plitay refers to Pytheas of Massilia and others who visited end wrote books about Britain, and many remember that in the South of England, at any rate, considerable trade was carried on. Corniah tin and copper mining was carried on centuries before Cresar came, and so was pearl and oyster fishing. There are still a few frag- ments remaining of the work of Pytheas, and they represent the earliest written record of our native land. He landed in Cornwall in the fourth century /3 CI wrote about its tin, and described a coast 'which may have been that of Hampshire and •Susse x. His account of the climate is as dismal as that given by Cam and by everyone else who has come" here from the Continent, including our visitors to the Franco-British Exhibition. Indeed, what strikes us about these earliest accounts of our islands is that the writers were impressed by the very thing which impresses foreign visitors to-day. We are accustomel to the idea that time changes all things,but,although this is true, there are some things which take a long time to change. One of these things, evidently, is the change ability of the British climate. That remains.</p>
        <p>-- Mr. Lloyd-George's griev• Cluisontow km) ance against theilaftragettes Two-PowEe is twofold. Not only did STANDARD. they make it extremely difficult for him to speak at all at the Pacestueeting in the Q9eer.'s Hall the other night, but their interruptions caused him to be greatly misunderstood at the most important 'Point in his speech, and put him to the trouble of Writing to the Press to explain the real tenor of his arguments. As they reached the reporter's table, the Chancellor's remarks appeared to some of his opponents to justify the charge that he had made an attack on the two-Power standard, which has been accepted by both the Liberal and the Unionist Parties as the necessary degree of strength at which the Navy must be main- tained to secure reasonable safety. Such an attack, had it in actual fact been made by the Minister responsible for the national finances, would undoubtedly have been particularly serious. But Mr. Lloyd-George was unconscious of the intention. He was arguing against the hysterical suspicion of Germany which has lately char- acterised a certain section of publicists. That outbreak of nerves, fostered by violent Teutophobia in a narrower circle, has been deplored by many who are not politi- - tally in agreement with the present Govern- ment. Mr. Lloyd-George set himself to show that . . .</p>
        <p>• whatever cause there mig' ht be for nervousness in Germany there was really none in England. He contrasted the position of the two countries. Germany depends on her army for her frontier defence ; England on her Navy. The British Empire can maintain a Navy that is equal to that of any two possible opponents. The German Empire cannot possibly maintain an Army that is - ‘vitnilarly equal to that of any two of the Powers whose frontiers touch her own, and who are there- fore potential enemies. Thils, argued the Chan- cellor, Germaby can never occupy the position of comparative superiority which is held by this country, and nervousness on the part of her People is excusable on that ground. It was a legitimate argument against the mental attitude of suspicion and alarm which is making the task of the diplomatist at' the present moment doubly hard. But we do not think it can be fairly charged against the Chancellor that it was an attack on the "two-Power standard." It was a Plea rather for calmness and confidence in our known strength as a means of maintaining peace in Europe.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>The Government is -pushing forward the Licensing 13ill. It is the strong push against the -" long Pill: • * * The following incident is given as authentic by a 'head teacher in a London County County school : A. teacher sent a message to a child's mother asking , her to wash the youngster's clothes, as they were - offensive. Back promr7ly came the reply : "My Jimmie ain't no bloomin' rose— you lam him, not smell him." * * • Prom the Cardiff" Evening News" : " We have beard lately of an estate where the rear- : • Ing of wild duck was made something of a feature, ' 'which suffered losses to the extent of 28 of the ducks . in a single night, all killed by rates." After some varied experience as a householder we can quite believe it. * 5 * The Junior Conservatives have resumed their Political propaganda in the shape of evening 'bus -drives to Lymm and other places, at 6d. a head. The idea is most popular among the women and children, ' but the man with the vote is a mama a via on the Tory , 'bus. Judging by the number of patrons, the Junior Conservatives should be making a good thing out of their political enterprise. Twelve 'buses ought to bring in a pound each at Cd. a head—and £l2 should .•leave a margin for other political exigencies. When ' Is the balance sheet to be presented ? * * * A serious warning to motorists was uttered in the souse of Commons on Wednesday by Mr. John Burns, President of the Local Government Board. The • " road-hog " has been responsible for so much nuisance and so many accidents of late that the whole Class of motorists are in danger of suffering through , his fault. Mr. Burns saie: —"Motorists would be well advised if they realised that public opinion was hardening considerably in regard to this matter. They bad got to be told, and, if necessary, compelled to put their house in order, and, if they did not take the hint, Barkis being willing, he would be compelled to • -see that they were kept decently in order." Well- ,' intentioned motorists should themselves take a more active part in seeing that the "road-hog "is brought to book.</p>
        <p>* * Wealth makes everything easy—honesty most of all.— Diane. Of all peoples the Greeks dreamt the dream of life the best.—Goethe. Remember this—that very little is needed to make -a happy life.—.llarcus Aurclilta. Gentle words, quiet words, are after all the most Powerful words (Wadden.</p>
        <p>Ilowe'er you babble, great deeds cannot die.— TCP7tyBon. , Evolution must be judged not by its beginnings but 11 its endings.—Bernard Snell. Patient endurance Attaineth to all things..—Long f cilmr. Kind words do not cost much. They never blister the tongue or lips. They accomplish much. They Izake other people good-natured.— Pascal. * * * Efaggerston, where a brief but strenuous electoral contest concludes to-day, is a compact political division 15f the borough of Shoreditch, separated from Bethnal .I?„rsen by the Hackney-road and from Boxton by the - \-ingsland-road. Now entirely occupied by factories and artisans' houses, little more than a century ago its •green fields rere pleasantly 'varied with the country</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <p>residences of worthy City merchants, and as the hamlet of" Hergotstane " it was duly entered in the Conqueror's " Domesday Book." Nichols-square, its one remaining green spot, is a reminder of the firm of Nichols, who for long acted as printers to Parliament, and of its eighteenth-century owner, John Nichols, the antiquary. naggerston's most famous resident was undoubtedly Halley the astronomer, who was born there, and became Astronomer-Royal, and whose nanie is associated with the great comet of 1.052, for whose third return since then astronomers will be on the look-out this winter.</p>
        <p>_ * * * There is considerable truth," writes " A Junior of Fifty," "in Judge Edge's statement that there are many well-qualified barristers wh ) are not earning as much as bricklayers.' Roughly speaking, there are about 9,000 counsel whose names are to be found in the Law List ;and of these probably 1,500 are actually engaged in the practice of the law. Of the 1,500 it is doubtful whether more than 10 per cent. make an income exceeding £l,OOO a year; 20 per cent may see from £5OO to £l,OOO as the net result of twelve months' work ; while the remaining 70 per cent. either fail to clear expenses or to have the scantiest margin for their livelihood. I know per- sonally several barristers of more than 20 years' ' standing whose net income is smaller than that of many an artisan.; and more than one who would gladly change incomes with a bricklayer. And matters are getting worse. Daring the last two years legal incomes have shrunk at least 30 per cent., a fact which scores of fee-books painfully demonstrate."</p>
        <p>* - The Council of the International Arbitration League met on Wednesday and appointed Mr. F. Maddison: M.P., as secretary of the league, in succession to the late Sir Randal Cremer. A better selection could not have been made. Mr. Maddison is a strong Radical, a keen Free Trader, and an ardent champion of inter- national peace. At the meeting the Council passed a ,resolution strongly deprecating the organised efforts that were being made to create ill-feeling between this country and Germany, and regretting that Lord Cromer had seen fit to give expression to the opinion that a great war was almost inevitable. The Labour party also took an important step in the cause of peace. They decided that the party should make an official visit to Germany at Whitsuntide next year. Their object is to fraternise with German workmen and do everything in their power to show that the Labour party are opposed to the efforts that are being made to create strife between Germany and this country. In the meantime we must all, as Lord Courtney says, steadily resist the mischievous fatalism which speaks of a conflict as inevitable. -</p>
        <p>* * * The Austrian Post Office Savings Bank is most enterprising, and has left the English institution, which once was its Model, far behind. It provides a drawing system as well as a deposit system, the use of safes and strongrooms, where deeds and effects may be placed, and also is a brokerage institution for the sale and purchase of all manner of securities. Cheques may be drawn for the smallest amounts, and ready transmission of money from place to place is facilitated, and the use of cheques for the payment of tradesmen's accounts and the like is far more common than in England. It is claimed that this portion of the bank's business is doing more for thrift than the mere deposit side, inasmuch as it is growing com- paratively common for an artisan or a clerk to..pit the whole of his wages in the bank,- to live on cheques, and then to transfer the residue to his savings account. Switzerland has imitated the Austrian system, and there is an agitation for the same arrangement in Italy. Yet it should be remembered that the Post Office Savings Bank is an English invention. Having adopted the British idea, our Continental friends have carried it much farther.</p>
        <p>* * * Mr. Joynson-Hicks explained in the debate on the Finance Bill on Saturday, that he was not a Protec- tionist so much as a Free Trader with "an Imperial instinct." Here is his idea of what Preference ought to be: \ He would have exempted as far as possible Indian %a and left the tax on China tea. That would not be an infringement of the principles of Free Trade, though it might be an infringement of the principles of Cobdenite trade of fifty years ago. It , would not be protective, it would not put a tax on ' the people of England for bolstering up an effete English industry. It has been asked how a prefer- ence was to be given to the Colonies without putting a tax on wheat. Here was an opportunity of giving such a preference without any tax on wheat—with- out imposing on the British public any single further tax on foodstuffs. We cannot help wondering (says the" Westminister Gazette ") what the Colonies would think of this. Instead of" slamming the floor "we ought to have invited them into our parlour and given them—Tea ! Tea, which they do not and cannot produce. We always sympathise with Unionist Free Traders who dislike Protection and so struggle to avoid it. But it is no use making-believe in the way that Mr.Joynson- Hicks does.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>POLITICS OF THE WEEK.</head>
        <p>THE POLITICS OF THE WEEK.</p>
        <p>[By OCR LONDON CORitESPONDENT.] If the politicians are taking a holiday next week they have not been grudging in their public services in the week now expiring. Both in Parliament and out of it they have been busy. Both Houses have been keeping hours that would scandalise the ordinary respectable citizen. Theie was one sitting of the House of Commons prolonged until four o'clock in the morning. The work done was none the better for that ; and if it were worth while, any old Parlia- mentary hand might show from a detailed examina- tion of such sittings, how much even the art of getting a little amusement out of such late sittings has declined. The present "all-night sitting " men are l a degenerate race compared with their rollicking predecessors. Mr. Jeremiah MacVeagh, however, supplies the Irish wit which is an essential ingredient of these post-midnight Parliamentary dishes. A lively election at the East End of London, and a Universal Peace Congress in the West, have further crowded the week's political bill.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>MR. BIRRELL'S SUCCESS.</head>
        <p>MR. BIRRELL'S SUCCESS.</p>
        <p>There was a thrill of genuine emotion in the House of Commons towards the close of its first Saturday sitting this Session, at the end of last week, when Mr. Birrell, having finished his long and weary fight over the Irish Universities Bill, and having got the third reading, rose and left the House with the air of a man relieved of a great burden. He was followed by a general cheer, and it is wonderful how expressive a cheer can be in the House of Commons. Tennyson in "In Memoriam" compared himself to "an infant crying in the night, and with no language but a cry.' But a child's cry and also a House of Commons cheer, can be eloquent enough without articulation. The cheer which followed Mr. Birrell said many things that were sympathic and heartfelt, and that would have been much more feebly expressed in such words Bravo ! " Well done ! " We are really glad,' or even God bless you !' And perhaps the pat on the back, which a friendly hand managed to give him as he left the House, gave a dramatic finish to the debate That no eloquence of speech could have given it. After failures and disappointments that were due to no fault of his own, Mr. Birrell had put his hand to , the hardest task of all, and staked his political exist- 1 ence, or at all events his continuance in the Ministry, on an enterprise that had baffled many predecessors, and once involved even the great Mr. Gladstone in a Ministerial crisis. And he had won ! He had made ready for the Statue Book, subject to the vagaries of the Peers, a settlement of the Irish University Educa- tion problem, and,written his name iudeliby in the annals of the Emerald Isle.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>NEW IRISH UNIVERSITIES.</head>
        <p>THE NEW IRISH UNIVERSITIES.</p>
        <p>There are now to be three Univer3ities in Ireland, two in Dublin and one in Belfast. The existing Dublin University remains untouched by the new measure ; for Trinity College, Dublin, like the City of London when the problem of London Government was tackled, stood by its ancient traditions and would not be the nucleus even of a noble expansion. As has been said in the Lords, it made "the great refusal." Mr. Birrell's-Act creates another University in Dublin which while it is not a Catholic University will give a University education to Catholics; and has balanced this institution by another in Belfast, which in the same way will probably for the present be regarded as having an atmosphere which will commend itself to Protestants, but which it is the hore of Mr. Birrell will unite Catholic and Protestant, and enable them 1 to merge their religious differences in academic unity I and ceprit dc CO Pp. Mr. Birrell's glory is that he has so far reconciled the apparently irreconcilable interests as to have set up these Universities without tests and with a free and academic government, and that he has smoothed the way for the Irish laity to direct their own University education in the future.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>ENGLAND AND GERUANY.</head>
        <p>ENGLAND AND GERUANY.</p>
        <p>The events of this week, it is hoped, may do some- thing to put a stop to one of these wicked prophecies which are apt to have in them the evil tendency to</p>
