      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>MOTORIST FINED.</p>
        <p>On Monday, at St.—Helens Police Court, William Else, a painter, of Hardshaw-street, was hoed ss. and ss. W. costs, for having no light on the rear of his motor.</p>
        <p>MARKET STALL THEFT.</p>
        <p>On Monday, at St. Helens Police Court a young married woman named Margaret Pickavancet pf Liverpool-street, was found guilty of stealing a pair of boots, the ptoperty of Lily Turnbull, from a stall in the market. There were several previous convictions against the prisoner, who was sent to gaol for twenty- eight days with hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>STRIKE AT ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>STRIKE AT ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>It is probable that the strike of moulders, which has been in progress at St. Helen's for the past fortnight, will be settled at an early date. The men struck work because the employers proposed to make a reduction of 2s. a week in their wages. It is understood that the men will return to work to-morrow at the old _terms ; and while they ate workings conference will be held between the masters and men to come to a mutual agreement as to wages.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>SUFFRAGIST MEETING AT S. HELENS.</head>
        <p>SUFFRAGIST MEETING AT S. HELENS.</p>
        <p>On Monday. evening a meeting was held at St. Helens in connection with the movement for securing votes for *omen, under the auspices of the Liverpool Branch of the Women's Social and Political Union.—Mrs. Farrar, who presided, said they claimed the vote so that they could alter the bad conditions of women's labour.—Mrs. Hillier, in urging the claims of women in assisting to make the laws of the country, said the militant section had done more for the enfranchisement of women during the last three years than all the peacefunagitation of the last forty years. They believed in constitutional agitation when it served their purpose, but when it did not, then as women, with a desperate cause behind them, they believed they must come out and use unconstitutional methods. FLOWER SHOW AT ST HELENS.</p>
        <p>Coming Events. As will be seen from our advertising columns the 23rd Annual Amateur Athletic Sports of the War- rington Football Club will be held on Monday next, August 3rd on the Football Ground, Wilderspool- road. A splendid entry has been made ; over 340 having entered for the various events A brass band will be in attendance. The sports commence at 2 p.m. Stockton Heath Garden Society's annual show and fête will be held on Saturday, August ]sth, find will be opened by 11.5. H. Princess Ludwig of Loewenstein- Wertheim. The annual agricultural show 'at Walton Park will be held on Thursday, August 13th. Entries close to-day. Drunk. At the Police Court on Thursday morning, John Hallam, residing at Manchester, was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Rylands-street on the previous night. Prisoner had that day been discharged from the infirmary. He was requested to leave the town which he promised to do. He was fined 53. and costs or 7 days. Still unsurpassed, PREMIER CYCLES at 6 guineas. —Agent, J. S. WALING, 51 to 55, Bridge st., Warrington.—Advt. Music in the Park. The Borough Prize Brass Band will play the follow- ing programme of music in the park on Sunday: Hymn, "Heavenly," J. A. Greenwood; march, " Dreadnought," "7. Rimmer ; selection, " Verdi," H. ,Round ; selection, " Maritana " Wallace ; Fantasia, "Pilgrim " H. Round; selection, "La Vestale," Spontine ; "God save the Kiag " ; conductor Mr. T. Griffin. Athletes and Sportsmen Are requested to note that the Warrington Football Annual and Sports Record will be published on Saturday next. It will contain Northern Rugby League fixtures, football facts and records, swimming, bowling and hockey records, etc., together with photographs of well-known local footballers, bowlers and swimmers. The best pennyworth published. Don't miss it I Art Successes. The following students in the Warrington School of Art have obtained successes in the Board of Educa- tion's National Competition :—Book prizes: Dobson Thomas Claik, for designs for hinge-straps and lock- plate, drawn and modelled ; Arthur Hayward, for his shaded study of drapery arranged on an antique figure. Commenced :. Sydney Clatwortby, for studies of plant form ; Herbert H. Dell, for shaded study of drapery on an antique figure. TRAVELLING TRUNKS, DRESS BASKETS, ETC., NORTH WALES SAILINOS. largo selection, reasonable prices.—J. S. GARLAND, Ironmonger,sl to 55, Bridge st„Warrington.—Advt. The Liverpool and North Wales Steamship Company The Cycling Club. have announced their sailings for the holidays. To-day Saturday last was "President and Ladies' Day (Saturday) La Marguerite will leave as usual for " !, Llandudno, Beaumaris, Bangor, and Menai Bridge. in connection with the Warrington Cycling Club, There will be also afternoon sailings by St. Tuduo for when a visit was paid to-Sandiway. The unfavourable Llandudno, due back in Liverpool in the early evening; weather of the morning, though better in the after', and the St. Elvies for Beaumaris, Bangor, Menai Bridge, noon, deterred many from venturing, and 35 ladies returning direct to Liverpool somewhat later. On and gentlemen only journeyed thither. Ten of the' will be special excursion round ! Island of Anglerey by the St fudno. his steamer Sunday morning there members cycled over, and the rest went by train from the • • • does not call anywhere, and is due back in Liverpool Bank Quay Station, getting off at Hartford and walk- • the same evening. Special cars will run in connection ing to the Blue Cap Inn, which was made the with this salting. In addition to this sailing there will rendezvous for the afternoon and evening. After a• be the steamer La c, Marguerite, also St. Ervies in the game of bowls, tea was served, Mr. Jan. Naylor, forenoon, and Snowdon in the afternoon. On Bank president, being amongst those present; indeed it was Holiday three sailings are advertisetTea day trip by St. in his honour principally that the outing took place, Teaudunrao arreiusnitiAnn,golresnrednairleiitri Sget.,El Bridge, with as for connection as the club is anxious once a year at least to have 13 him as its guest, he being such a generous friend. Carnarvon, also back in Liverpool the same evening After tea an interesting presentation was made to Mr. and La Marguerite for Llandudno and Menai Straits. Naylor, in the shape of an enlarged photograph of With anything approaching fine weather there is no doubt the steamers will carry a large number of ! himself taken by Mr. B. Jones. passengers during the holidays.</p>
        <p>On Saturday afternoon it was arranged that the annual works' sports and flower show, under the auspices of the St. Helens Recreation Club, which is in connec- tion with the mat glassworks of Messrs. Pilkington Bros., should take place in the City-road ground, but owing to the heavy rain it was decided to postpone the sports. The flower show, however, was very successful, there being over 200 exhibits, the choice roses, sweet peas, geraniums, fuchsias, &amp;c., showing what a great interest the employees of Messrs. Pilkington take in their gardens and greenhouses. There were also classes for the produce of plots in Denton's Green which the firm have allotted to their men, and the results were highly satisfactory. The judges were Mr. Archer (gardener for Colonel W. W. Pilkington), Mr. Finney (gardener for Mr. It. A. Pilkington), and Mr. Hurd (gardener for Mr. G. H. Pilkington), and they had no little difficulty in allotting the prizes, the competition in many of the classes being very keen indeed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>YOUTHFUL PICaTOCKETS BIRCHED.</head>
        <p>YOUTHFUL PICaTOCKETS BIRCHED.</p>
        <p>At the St. Helens Police Court, on Tuesday, three boys, named Richard Cardwell, of Crab-street ; James Bradbury, Queen-street ; and Walter Tierney, York- street, were charged with stealing a purse containing 16s. from the person of Mrs. Elizabeth Webster, of New Cross-street. Chief-Inspector Goodall stated that Mrs. Webster was in the market-place on Saturday night, and made a purchase at a sale there. She saw the three defendants standing 'near, and shortly afterwards she missed her purse. She gave information to the police, and Police-constable Anders arrested the defendants, who each pleaded guilty. Arthur Ashball, Union-street, and Edward Cardwell, of Crab-street, were charged with receiving the money knowing it to have been stolen. I They also pleaded guilty, and Cardwell admitted having afterwards hidden 10s. in the Hardshaw Fields, where / the constable afterwards found it. Each boy blamed the other for having taken the money. Tierney was fined ss. and costs ; Bradley, who had been up before. was orderad twelve strokes with the birch ; and the / others were ordered six strokes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>WORKSHOP THEFT. At Widnes, on Monday, Henry Smith, an elderly man, of Widnes, was seat to gaol for one month on a charge of stealing several articles of tools from the workshop of James Bailey, cooper, of Dittoa-road, on the 23rd of Cricket. last month. Fortunately cricketers are a hopeful kind. Had they WIDNES DROWNING CASE INQUEST. been accustomed to despair early there would have Coroner-S. Brighouse held an inquiry at Widnes on been no cricket last Saturday. Rain fell in torrents all Monday touching the death of James Harrison, aged morning, up to 2 p.m. and the conditions were such as nineteen, residing at 42, David-street, IVidues. lie was to make cricket apparently impossible. Then the an apprenticed plumber at Messrs. Pilkingtoa's works. , weather suddenly cleared, and by the middle of the On ;Friday, duriug the dinner hour, he and another lad afternoon the game was everywhere in full progress. named Nolan went for a bathe in the Sankey and St. _— Helens Canal, near where they were employed. Shortly Warrington went to Newton in the pouring rain, and after entering the water, Harrison got into difilaulties, : found cricket quite possible at 4 o'clock. The home and was drowned before assistance could be proaured. lot went to the crease first and put together 142 for 9, The body was not recovered until the following even-'no less than 67 of the number coming from the bat of ing. The jury returned a verdict of Accident death." F. Harrison, who played a fine forcing game after WIDNES SECONDARY DAY SCHOOL SPOR TS. I being early missed in the long field. Cresswell did Owing to the inclement weather of Saturday the great execution on the soddea pitch, and his 8 wickets sports held under the auspices of the school were held for 41 runs was a capital performance for a fast bowler on Monday afternoon, before a capital crowd of specta- , under such Conditions. May he do as well to-day in tors, chiefly parents and friends of the boys. The I his benefit match against St. Helens! numerous events were watched with the keenest of I -- attention, and some splendid results were achieved. Past efforts on the Lowerhouse-lane Ground in connec- tion with these sport a were easily surpassed. Gossage's Band enlivened the proceedings during the afternoon. ENGINEER AND HIS MOTOR-CYCLE. On Monday, at Runcorn, James Wallis, of Church- road, Seaford', Liverpool, engiueer, pleaded guilty to having, on June 23rd, at Sutton Weaver' driven a motor-cycle without being registered ; aud, further, to fraudulently using certain marks for identifying the cycle. The defendant said he had only bought the cycle that day from Mr. Syvert, of 25, Burscough- street, Ormskirk, and the number plate was upon it. tie had not time to get it registered.—The case was adjourned for a fortnight in order that defendant might bring Mr. Syvert as a witness. WIDNES CONSERVATIVE GARDEN PARTY. Colonel W. Halt Walker, M.P. for Widnes Division, and Mrs. Hall Walker, entertained a large party of Conservative workers and friends in the division at Gateacre Grange on Saturday. Rain fell heavily to within an hour of the time fixed for the assembling at the Grange, but the ground recovered rapidly, and the gathering took place under almost ideal conditions. Excellent arrangements had been made for the comfort of the guests. The whole of the entertaining rooms at the Grange, and the magnificent conservatories, con- taining the famous vines, peach trees, and fig trees, just now ten with fruit, were thrown open, whilst the lawns on the floral terraces were set apart for bowls and tennis. Especial interest was shown in the large collection of cups, plate, and trophies which have been secuted by Colonel Walker on the turf, in the hunting and polo Held, and as a yachtsman. In the early even- ing Colonel Walker and Mrs. Jewett, and Alderman Jowett and Mrs. Walker led the dancing. There were no speeches. WIDNES LIBERALS ENTERTAINED. I On Saturday afternoon Mr. and Mr. Max Muspratt gave a garden party, at the Old Bluecoat School, Liver- pool, to about 400 Liberal workers of tho Prince's Park Ward, Liverpool, and of the Widnes Parliamentary I Division. The host and hostess were supported by Colonel J. E. B. Seeley, M.P. (Under-Secretary of State for the Colonies), Mr. Austin Taylor, aI.P., Dr. Permewan' Mr. and Mrs. Egetton Stewart-Brown, Mr. Clifford aluspratt and Mr. Horace aluspratt and others; while during the later proceedings there were present Mr. John Japp, Miss J app, Alderman \V. H. Williams, Mr. Albert E. Jacob, Mr. Barrington Jones and Mr. Alfred E. Davies. The Liberal agent for the Widnes Division (Mr. John Williamson) assisted in the arrange- ments, and Miss Ross's band played acceptably at the assembly and during tea, which was served at tables partly in the open. Rain threatened, but only a momentary shower fell, and the enjoyable programme was lengthened. The speeches, which, bad the sky been cloudless, were to have been delivered from an open- air platform, were made in the lecture hall, which was filled, no little enthusiasm being displayed. The Arts and Crafts Exhibition at present being held in the school was much appreciated by the visitors. THE MAY QUEEN'S PORTRAIT. The usual ceremony of making a presentation of a portrait of herself to the May Queen, on this occasion , Miss Gladys Edith Cropper; daughter of Mr. Arthur ' Cropper, took place at the Technical Institute, Runcorn, last Friday evening. Mr. I'. Dean' chairman of the Urban District Council, presided, and spoke in terms of appreciation of the manner in which Miss Cropper dis- charged the duties of her high office at the festival. The presentation was made by Mrs. Dean. A mis- cellaneous entertainment, which afforded much pleasure was contributed to 46 the Misses Devonport, Joynson, D. Pringle, L. Pringle, Wilcox, A. Crowther, and A. Turner, and Messrs. Worrall, Shaw, T. Clare, H. Lautdberg and E. Roberts. The accompanist was Mr. W. Grounds.—A cordial vote of thanks was passed to Mrs. Dean on the motion of Mr. Westbrook seconded by Mr. Hutton.—The Hon. Secretary (Mr. in the course of a report on the festival, said the Eaancial result was the main consideration and he thought every- one would admit that the not result was exceedingly satisfactory. All who had contributed in any way to the management were agreed that their sole object was to administer with the strictest economy. They had helped four most worthy institutions and that was the first thought and idea they had. The festival was a most pleasurable event for young and old, particulaily the former. The lion. Treasur3r (Mr. R. H. Pringle) submitted the balance sheet, and it was adopted on the motion of Mr. Grounds. CEMETERY HOTtL BowLING CLUB</p>
        <p>Warrington had 75 minutes to make 113 runs in, to lose, or to save the game. Not having a dessop handy they decided to adopt cautious tactics, and in putting together 69 runs for 4 wickets they did all that could be expected of them. Wright rattled up 25, J. W. Bibby 12, and Silcock, the captain, sat on the splice for some time for his 14 not out.</p>
        <p>Earlestown wore also at home and scored an easy victory at St. Helens' expense. Stanley and Prescut had to be content with dividing tho honours.</p>
        <p>The West Lancashire League stands as follows :</p>
        <p>- , P. Nt . L. D. trrs. Earlestown St. Helens Rees. Prescot Newton Warrington Stanley St. Helens</p>
        <p>\Carrington Second pulled themselves together again or Saturday,and took to heart the unexpected thrashing of the previous week. Consequently, Newton second string caught it hot. The start was delayed until four o'clock, but Newton, who batted first, failed against the deliveries of W. Bennett and P. B. Hawtin, the aggre- gate only reaching 84, of which number W. Rylance (44) and J. Pend y (24) batted ,k ell for their runs. Bennett six wickets for 3:3 runs, and Ilawtin three for 23, did exceptionally well considering the easy nature of the pitch. Warrington made light of the task, and some phenomenal scoring was witnessed in the first stages, P. B. Ilawtin and A. Thewlis forcing the pace so well that the requisite runs were actually obtained in the course of 33" minutes. Considering that Hawtin's chief characteristic is more of the defensive than defiance, his mile scoring was ail the more praiseworthy. lie completed his fifty in 35 minutes, and at the close was not out with 85 to his credit, not a faulty stroke marr- ing the exposition. Thewlis (85), J. W. Bainbridge (21), and W. V. Turton (24) played well for their runs.</p>
        <p>There was a close struggle in the District League bet‘reen Ryland and the " Guardian," the latter winning by 55 to 47, chiefly as the result of Burrell's bowling-5 wickets for IS runs.</p>
        <p>Tiewsey Wesleyans cot up rather badly against St. James', the whole crowd being out for 37 runs. The Saints were quite comfortable in passing the century by ten, double figure scores being quite plentiful. J. Lester healed the list with 23, and C. Holt captured 8 wickets for 12 runs, in addition to making the useful score of 23.</p>
        <p>Among the Juniors there was nothing very startling. St. James' did not equal the performance of their seniors, and 25 was a poor total to put up against Sankey's 114. Bradhurn lei the way for the winners with a capital 22, while it Dahatith hit up 17.</p>
        <p>Lovely-lane compiled the lowest score of the day, 23, against the Excelsiors, who had a modest but all sufficing 50 to their crAit. Caldwell had a lot to do with the poor display of the Lovely-laners, as he had six victims for 9 runs.</p>
        <p>The P. S; K. team reached the final of the Works Competition on Tuesday, when they got the better of the Corporation eleven by 49 to 3R. There was nothing very brilliant on either side, Dr. Smith and Cliffe getting 10 each for the Corporation, while.]. Thompson gave the forgeraen a good start with 1, Tarbuck following later with a useful 15. Mr. Dodds again ' appeared for the foremen and made a very good catch, which disposed of Cliffe.</p>
        <p>The Final between Messrs. Pearson and linowles and Walker's will take place on Thursday next on the Cricket ground, Arpley.</p>
        <p>-- Ifarshhouse-Tane did not easily get the better of St Mary's. the scores being 83 to 57. At one time they looked like " going down the nick," but a list wicket stand of 40 runs by F. Horrocks (251 and F. Cumin (15 not out) altered the state_ of affAirs. Warburton, for the winners, took flYe wickets for seven rans,</p>
        <p>Bowling. Lancashire and Cheshire played their return match with Yorkshire for the Crosfield Cup and medals at Huddersfield on Saturday, the venue being the ground of the Lindley club. The Tykes were very determined and won on the day by 63 points, which left them narrow losers of the home and away engagements be 6 points. Lancashire ran matters rather fine, and seeing that there is so much interest in the Competition now, they had better consider the advisability of playing their engagements on one green,-to which the men will have a chance of becoming accustomed.</p>
        <p>Ladies' Day. " Ladies! day " in connection with tha above club took place on Thursday in fine, though rather dull weather.3This, the fourth annual main, proved to be a record one in point of entries. The competitors numbered 63, and 48 prizes were distributed. The arrangement were in the hands of a committee, its chairman being Mr. J. Willett. Many thanks are due to the friends of the committee, who so kindly gave the prizes. Messrs. Crosfield's baud was in attendance and gave several very choice selections, which were very much enjoyed by those present. The prize winners, which we give in consecutive order, were as follows:—Miss Higham, Mrs. Jones, Mrs Cothant, Mrs. Hollins, Mrs. Bothwell, Mrs. Hill, Mrs. , Ellison, Miss Ball, Mrs. Willett, Mrs. Burke, Mrs. Holden Mrs. B. Jones, Mrs. Wilson, Mrs. A. R. Jones, Mrs. Holden, Mrs. A. Willett, Mrs. Webb, Mrs. Rathbone, Miss A. NVillett, Mrs. NV. Rostern, Miss Holden, Mrs. W. Gibson, Miss C. Rostern, Miss Tilling, Mrs. B. Gleave, Mrs. flumphris, Mrs. Barton, Mrs. T. Berns, Mrs. J. E. Harrison, Miss It. Baldwin, Mrs. Ferguson, Mrs. Wood and Mrs. Allison. The consolation prizes were awarded to Miss Ball, Mrs. Newton, Miss Littlewood, Mn. A. Foster, Mrs. Bolton, Mrs. Howarth, Mrs. Ford, Miss L. Baldwin, Miss M. Foster, Mrs. W. Taylor, Mrs. Povey, Mrs. Miller, Mrs. J. Willett, Mrs. Sunderland, Mrs. Maddock, Miss Povey and Mrs. Reddish. After Councillor Locker had presented the prizes to the fortunate o9mpetitors, he said he was very proud of the honour given him by the members of that club in their desire that he should present the prizes to the successful competitors in that most interesting com- petition. For many years he had spent hours at the game himself; but he little thought that he should ever see the day when so many ladies would be participating in that healthy exercise. To many mothers and daughter% of their members, who were toiling the week long, it must have been a very bright moment to look forward to. Votes of thanks were passed to Councillor Locker, and Mr. and Mrs. Williamson.</p>
        <p>'The games at Huddersfield were eagerly followed by a large numer of spectators, who in spite of some heavy showers of rain, were always in close attendance to cheer on their especial favourites.</p>
        <p>The Lancnshlre depnty-president and vice-president, Messrs. Coombs and Williamson, were the first two oil the green, each defeating their _opponent comfortably, viz., 21-12 and 21-3 respectively. The lead obtained by these two players, however, was nearly lost when G. Holbrook and H. Dawson went down.</p>
        <p>T. Ellison played a splendid game between the showers against J. Stead. Ellison went off with a rush and on a short length falling mark, about ten yards from the edge of the green, he scored 12-0, and there is no knowing how many more he would have got only for having to change his length on account of somebody else being in the way. However he did very well indeed, to win 21-0, which was the best win of the day for ;he visiting county.</p>
        <p>H. Scott (like Clark) who was making his initial appearance against Yorkshire, proved himself quite up to county form, and another who was making his debut J. Westhead, came through the ordeal very well. It was rather a surprise to the Tykes when their champion, W. Mitchell, was defeated after a tough struggle, by three chalks. _ _</p>
        <p>The game of the day was undonhteily that between T. Glover nnd G. P. Thewlis. They were level at 17, and the Yorkshireman's jack. Thewlis's first wood was about 3 yards short, and Glover's V, yards over. With his last wood Thewlis played almost a dead length, but a yard wide, at which Glover struck. He, however, msde a terrible mess of it,as instead of knocking his opponent . out he missed it aasily, and sent his first wood flying, thus leaving Thewlis two in.</p>
        <p>G. Ball, jun., had rather a surprise when he only chalked n against J. Garthwaite, but on the tricky green many of the Lancastrians did cot bowl as well as usual.</p>
        <p>In the Association second team Competition, Ball's Head lost at Heaton Mersey against Whitetield by 17 points-193-210. T. Rigby (8), \V. H. Eyes (8) and J. Bird (7) had a lot to do with the dropping of points.</p>
        <p>The Works Competition for the Finlesson Cup made progress on Saturday, and the semi-finals will be played to-day between P. and K. v. L. N.-W., and Ryland's No. I. v. Whiteeross No. 1. Referee, T. Makin,assistant W. llolford.</p>
        <p>In the matches on Saturday, Ryland's No. 1. regis- tered the most striking victory,, winning every game but three against Whitecross No. 2, and scoring 171 points to 1:35. The IThitccross men made a fair show individually, the lowest return being W. Ball's 8, against A. Stringer's 15.</p>
        <p>Pearson and Knowles triumphed over Houghton's No. 1, by 13 points-154 to 141. Seven games were worrhy the leaders against five, bat there was some close bowling. A. linstern, the I'. and K. captain gave his side a good start by winning 15-G. There was a G and a 7 on his side later on, but Houghton's went one worse with two six's and a seven.</p>
        <p>There were only two single figure returns in the match between L. and N.-W. and Monks Hall No. 1, the first-named winning by 163 to 145 and seven games to five. W. Hatton for the losers only got four chalks against J. Hutchinson, while J. Lythgoe just missed double figures with S against J. Rowlands. ,</p>
        <p>Naturally with over 100 entries, great interest has been shown in the competition at the Liberal Club for the Crosfield Challenge Cup. It is expected that the third round will be concluded to-day. Given fine weather there is sure to be some good bowling Id the Liberal Club.</p>
        <p>Rugby Football. The following clubs have entered the Warrington Junior Rugby League, viz.:—airfield, St. Elphin's, St. Mary's St. John's, Rylands Wet Mill, Greenhalgh's, Fletcher Russells, Park Juniors. Further entries may be sent in to Mr. R. C. Essenhigh, 201, Manchester road, up to and including Wednesday next, August sth. A meeting of the League will be held on the Football I field on Wednesday evening next.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>PALfGATE.</head>
        <p>PALfGATE.</p>
        <p>PRESENTATIONS To AR. AND MRS. WRAG:II%—m. r and Nlrs. Arthur Wright, the 6uperintendent and Matron of the Padgate Cottage Homes, were on Wednesdey afternoon the recipients of several presents from the officers and children in celebration of their silver wedding. Mr. and Mrs. Wright have been connected with the Institution for 12 years, during which time they have deservedly gained the respect not only of the members of the Board of Guardians and the Padgate Cottage Homes Committee, but of the officers and children, all of whom assembled in the schoolroom on the day in question, when the Chaplain (the Rev. G. W. Dickenson), who presided, presented the Superintendent and Matron on behalf of the officers with a handsome silver cake basket. The girls presented Mrs. Wright with a hake knife and the boys gave Mr. Wright a silver ash tray and the officers a silver flask.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>Swimming Gala.</head>
        <p>Swimming Gala.</p>
        <p>~ At the Warrington Corporation swimming baths on Thursday night there was a large assembly to witness the polo match between Wigan and Warrington. Wigan lost the toss and were called upon to defend the deep end which the did with great vigour. After a few minutes had elapsed Wigan scored the first goal The referee had occasion to blow his whistle several 1 times to caution players for foul play. The Wigan 1 goal-keeper did his share of the work, stopping a very 1 line shot from the Warrington side. Half time then intervened, the scores being Wigan 2 goals, Warring- ton nil. In the second half the play was very slow, the Warrington team making little headway. The Warrington goal-keeper (Woods) stopped a hot shot from one of the Wigan players. Wigan scored cleverly, shooting three goals in the second half. Warrington scored a goal in the last moments through Sutton. The final resulted: Wigan 5, Warrington 1. In addition to the polo match there were two other events including the first round of the 50 yards club handicap, which will be concluded on August 6th. There were two squadron races both being very inter- esting. The first which took place was between llylauds and Whitecross, the former being proclaimed the winner. The second should have been between the Corporation and W. D. Houghton Co., Ltd., the ilatter, however, did not appear, but to prevent dis- appointment a scratch team was formed against the Corporation. In this race the Corporation were the winners.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>TERRITORIALS AT PEEL _ WARRIIGTON MEN'S DEPARTURE LAST SUNDAY.</head>
        <p>TERRITORIALS AT PEEL _ WARRIIGTON MEN'S DEPARTURE LAST SUNDAY.</p>
        <p>The quietness of Sunday morning was broken by the huge calls of the departing " Terriers "or as we know them best the 4th Battalion Prince of Wales's Volun- teers ( -outh Lancashire Regiment). They were in command of the new Commanding °Meer, Lieutenant. Colonel J. D. Fairclough. Cuarming weather prevailed and thousands of people gathered in the streets to show their interest in our local defenders and to give them a hearty send-off on their way to Peel, Isle of Alan,where they will remain for a fortnight. The men fell in at 9 a.m, on the lawn in front of the Town Hall, and when all had assembled the total strength was 11 olficers and 443 men. This is exclusive of the Newton Company who went direct to Liverpool and joined the Warringtoniaus at the Landing Stage, so that the attendance at camp will be quite as large as was expected. Among others who saw the men assemble was Lieutenant-Colonel Edelsten, the late commander of the battalion, whose interest in its welfare appears to be as keen as ever.</p>
        <p>PRESENTATION OF PRIZES. After the usual medical inspection by Surgeon- Captain Adams, Lieut.-Colonel FAIRCLOUGII presented the prizes to the suceesssul nou-cotnmissioned officers in the examination at the end of the class of instruction held by Major Appleton, while acting adjutant during Cuptaia Mott's two months absence at a signalling 1 coarse at Strensall. The examination consisted of practical work and a written paper, and was conducted by Captain l%lott, assisted by Sergeant-Major Ingram, who had also assisted Major Appleton with the class. Marks were given for attendance, drill and for the ' written paper. The prizes were. provided by Major Appleton. Mr. Parr and Canon Stevenson was kind enough to lend land for the prlctical work -- Sergeants : 1, field glasses, Sergeant Richardson, Supply and Transport Company; 2, wrist watch, Sergeent T. S. Davies, F. Company ; 3, compass Sergeant G. Fogg, C. Company. Corporals : 1, wrist, watch, Corporal Dower, F Company; 2, Canteen Corporal Heacock, li Company •, 3, compass, Corporal Bossward, C Company. The Colonel expressed the" pleasure it afforded him to make the presentations, and also to see such a good muster. He gave the men their instructions with reference to the entraining and boat arrangements. Headed by the reemental band, under the direction of Bandmaster Richardson and the bugle band under the direction or Bugle-Major Plinston, the battalion then marched to the Central Station, through the huge gates and along Sankey-street and Horsecaarket-street, the route being lined with spectators. Only a few ptivi- leged persons were admitted on the platform. At 10.15 the special train, which had been chartered to convey the Territorials to Liverpool steamed into the station and promptly to time it left, amid the cheers of those on the platform and in the street below. THE NEW COLONEL Lieutenant-Colonel J. Dutton Fairclough received his commiision in April, IS)), so that he has been con- nected with the Volunteer movement for 18 years. He has passed through all grades (2nd Lieutenant, Captain, land Major). fleholds the (Q) certificate, having passed in tactics, topography, organization, military law, and military engineering. and has attended all the camps which have been held since he was commissioned with one exception. He served for a few months with the cadet corps of his school. He was born at %Carrington in the year 1872, and is the son of Mr. John Fairclough JP., of Latchford Grange. He was educated at the . Warrington Grammar School, and afterwards at a private school in Wales and at another at lippingham. . He has been connected with Mersey Mills for the past 14 years, and is a director and secretary of the firm. 1 An advance party under Captain Pottes, left for Peel on Wednesday of last week, and had a very busy time I and rough weather in preparing the camp. However, the gallant band of 20 had all in readiness for the arrival of the battalion on Sunday. The Warrington and St. Helms battalions embarked by the Sc. Viking, and reached Peel in good time after a most enjoyable passage. With the C.mainanding °ricer are Majors B. Fairclough and F. M. Appleton, Captain G. R. Mott, the adjutant; Quartermaster Potter, V.D., who since his arrival at Peel has been appointed staff camp- quartermaster; Captains G. R. Crosfield, C. L. Williamson, P. N. Avery, A. B. Layton, IL Owen, Lieutenants h'. R. Monks, It. 11. Lepage, Egt. Fairclough, alas Woods, and Second-Lieutenants W. McClure, 0. L. Whittle, L. S. Ilenshall, Eric Fairclough, H. 0. Adams, Surgeon-Majors J. Adams, Surgeon-Lieutenant W. J. Collins and Lieutenant R. Roberts, who is nocv attached to the Army Service Corps. Captain It. Smith is not expected until rest week, when he will come along in charge of those men unable to get to camp for the fall period. There are also three officers and 131 men from Newton-le-Willows, and with those of the Warrington Battalion already in camp, there is a grand total of 18 officers and ill men. The Brigadier-General is again Colonel J. H. Verscoyle, :tad acting with him as brigade-major is Major A.G. Cartwright. The other battalions in camp , are 450 of the Liverpool Scottish, a floe body of men, lall in kilts, over Zift. Bio., under the direction of Colonel Menne, VD.. and the 9th Liverpool. under Colonel S. H. Perry, V.D., about 700 strong, and sth South Lancashires from St. Helens, Widnes and Prescot. under the command of Colonel It. W. H. Thomas, V.II, a battalion 720 strong. Besides these men are three companies of the \Vest Lancashire Medical Ambulance Corps, who hail from Kendal, Blackpool, Penrith, Liverpool awl St. Helens. They are well equipped with about 53 horses for the field ambulance carts, waggons and transport, and have a field hospital which will be available in case of accidents and sickness in the South Lancashire Brigade. The camping ground is very pleasantly situated on a gently sloping hillside, which culminates a few hundred yards away in Contrary Head, overlooking the sea, where the coastguard station. In the opposite direction a few miles away is Greeba Mountain made more famous by its overlooking the residence of that famous author, Hall Caine, whilst:away southwards is the com- manding height of South Burnie.- During Sunday night there was heavy rain, which ' fortunately cleared before the first parade, but the grass was wet, making it decidedly unpleasant getting ' about. To exercise tho men they were taken on to the adjoining road and given a good stiff dose at the double. The idea of those in authority is to warm the ' men up and to prevent the damp doing any damage. The drills during the day were of the company type, ' such as firing exercise, &amp;c., under the supervision of the ' Colonel. During the midday parade heavy showers fell : and all quickly took shelter, but the rain did not last long and for the remainder of the day there was a spell of brilliant sunshine, culminating in a glorious ' sunset. The men had their first field practice on Wednesday. The 4th South Lancashire (Warrington) Battalion marched out shortly after nine o'clock. and half of them were detailed off to capture Peel Hill, the others having to defend the position. The attack did not succced. Other battalions had similar practice, and the Liverpocl Scottish had a particularly heavy day. There was a scorching hot sun, and the men were glad to get . to.get back to camp, after which there was a rifle inspection. The men by now have had an opportunity L of contrasting the new with the old conditions of I Volunteer camping, and from all quarters the verdict is unanimous that great improvements have been made in ' the conditions of service and of the usefulness of the ! citizen Army. ' The Territorials of \Carrington had an easy time on ' Thursday. Rain fell in the early morning and upset the plans for the day. The officers in command con- tented themselves with a parade and inspection, bat , yesterday it was intended to strike tents and march I out at an early hour to spend twenty hours on Peel hill • in mimic warfare.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
