      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>tax Routs re.szavEu.]</head>
        <p>tax Routs re.szavEu.]</p>
        <p>LOVE'S SECOND DREAM.</p>
        <p>affair!" he concluded. "The patient ia a ia of more than usually perfect physique." It was many days before Rosamond saw her loser. The patient's progress had not been quite so satisfactory as the doctors had antici- pated; they prescribed perfect quiet and, &amp;bora all, freedom from anxiety. But the grim shadow of anxiety was already the sharer of Rosara..nd's uueasy couch. The knowledge which bet friends, by skilful evasions and soothing assur- ances, had withheld from her, had come to I.er by an unfortunate chance. She had been lying ; AlOr</p>
        <p>alone, between sleeping and waking, one alter- I noon. The drowsy humming of the bees Coated. by the open window. mingling with the voice of her companion- on the filmy borders of dream- land over which she. was hovering,. The shadowy ' form by her sido was Maurice Waring's, and the words that floated tremulously on the vibrating drone of the bees were almost the last words the had heard from his lips:—" I love you! Heavent hew I love you!' :She had heard them so often • in her dreams that they had become like a familiar refrain fraught with tender ass.ocia- tions. They lulled her to deeper sleep, a more complete abandon to the gracious embrace of oblivion encircling her as his arms had encircled her on that- memorable dcy. But now the .silence was broken abruptly by the sound of a shrill voice rising above the murmur of insect life without. The words fell on her ear. recalling her to consciousness, and lingering like an echo in her startled mind as she turned. wide-eyed and alert towards the window. "Poor cousin Rcsamond !" floated up in the clear, far-reaching voice of a child. "Never to waik again?" "Hush!" replied the child's companion: "she will walk. bet—" The rest was lost as the two moved away. Rosamond's heart stood still for a moment as she sank back, white and trembling, to the pil- lows. The walls of the room accrued to dip threateningly down upon her, swinging up as suddenly, while her head swam round, and the blood sang in her shrinking ears. Fcr an hour she lay alone, turning in her startled mind the possibilities of one word, till it grew to a hideous fiend, termenting her till she longed to cry aloud the question which she dared not summon her nurse to answer. The terrible fut. which she had heard following the verdict which had just reached her ears, took a dozen terms of sugges- tion, each warded off by a piteous cry from the heart—a refusal to grasp the possibility of its realisation. She who had been so strong of limb, so physically independent, whose chief endow- ment had been a supple ease of motion and the delight in its practiee! A helpless cripple—the thing which in all 'humanity had most Invoked her pity and shrinking when she met it! And, suddenly, her thoughts flew to Maurice Waring, the man who bad turned to her iii love as she was then, not as the thing she would be. Would he Still cling to her? Ifer heart told her that it could not be. He would learn to forget, and she —she had her whole life to lead, crippled and alone! A great trembling took her limbs as she looked round the room, grown strangely un- familiar. She hid her eyes in her quivering hands, and wished she had the power of ready tears which some women have. When her nurse came in, she lay with closed eyes, feigning sleep —the question which she wanted to ask would not take shape on her lips. But suddenly, in the middle of the quiet attentions of the woman, who had won her heart from the first, she broke into a low wail, that would not be stilled. "I know, nurse!" she sobbed, when the storm had nearly ,abated. "I know! I heard little Flora under the window!" So the mischief was done, and Rosamond's convalescence was a protracted and somewhat anxious one to her friends. For herself, the greatest stimulant, the desire for recoVesay, had gone. There were times when she wished she had died on the moor that day, the happiest of her life, before the sunshine had caught that strange power of mockery that was harder to bear than gloom. But the day dame, in spite of her slow re- covery, when Rosamond could no longer put off the ordeal which she knew awaited her with the corning of 'Maurice Waring. She had clung to the respite, knowing that she would need all her strength for the task before her, if she were not, in spite of her resolution, to break down before the opposition of the masterful will, the only kind of nature that could have revealed to her heart its own weakness. 'Bitter as the interview would be, the days that followed would be still more bitter: and she shrank from putting the seal to the resolution which, though it never wavered, wrung her heart in the necessity. Waring, she was determined, must not make the sacrifice which she never doubted his honour, if not his love for her, would dictate to him. But with all her preparation she had scarcely* gauged the endurance that would be needed to carry her through the interview. He came in with a quiet step, subduiag a demonstrative im- pulse that, with him, was the stronger for its rarity; but she was as conscious of the force of his passion as she had been when, under her win- dow in the blaze of sunlight, he had lifted her speechless to the saddle that fatal morning. For one minute she was as helpless in the vibrating response which swept over her, the next, she had thrown off the dizziness, and was looking clearly into the eyes that searched her own. She broke the throbbing silence with trembling lips that al- most refused to do the bidding of her will: This time it is good-bye," she said, with wavering tones. "Yoti will make it a short one, Maurice? I am very weak." Her hope lay in that, her physical weakness would be a tower of refuge. He took again the hand that she had with- drawn, and, not a trace of the quick concern at her words escaping him, looked •steadily out to the fields sleeping in the afternoon sun. "I had been thinking, Rosamond," he said, deliberate tones that were natural to him, that morning before the storm, that _if ever I could hope to become worthy of you, of winning you, it would be the love of you that would raise roe to that fitness. During the past few weeks," he went on, the hand that held her own trembling a little, "you have grown, if possible, still dearer to me. I love you more than my own soul. Do you doubt me, Rosamond?" She bovscd her head, hiding the wave of exul- tation that swept over her. "Doubt is no word for our use. Maurice," she said, a little breathlessly. "No, there is but one word for our use," ho exclaimed: 'lt is love, Rosamond. I have been counting the hours since your message. Lean- not live without you!" She dared not look up and meet the love which she knew was burning in his eyes. "This is no hasty decision, Maurice," she said entreatingly. "I know all you would say. Spare me!" "My darling," h? pleaded. • I would sparo</p>
        <p>,-you! All the weeks I have been planning for our happiness. Arc icy own hopes nothing to you?" 1' "I have planned too," she said with a quiet emphasis—" planned for your sake—and my own. Some day you will thank me in your heart. There was an air of moral strength about her that awakened in him a chill doubt. Hitherto he had thought that the matter was to be one of painful argument merely: he had never really questioned the issues. "What is it, Rosamond?" he asked, hoarsely. "For Heaven'e sake, let there be no misunder- standing between us!" "No, no," she replied, "I do not doubt you, Maurice. I know your—your goodness, but it is best that we should part now, and save future pain for us both." - He turned and strode impatiently to the win- dow. "Do. you talk of pain?" he -cried, hotly. "You are cruel! You cannot mean it, Roea- mond! You do not know what love is! You I have never loved me!" • I She covered her eyes, in tehich she felt the love-light leap flickeringly. Perhaps it were better that he should think her cold. "Rosamond," he pleaded, timing and seizing her hands, -look up at me! Dear, I 'came to take you in my arms, and you can turn away from me t'atas! Love,." he murmured brokenly, bending ever her till she felt his breath in :lei eyee, "you are mine! There is nothing can part us—nothing in life. Do not fear the future, Rosamond, I love you more than life. For ma the future holds no happiness without you!" His voice, tremulous with passion, seemed to , grow strangely distant End unreal to her; but, in I the physical weakness that was creeping over her, lay her chief safety. It troucht a numbing weariness that took away the keennesa of hie I reasoning, the power to respond to the appeals I which wrung her heart. "I love you too well to let you sacrifice your- self. Maurice," she said, quietly—" too well to let you blind my reason. I shill never change. Will you say good-bye?" He stood looking down at her with corn- preessed lips. "If I were a helpless log, with I nothing in life left to me, save yon." he went on . unheeding, "I would ask nothing better— nothing but your love and your compasion."- [ He saw the light that leaped with the tears unrestr.aine.d to. her..eyes.. "If it had been you,"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>By ANITA GIBSON,</head>
        <p>By ANITA GIBSON,</p>
        <p>Author of "Poses and Tears," "A TwiTigb Adventure," " Thc:r Study of Psychology," "Fluffy," &amp;a.</p>
        <p>As Rosamond Fleet fastened her riding-habit with trembling fingers that August morning, there was a little fear in her heart of the new happiness that possessed her; but a greater fear of going to meet that ehich she had known since yesterday was to befall her to-day—the thing which she would not have missed for the world. She took up her riditil-whip and turned to leave the room, but hesitated before the mirror, which reflected her as she turned, criticising her face, but seeine nothing of the charm which lay in her personality rather than in any claim to great beauty of feature. She was a woman of magnetic power and intense femininity, com- bined with a grace of shape and movement, subtle attractions of whide she was unconscious. Standing by the horses' heads below, in a flood of sunlight, Magritte Waring awaited her. She stood for a whole minute looking down at him, loth to break the spell that hung over her like the hush before daybreak. The joy that was to come to her this morning could not come twice in her life. She felt like a child that saves its candy for as long as it has the strength. When the power had gone, she went down with quick steps, out into the dazzling sunlight, and put her hands into his without a word, as he helped her to mount. He gave her one swift look as their bands met, and leaped speechless to the saddle. Commonplaces had no part in the golden day, and her own silence had set his pulses leaping more than the touch of her hand. He turned his horse's head in the direction -which their daily rides had taken for the past week, across the moor to tho hollow by the stream, where the path was too narrow to ride abreast, and out into the open, where he would join her. She knew the moment had come when the path widened, and she felt his hand on her own grasping the bridle. "Rosamond!" Her heart leaped, but she made no answer. It Was the first time she had heard her name in its fulness. Surely our name is given us for love to use in the calling. She wanted to hear it again. "Rosamond," he said, forcing her to meet his gaze, "you know—l am sure you know—" -His voice grew thick, and, in the momentary pause, he thought she swayed in the saddle. A short giddiness had overtaken her; but she threw it off as she brushed away the mist from her sight. '"I know," she said, almost deprecatingly, SS he Rung himself from his horse. His arms were round her-as she sat, his eyes raised to her own turned down upon him, fasci- slated. There was a look of waiting on her face. He saw the -expectation dilating her eyes, lit evith the light of love, waiting for the sweetest of mortal words. "I love you!" he said, With vibrating voice. 4. Heaven! how I love you!" She bent down with a sudden trembling weak- ness, as he lifted her from the saddle and kissed her eyes with lips that were cold with passion. A faint breeze stirred the grasses to soft whis- pers, and, fainter still, on the hillside, rose the sibilant chorus of the grasshoppers, the shimmer of the burning August sunshine expressed in sound. Above the golden fire of the dwarf furzes, between the purple mantles of the cloud- shadows, azure-rfinged butterflies sailed and flut- tered like soft sighs escaped from the lips of love. And, over all, the meaning of it all vibra- ted tremulously—"l love you! Heaven! how I love you!" Presently she lifted her head and took the mare's bridle, walking erect, but conscious of a little uncertainty in her stens, the gladness in her rieart rising anew and thrilling her like the re- curring of a subtle interval in a familiar refrain. They walked in silence. Waring was a man of intense feeling, and his words were few. Both knew what was in the other's heart, and it was enough. The full meaning of life had been dis- closed to them, and the revelation walked by the side of each. With her a very simple thought rose above the complexity of her emotions like a soothing hand. She would never be lonely again! Love had come to her, illumining the past and the future. The past was no longer meaningless, but the approach to the gate of life where love had met her. The future would hold love, not as a memory, but a flame undying, uneuenchelle. She could not give love and recall it, any mare than she could recall the breath of Iles nostrils:. There was something of fear in the thought that already the floodgates were opened, and the tor- rent must flow on, whether she willed it or not. Her thoughts were broken by the touch of hii. ihand on her own. "Come!" he said hastily, "there is a storm gathering fast!" In the roseate dream that had wrapped them they had not seen the golden flush fading from the moor. The music of the insects had ceased, and an unbroken hush fallen upon the purple- clouded hills. Already, as they mounted, a low volley of thunder broke at the horizon and spread over their heads, as the first scuttercd sirens of rain pattered among the furzes. Waring scanned the darkening sky anxiously. "I am afraid we shall not escape it," he said, looking at Rosamond questioningly. She laughed hack carelessly; a mad gallop through the storm was the fittest expression of the mood of wire- *taint that possessed her. , The next minute, in a blinding torrent, the gain came down, lashing tire outstretched heads of the horses, and streaming from their quiver- ing flanks. Rosamond laughed again, but a Intl° uneasily; the gathering darkness and the unceas- ing beating of the rain was disconcerting, almost bewildering. She drew rein, pausing: for a moment's respite from the driving wind that caught her breath. Waring's voice, shouting to her to keep close, was almost lost in the swirling tempest. At the moment a deafening crash Of thunder. accompenied by a vivid flesh of violet lightning, terrified her mare. Perhaps the state, which Rosamond was unable to restrain, in- creased the panic of the nervous beast. With distended nostrils and quivering muscles, she drew back stiffly for a moment ere starting head- long across the moor, a mad course that Rosa- mond was powerless to check. With an exclamation. Waring turned and fol- lowed. Through the whole of the run lie was net mere than twenty feet away from her, and twie e his hand was within an inch of her bridle</p>
        <p>Ile shouted advice, which she -tried to obey, but her arms ached to numbness, and the strength that she could wield in the breathless race, seemed little more than that of a babe. Again and again she tried .to turn the head of the brute, but she could not; and she knew, as well .as Waring, that somewhere just ahead of them must run the deep waggon track that cut the crest of the hill in twain. Could they clear it? Another minute and they were on it. With a momentary, indecision that spoilt her kap, the mare cleared the low hedge, strained frantically for a footing on the further bauk, and rolled backwards to the streaming cutting with a thud that turned Waring first sick, then furious with rage. ..-- rle flung himself from the isaddle and scram- bled down the steep incline, lashing madly at the prostrate beast with his whip- Twice it eesaved to rise, and twice it sank back 'exhaueteel while Waring, shouting curses on it, endeavotati in vain to snatch the motionless form of Rosa- mond from beneath it. Blinded with horror, lie draw back, the scene swimming before him, sod, for a few moments, his trembling limbs lest the rower to support him. lie reeled round the mare, sweeping hie hands across his brow and flinging the sweat, from his fingers: then, in a rage o, de- spair, he lashed fast and furiously again at the brute, maddening it to a fresh effort to rise, one bind leg hanging limp and ueelees, as it stag- gered forward. In a moment, Waring had caugat the death-like form of Rosamond to his brt. Be laid her in the long, wet grass by the road- side, calling her name, and waiting for the re- sponse which; did not come. The closed eves gave no sign, and, thoueh he held his cold palm before the parted hip, he could detect no birath issuing from them, and his eager hand, rreeeing the hollow of her bosom, shook so thet he could not tell whether any life throbbed there. With, a rick fear at his heart he turned helplessly away, and, at the instant, his ear caught the bound of slow-moving wheels. They quickened in. respone.e to. his cries for help, and in a few minntes, aich:d br the awe-stricken waggoners, he Ives beaein he still burden homeward, under the Ai sunshine that streamed down derisively, roaeiee his dozed mind to a dull wonder whether the storm and it ghastly sequel were not all a hideous dream from which he would pzree:lLly to mercifully permitted to eeee'ee. • • •</p>
        <p>Rosamond did not die. A few hours after hie s s-Ve--eo—ga—ea--:-+" but it s'is .different. A man7tnest -hasty summons, the surgeon was able to give .2 : live his life. Think, Maurice—on the day that reassuring verdict. Waring had been waiting! you wearied of me —" • below; it seemed to him a weeez sineo the :ran j He started as though he had been stung. "I had entered the hushed room from wh:ch he now will not think of it!" he cried. "May Heaven -emerged, giving final directions with the aelibe.• judge me, Rosamond, if that day should ever ration born of long familiarly with such scenes. come!" Elie would not die, he replied to Waring's eager 4 She grew dizzy in the rush of tenderness that questions; at least, there was no present cause to swept over her, and again her weakness became anticipate such a contingency. The case was her refuge, almost in 'spite of herself. -serious, yes. No vital injuries, fortunately, a bad "Maurice," she said, with a pathetic appeal in fracture of the left femur, at the juncture with her eyes. "I am tired!" the acetabulum. The effects would be lasting—: The deathly pallor of her face and the dark he was sorry to have to say, lifelong: ' Shadows of weariness in her eyes struak him Waring flinched: "You mean se will be— suddenly with remorse. He was helnless to-day. fame?" he asked with an involuntary clenching but ne would net believe that her resolve was of the hands. final. He bent vier- her, and reading no denial s rz, saii.e for li!'n," 0,-+ ether replied, lock- in her eyes, kissed her with a tenderness that ia6 with some atte;die:n - et Warin's white face bore a greater homage than lies in the unre- with =is •*":4 4ineptable straint of passion. To him it we, good be br _</p>
        <p>a day, but, in her heart, she knew that he must not come back; that a repetition of to-day wore more than cruel. She wanted to throw her arms around him and bid him stay, to return the kisses still thrilling in her heart, to give voice to the love that was welling in her tor- tured breast. He saw the anguish in her face, and hesitated—another moment and it would be too late. "Good-bye!" she said, bur. riedly—" Good-bye!" and turned her head. To see him go, thus, out of her life, was more than her heart could endure.</p>
        <p>TS</p>
        <p>Waring had hoped for other and better op- portunities to break down the fixed resolve in which he could scarcely believe Rosamond was capable of persisting. Could he but meet her de- prived Of the armour of physical frailty which, he felt, had cruelly turned aside his weapons of appeal at their meeting! He felt that he had been unfairly used. To -have taken her ty storm, in the abandon of passion, as he had taken her that morning on the moor would, he felt, have been a sure way to victory, and that way had been denied. him. But Rosamond was indomitable in her detarmination to grant him no further interviews. On the day following his visit, he received a note from her, setting forth more clearly and even more emphatically her recognition of the duty of renunciation which lay upon her. Nothing, she repeated, could ever shake her resolution; and indeed the ordeal which she had passed safely through had given her increased strength while opening her eyes to her weakness. In spite of repeated ap• peals, she refused to see Waring again, and when, in the early winter, he learned that she had gone abroad for change, without acquaint- ing him of her intention, he felt that her sin- cerity of purpose was equal to his .own. Rosamond had hoped that Waring would for- get her. Had she wished to become even more necessary to his happiness, she could not have taken a wiser course than the abrupt departure which emphasised his loss to him in its full force. Some men might have resorted to travel, or other kinds of dissipation, as a means of banishing the image of the beloved one from their heart's throne. Waring had neither the power nor the inclination to forget. Instead, he turned over in his mind every means which sug- gested itself as a possible ladder for scaling the fortress of Rosamond's will. He had always thought his own will was an iron one, and to be thwarted by a woman, the one woman over whom his power should have been complete, was unendurable. He chafed against his sentence. and the apparent coldness of heart which had permitted Rosamond to pronounce it, till he could no longer remain inactive, and then, such. denly the inspiration came to him. He had often wished that the crushing blow had fallen upon himself, that he could have borne the pain of those. long weeks instead of Rosamond, could have thrown himself, maimed and dependent, upon the passionate pity which he knew would have awaited him. He had repeated her words, lingering tenderly in his memory---" If it had been YOU "—till, at last, light broke upon him, and he thought he saw a sure way of making her hold out in pity the hands which she would not trust to him in his strength. He went Abroad, ostensibly to shoot big game, but, in reality, his tiger-hunting expedition was but a detail in the more significant hunt which employed his days. . He was looking for a...man who would be willing to assist him in his scheme, for a consideration, which he was willing to make substantial enough. At Calcutta, the city of appalling slut s andr delightful- gardens, he hoped, if other resources failed, to obtain the services of an acquaintance of his college days. George Doolan, an Army surgeon, whose life had been wrecked by a fatal weakness—the drink habit—and whose melan- choly career had been recently well-nigh termi- nated by the indulgence of his weakness in the hot climate, was the man to whom Waring turned as his abettor in the scheme upon which his mind ,was fixed. .</p>
        <p>He found him convalescent in th• cantonment hospital, a prey to the despair of mind born of his enforced abstinence and the leisure for con- templating his position which, he had every reason to believe, was, professionally, beyond hope. In the course of his chaguered career, Deolan had figured in not a feragurprising and dramatic situations; but the unempected appear- ance of Waring, and the unique proposition which he confided in him, made him open his eyes wider than they had been for many days. He gave a low whistle, letting his glance fall to his long, capable fingers lying idly on his knees. "By Jove I Waring," he said, under hie breath, "have you thought it well out in cold blood?"</p>
        <p>"Fully," replied Waring. decisively. waste your breath. Doolan." "It's not my affair." replied the surgeon, non- chalantly: "When will you have it done?" "As soon as you can be ready," answered Waring. "I've taken Jephson's bungalow, where I can lie up, and the sooner I can get out of this furnace the better I shall like it."</p>
        <p>"Don't</p>
        <p>"I expect to be out in a week or two," said Doolan. "If you haven't changed your mind." So Waring had his two weeks before him, the last weeks of the old life, before turning a new page. He filled in some of the interval by- run- ning off to Rangoon; leaving Calcutta with its intolerable atmosphere and brown, opaque river, to steam down a fairer Hooghly, where the hanks gave glimpses of -sleeping villages beyond the fringe of palms and rushee, and out to the cool, sapphire sea. In eight days he was back in Calcutta, with still ti week before him ere Doolan would be ready to carry out his share of the project. In the cool of the day, he went out each morn- ing and walked over the common of the Maiden to the plains beyond; walked till his weary limbs refused to carry him a step further. It was a curious pastime. in a land where walking is al- most a, forgotten art among the Europeans, but each day he . was seized anew by the desire to taste once more the joy of tare, unfettered motion which was to remain his for so short a time, and to feel stealing over him the las.situds which would make the long rest in the shade a privilege not less valuable. It was chiefly for the take of acquiring the habit of resting that the long, tiring expeditions were taken. He wanted to get used to lifo under- an aspect entirely new to him: to cultivate the art of being still. At the end of the second week Deolan was ready to undertake the duty which he had pledged himself to perform. He had already betaken himself to his old habit, but in one of his sober moments—a very sober moment for the pair of them—he accom- plished the operation which left Waring, for the remainder of his life, paralysed in one leg. The thing had been quite simple—a careful severing of the sciatic nerve—and Waring had in his possession a weapon of weakness which he felt to be without flaw. It was in the following spring that he returned to England:- Since hi st " accident " he haat re- sumed correspondence with Rosamond. His last letter had told her of his speedy • return, but not 'is word of supplication had escaped him: he was coming to her without any implied doubt as to his reception. His letters breathed the necessity for the sympathy and aid which she alone could bring into his crippled existence. And Rosamond, restless and expectant, with a yearning in her heart that grew to a sweet pain as the days passed, waited; all thought of her own sorrow forgotten in the new care that had come into her life. Maurice needed ber help. Through the lengthening clays, the waiting look in her eyes grew to a dawn of hope that found - gracious response in the face of nature, like her - sad, learning to smile anew after the chill stupor Of winter. And in April, when the peeping sun was turning into diamonds the raindrops that trembled on leaf and. flower in Rosamond's gar- den, she put her hands once rnoro into Waring's after the long separation, the hands that were henceforth to remain in his keeping, as her heart had remained throughout. " The gods would not have me spoilt, Rosa- mond," be said with a grim smile—" but, dearest. I am their debtor still." "Hush, lafaurice! " she replied, tremulously, "I have grown timid. Do not let them guess how happy we are." "Ah ! but the gods are not deceived," he ra. plied, archly; "even the blind Cupid will soma,- - times whisper a hint in our ear." Of the fulL meaning of which speech she was ignorant, for, to the end of her life. she never , learnt that Waring's disabling shaft had, not oome direct faona-the hand of the Fates.</p>
        <p>[Tilt END.]</p>
      </div>
