      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday August 1, 1908.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday August 1, 1908.</p>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>PAROCHIAL COMMITTFE.—The monthly meeting of the above was held last Tuesday evening in the Hall Caf4, under the chairmanship.of Mr. G., Pilling. 'The following members of the committee were present : Mr. H. J. Westbrook, Mr. 'l'. R. Westbrook, Mr. F. Pearson, Mr. 3. W. Bonchier, Mr. J. McDermind, Mr. Mr. J. Whitley, Mr. G. J. Brown, Mr. J. Swinton (surveyor) and Mr. E. Hough (assistant secretary.)—The Chairman said that since the corner street had been made the drain in Whitetleld-rood did not seem to be large enough, for it flooded property and was very objectionable. He read a letter from Mr. Bouchier, who complained about the flooding, of his cellar.—lt was decided that Mr. Swinton shenld do the best he could to deal with the nnisance.—Mr. Brown referred to the letter from the contractors for the paving work at, Stockton Heath, who asked for 10 days to complete a contract elsewhere. They had already had a month, and it Naas not fair that the men should be kept oft their work for so long.—Mr. Pilling said that the men did net go away at the time intended, but a few days after- wards. They did not mate any use of the privilege they had given them.—lt was decided that they should write to the contractors about the delay.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>Cricket.</p>
        <p>Fortunately cricketers are a hopeful kind. Had they been accustomed to despair early there would have been no cricket last Saturday. Rain fell in torrents all morning, up to 2 p.m. and the conditions were such as to make cricket apparently impossible. Then the weather suddenly cleared, and by the middle of the afternoon the game was everywhere in full progress.</p>
        <p>Warrington went to Newton in the pouring rain, and found cricket quite possible at 4 o'clock. The home lot went to the crease first and put together 142 for 9, no less than 67 of the number coming from the bat of F. Harrison, who played a line forcing game after being early missed in the long field. Cresswell did great execution on the sodden pitch, and his 8 wickets for 41 runs was a capital performance for a fast bowler under such conditions. May he do as well to-day in his benefit match against St.Helens !</p>
        <p>Warrington had 75 minutes to make 113 runs in, to lose, or to save the game. Not having a Jessop handy they decided to adopt cautious tactics, and in putting together 69 runs for 4 wickets they did all that could be expected of them. Wright rattled up 25,1 W. Bibby 12, and Silcock, the captain, sat on the splice for some time for his 14 not out.</p>
        <p>Earlestown were also at home and scored an easy victory at St. Helens' expense. Stanley and Prescot had to be content with dividing the honours.</p>
        <p>The West Lancashire League stands as follows :7</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. PPS. Earlestown St.Helens Kees. Prescot Newton Warrington Stanley St. Helens</p>
        <p>Warrington Second pulled themselves together again on Saturday,and took to heart the unexpected thrashing 'of the previous week. Consequently, Newton second string caught it hot. The start was delayed until four o'clock, but Newton, who batted first, failed against the deliveries of W. Bennett and P. B. Hawtin, the aggre- gate only reaching 94, of which number W. Rylance (44) and J. Pendry (24) batted well for their runs. Bennett six wickets for 33 runs, and Hawtin three for 2, did exceptionally well considering the easy nature of the pitch. -- Warrington made light of the task, and some phenomenal scoring was witnessed in the first stages, P. B. Hawtin and A. Thewlis forcing the pace so well that the requisite runs were actually obtained in the course of 33 minutes. Considering that Irawtin's chief characteristic is more of the defensive than defiance, his fine scoring was all the more praiseworthy. Ho completed his fifty in 35 minutes, and at the close was not out with 85t° his credit, not a faulty stroke marr- ing the exposition. Thewlis (35), J. W. Bainbridge (24), and W. V. Turton (24) played well for their runs.</p>
        <p>There was a close struggle in the District League bet,reen Rylauds and the " Guardian, " the latter winning by 5g to 47, chiefly as the result of Burrell's bowling-5 wickets for 18 runs.</p>
        <p>Bewsey Wesleyans out up rather badly against St. James', the whole crowd being out for 37 runs. The Saints were quite comfortable in passing the century by ten, double figure scores being quite plentiful. J. Lester headed the list with 23, and C. Holt captured 8 wickets for 12 runs, in addition to making the useful score of 20.</p>
        <p>Among the Juniors there was nothing very startling. St. James' did not equal the performance of their seniors, and 23 was a poor. total to put up against Sankey's 114. Bradhurn led the way for tbe winners with a capital 22, while H. Daintith hit up 17.</p>
        <p>LoVely-lane compiled the lowest score of the day, 23, against the Excelsiors, who had a modest but all sufficing Nto their credit. Caldwell had a lot to do with the poor display of the Lovely-laners, as he had six victims for 9 runs.</p>
        <p>The P. &amp; K. team reached tho final of the Works Competition on Tuesday, when they got the Lettsr of the Corporation eleven by 49 to 38. There was nothing very brilliant on either side Dr. Smith end Cliffe getting 10 each for the Corporation, while J. Thompson gave the forgernen a good start with 18, Tarbuck following later with a useful 35. Mr. Dodds again appeared for the forgemen and made a very good catch, which disposed of Cliffe.</p>
        <p>The Final between Messrs. Pearson and Knowles and Walker's will take place on Thursday next on the Cricket ground, Arpley.</p>
        <p>Marshbouse-lane did not easily get the better of St Mary's, tbe scores being 86 to 57. At one time they looked " going down the nick," but a lest wicket stand of 40 runs by F. Horrocks (25) and F. Carman (15 not out) altered the state of affairs. Warburton, for the winners, took five wickets for seven runs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>Bowling.</head>
        <p>Bowling.</p>
        <p>Lancashire and Cheshire played their return match with Yorkshire for the Crosfleld Cup and medals at Huddersfield on Saturday, the venpe being the ground of the Lindley club. The Tykes were very determined and won on the day by 63 points, which left them narrow losers of the home and away engagements be 6 points. Lancashire ran matters rather fine, and seeing that there is so much interest in the Competition now, they had better consider the advisability of playing their encagcraents on one green, to which the men will have a chance of becoming accustomed.</p>
        <p>The games at Huddersfield were eagerly followed by a large number of spectators, who in spite of some heavy showers of rain, were always in close attendance to cheer on their especial favourites.</p>
        <p>The Lancashire depnty-president and vice-president, Messrs. Coombs and Williamson, were the first two off the green, each defeating their opponent comfortably, viz., 21-12 and 21—S respectively. The lead obtained by these two players, however, was nearly lost when G. Holbrook and 11. Dawson went down. T. Ellison played a splendid game between the showers against J. Stead. Ellison went off with a rush and on a short length falling mark, about ten yards from the edge of the green, he scored 12-0, and there is no ',cowing how many more he would have got only for having to change his length on account of somebody else being in the way. However he did very well indeed, to win 21-6, which was the best win of the day for the visiting county.</p>
        <p>11. Scott (like ,Clark) who was making his initial appearance against Yorksbirp, proved himself quite up to county form, and anotherl7who was making his debut, J. Westhead, came through the ordeal very well. It was rather a surprise to the Tykes when their champion, W. Mitchell, was defeated after a tough struggle, by three chalks. . . .</p>
        <p>The game orthe day was undoubtedly that between T. glover and G. P. Thewlis. They were level at 17, and' the Yorkshireman's jack. Thewlis's first wood was about 3 yards short, and Glover's 2 yards over. With his last wood Thewlis glared almost a dead length, but ft yard wide, at which Glover struck. He, however, made a terrible mess of it,as instead of knocking his opponent out he missed it easily, and sent his first wood flying, I thus leaving Thewlis two in.</p>
        <p>G. Ball, jun., Mid rather a anrprise when he only chalked 9 against. J. Garthwaite, but on the tricky green many of the Lancastrians did tot bowl as well as usual.</p>
        <p>In the Association second team Competition, Ball's Head lost at Heaton Mersey against IVhitefleld by 17 points-193-210. T. Rigby (8), W. H. Eyes (8) and J. Bird (7) had a lot to do with the dropping of points.</p>
        <p>The Works Competition for the Finlayson Cup made progress on Saturday, and the semi-finals will be played to-day between P. and K. v. L. N.-W., and Ry!and's No. 1. v. Whiteeross No. 1. Referee, T. klakin,assistant %V. Rol ford.</p>
        <p>In the matches on Saturday, Ryland's No. 1. regis- tered the most striking victory, winning every game but three acainst Whitecross No. 2, :Ltd scoring 171 Points to 135. The Whitecross men made a fair show individually, the lowest return heing W. Ball's 8, against A. Stringer's 15.</p>
        <p>Pearson and Knowles triumphed over Houghton's No. 1, by 13 points-154 to 141. Seven games were won by the leaders agairst five, but there was some elate bowling. A. Rostern, the P. and K. captain gave his side a good start by winning 15-6. There was a 6 and a 7 cn his side later on, but Houghtcn's went one worse with two six's and a seven.</p>
        <p>There were only two single figure returns in the match between L. and N.-W. and Monks Ball No. 1, the first-named winning by 10 to 145 and seven games to five. W. Hatton for the losers only got four chalks against J. Hutchinson, while .7. Lytligoe just missed double figures with 8 against J. Rowlands.</p>
        <p>Naturally with over 100 entries. great interest has been shown in the competition at the Liberal Club for the Crostleld Challenge 'Cup. It is expected that the third round will be concluded to-day. Given fine weather there is sure to be some good bowling at the Liberal Club. _ _</p>
        <p>Rugby Football. The following clubs have entered the Warrington Junior Rugby League, viz.:—vairtleld, St. Elphin's, St. MaWs St. John's, Rvlauds Wet Mill, Greenhalgh's, Fletcher Russells, Park anniors. Further entries may be sent in to Mr. B. C. Essenbigh, 204, Manchester road, up to and including Wednesday next, August sth. A meeting of the League will be held on the Football field on Wednesday evening next.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>WARRINGTON</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON</p>
        <p>Musical _Success. At the recent examination held by the Victoria College of Music (Manchester centre), Miss Doris E. Read, Grappenhall, passed with honours the Assaciate examination, which entitles her to append the letters A.V.C.M. to her name. A Plucky Young Warringtonian. News has just come to hand that Mr. W. Pierpo'nt, koungest son of Alderman Pierpoint, J.P., the other day saved the life of a small boy who had fallen into the river near Oberlichtenau, Germany. Mr. Pier- point, junr., is on the Continent studying the hide trade, and while fishing he saw the youngster fall into the water, and at once went to the rescue. Temporary Authority. At the Borough Police Court, on Monday, Mr. C. H. Unsworth applied on behilf of Mr. James Lodge for temporary authority to sell at the Hop Pole Hotel, Hoesemarket-street, in lien of Mrs. Looker. —Superin- tendent Monaghan stated that the police had no object*. Mr. Lodge had been at the General Elliott Croft, for eight years.—The application was granted. For Sale, cheap, 28inch GREEN'S PraNy Laws MOWER, good condition, suit cricket club.— GARLAND, 51, Bridge at., Warrington.—Advt. Another Success of Crosfield's Band. The fourth annual horticultural show and brass band contest promoted by Rowntree's Cocoa Works took place in the York Exhibition Buildings on Satur- day. The test work was one of Rossini's, and Mr. J. 0. Shepherd, of Liverpool, was the judge. The award was as follows :—Black Dike, first prize (125) ; Goorishaw, second (145) ; Shaw, third (£10) •, Perfection Soap Works, fourth (£5) ; King Cross and Norlard, fifth and sixth prizes divided (£2 ss. each.) Golborne-street Baptist Church. Special flower services were held in this church on Sunday, when the preacher, morning and evening, was the Rev. R. E. Letheren (pastor). In the morning he spoke upon "The order of Onesiphorus " and in the evening he delivered an address upon " God's Garden." A service was held in the afternoon, when special hymns were sung by the scholars. Mr. C., Dowson was the conductor and Miss Hankey the pianist. Mr. S. Rickers presided at the organ. PUBLIC: BENEFIT.—IINDEK THE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.—Advt. Matriculation Examination Results. The following names of local students appeal,. in the pass list for the July matriculation examination of the Universities of Manchester, Liverpool, Leeds, and Sheffield : —Ems' DIVISION: Percy F. Boardman, Warrirgton P.C.T. ; Albert E. Eden, Runcorn Institute ; Harry Walter Lea, Widnes Secondary School'; Florence Wailing, Widnes Secondary School. SECOND DIVISION: Mary Louisa Boardman and Hector Ireland, Widnes Secondary School; Frederick Jolley and Marian Jolley, Warrington Sc iondary School ;.,Qecil J. Robinson, Widnes Secondary School Step-Mother and Step-Daughter. At the Police Court on Tuesday,Luby Holt, 17,M iller street, pleaded guilty to assaulting her step-daughter, Edith Holt, the previous afternoon.—Prosecutrix stated that she was RI the street with her father' when prisoner.struck her in.the face and knocked her down. She said she "would give her more before she had finished with her." —Harry Holt, prisoner's husband, said his wife committed the assault in a temper. There had been an ill-feeling between his daughter and his wife for some time.— Prisoner was bound over in the sum of £lO to kcep the peace for six months and ordered to pay the costs (14s. 6d.) LAWN TENNIS, CROQUET AND GARDEN. REQUISITES.—J. S. GARLAND, Ironmonger, Bridge- street, Warrington.—Advt. Wycliffe P.S.A. An open meeting in connection with the Wycliffe P.S A. was held on Sunday. In the unavoidable labsence of the President', Mr. Farrar presided over a good meeting. Mr. W. Beecher Smith, of the T.M.C.A.. Manchester gave a good address upon "'Words of good and great men." Miss Marie Houghton afforded a perfect treat to the meeting by her singing with great taste and feeling, "Far from my heavenly home" and with "verdure clad." Mr. W. H. P.ayton presided at the organ. Mr. W. Beswick alsi took part in the meeting. The chairman expressed the pleasure of having the speaktr !Lad Miss Marie Houghton with them on that occasioa" Warrington Man Drowned. William Burgess (29), of Fairclough's-avenue, Warrington, was drowned in the Mersey at Howley, Warrington, on Saturday night. He was seen by a man named Woods sitting on a boat stage dadgling his feet in the water. Only partially divesting him- self of his clothing, he entered the water and swam for a considerable distance. He appeared to be in difficulties,and an alarm was raised,but before he could be assisted he disappeared. The body was afterwards recovered by George Platt, a pleasure boat owner. An inquest was held on Monday by Mr. Brighouse on the body, and the jury returned a verdict of "Acci- dentally drowned.' BABY CARRIAGES Or every description.—J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55,Bridge st.,Warrington.—Advt. Heavy Penalty for Betting. At the Warrington Police Court On Monday morning, Annie Newton, 16, Friars' Gate, and William-Singleton, porter, 32, Leonard-street, Stockton Heath, 'were charged with loitering in Barbauld-street for the purpose of betting. Mr. T. S. Steel defended Mrs. Newton and pleadeA guilty.—lt whs stated by Superintendent Monaghan that about 1.30 on Saturday, Detective Woollacott was 'passing Friars' Gata when, looking up Jolley's-court, he saw the man Singleton engaged in a betting transaction on behalf of the female defendant. I his possession were found 60 betting slips and £l4 18s., which were balanced up and it was found that she had drawn £9 6s. 93. for that day's event. She admitted to the officer that it was a clean catch. —Mr. Steel said it was her first offence. —Superiatendent Monaghan said that there were previous convictions against Mrs. Newton.— Defendants were fined £lO 63. 61. each. £l4 14s Good second-hand piano.—Woon, London rd., Stockton Heath. Tunings and repairs.---Advt. Assurance Agents' Pic-nic.</p>
        <p>The annual Pic-nic to Southport in connection with the staff of the Wesleyan and General Assurance Society in Warrington,took place on Thursday,July 23rd. The company departed from the Central Station by saloon carriage a little after 930; en route a pleasing event took place. Mr. Abner Jones, on behalf of the staff, presented a pipe in case, and a tin of tobacco to the Superintendent, Mr. A. Shepherd, in appreciation of the time and forethought spent in organizing the out- ing. The party arrived at their destination at 9.35. A char-a-bane was in readiness to convey them for an hour's drive through the town, promenade, Marine Drive. At the conclusion of this, the party was photographed, then a sail on the Marine Lake preceded an excellent dinner in the best style at Rowntree's Cafe. The programme was proceeded with by a drive to Churchtown Botanical Gardens. A couple of hours was spent there, the party being shown everything of_ interest by the curator of the gardens. The return journey to Southport was _taken at 4.15, and a first-class tea served at the same cafe at 5 o'clock. After tea the party followed their own desires until the 0.5 train, arriving at Warrington at 10 o'clock, all voting the outing to be the best ever organized. PUBLIC BENEFIT UNDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LLNCASHIRE.—Advt. Shepherds' Quarterly Meeting. The Quarterly Meeting in connection with loyal order of Ancient Shepherds (AU.) Warrington District, was held on Saturday at the Morning Star Lodge, "Big Horse Shoe," Warrington, under the presidency of Bro. P. L Edgar, District Chief. He was supported by the Deputy Chief (Bro. J. Gilhooley) Past Chiefs Thomas Baskerville, jun., L. Weir, T. Miller, J. Gresty, J. Mather, Dis. Sec. Bro. Peter Edgar and Delegates from the various Lodges forming the District.—The District Secretary presented the Quarterly Accounts, which were of a satisfactory character, and the same having been certified as correct by the Auditors (Bros. Gresty and Miles were confirmed by the meeting. Bro. J. Gilhooley was unanimously elected Chief Shepherd of the District for the ensuing twelve months and the honour of Deputy Chief was bestowed on Bro. W. Miles by a majority of two votes over his opponent Bro. W. Clegge.l Bro. Thomas Miller was appointed DistrioWitorl for the following six months. The represk :live of the District (Bro. T. Baskerkille, pin.), gave his report of the proceedings of the A.M.C. held at Wrexham during Whit-week. Tt contained many notable passages touching on Shepherdry, and altogether proved very instructive and interesting. Bro. Baskerville was accorded a hearty vote of thanks to which he suitably replied. —After the installation of the new District Officers, the meeting terminated, with a hearty vote of thanks being accorded to the retiiing Chief, Bit P. L. Edgar, for his services awing the past twelve mouths›</p>
        <p>Mv DELE CHILDREN,— Since writing you last week, I have been away from the office for a few days, and have been to the sea. I wonder if any of you know a place called Sheringham, on the East Coast— a most delightful little place, with high hillei sand cliffs, where the breeze is keen and fresh; so very different to the close air of London and other large towns. City life is all very well, but nothing compared to country life, with all its pleasures. I wonder when your holidays commence. I have had one or two more attempts for the as. prize for the best Essay on "The Ideal Holiday." I hope you will send me a lot more. So far, my inenalocrs seem to prefer .spending their holiday out of England. Well, there is nothing like seeing other countries. I remember the first time I went to France I was most pleased with all I saw—the people, scenery, and every- thing seemed so different to England; but ,do you know I felt quite glad to get back to the old country again. I hadn't tasted a good cup of tea since I left—only a curious mixture of herbs, not like the drink we get here. But, still, I hope all my members, if they get a chance, will go and see other countries, Don't forget the Xssay.—With love, UNCLE NED.</p>
