      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>AN IDEAL HOLIDAY.</head>
        <p>AN IDEAL HOLIDAY.</p>
        <p>I think one of the nicest kind of holidays Would be "Camping out on one of the thou- sand islands in the river St. Lawrence, in Canada."</p>
        <p>To sleep in a tent and have all our meals out of doors, under a shady tree, to picnic on the other islands, and amuse ourselves with fishing, shooting, bathing, and boating. If it were wet, to sit rotlnd the camp fire and tell stories, read, or play games.</p>
        <p>GLADYS WHITTON.</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle Ned,—l hope you are quite well, as I am at present. I must tell you L have received the beautiful box of chocolates. You must please forgive me for not writing before, but mother has been so ill. I am pleased to say she is much better now. fthank you very much for the- chocolates, and hope you will not think I have forgotten you. I wrote out An essay on the 7th, but have not found time to send it until to-day, the 16th of July. It is, "How and where, I should lire to spend 'a month.r You will see I choose %Prance, and you do also. Please slnl my sister a.mirror. I Del:lain, your loving Niece, FloarNms E. Dane.</p>
        <p>_+___</p>
        <p>HOW AND WHERE I SHOULD LIKE TO</p>
        <p>SPEND A MONTH.</p>
        <p>I should at first visit Switzerland, as,Sivitzer-. land is nearly covered by the Alps. The scenery is magnificent, and attracts large num- bers of tourfets 'every year. ,The Swiss are an intelligent and well-educated People, so I think it would be: a charming place •to visit Of course, the Swiss are noted for wood-carving. and their 'clocks, watches, toys, and jewellery, and 1 should be pleased to See eamPles Of these. It would be exceedhigly interesting tc see the Swiss silk weaving. 6otton and silk weaving, are cairied On at Zurich and Basle. Switzerland w.m1.1 be a fine place Ice delicate people; Swii-zerland .! is the 'sanatorium of Europe. Cattle,' sheep, and gcats .are reared on the mountains. From the ggat excellent • milk is obtained, with which dellcate "people are fed. I should lie-to po with. a guide to the Alps. To do what I•should Elle to in Switzerland would' take me abmt a fortnght. For the 'employ- ment of the second fortnight .1. should like to visit Paris. Pars is the capital of France, and stands on the rive: Scinet It is strongly forti.</p>
        <p>- „ • tied, and possisses' many handsome building's. noble squa:es, and beautiful gardens. I should visit these gardens and squares, and no doubt thoroughly enjoy, myself. I should, after seeing all these places, return to my home. •</p>
        <p>'ELORENdE ELLEN DARE.</p>
        <p>ME" ORNAMENTS OF "LADItS " ON THE . , • • GOLD COAST.</p>
        <p>The women on the Gold Coast wear garters of glass beads just below the knee, but they do not wear stockings.-; The garters are not always wrought out of such precious materiak is glass beads, but are often composed of twine. These garters, whether of beads or of twine, are -generally worn by unmarried girls, and are marks of virginity. Married women like, wise wear them. The women wear anklets too. The anklets are frequently manufactured out of copper found in Ashantee. Rings of silver, and even of gold, are alsq worn round the toe nearest to the big tve. Rarely is any other toe treated with the same respectful attentiOn. Glass beads are of value among the ladies of the Gold Coast; and it is said,that glass beads which may be bought in England for 9d. will fete.' as many shillings in the African market. The women of Cape Coast have their hair bruched or combed info a cone of six or eight inches In height, and they do not like it to be touched. A stranger cannot discompose a girl more than by interfering with her hair. Some- times, instead of being raised like a cone, the hair is twisted into spike-like projections, -so that a woman's head has the appearance of a DO lb. shot fretted with nails.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <p>.......i....-</p>
        <p>When Madame Albani was at the Norwich Musical Festival, she received a bill from a tradesman with whom she dealt, addressed, "Mrs. L. Barney." Mistakes in artistes' names are, however, by no means uncommon. The veteran conductor Arditi—the story is not in his Reminiscences—once received a letter addressed ",Signor IL Ditty."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <p>.......+....</p>
        <p>"What are 'you doing here?" said the woman to the tramp, who had got over the wall just in time to escape the bulldog, who evidently wanted a bite. , "Madam;" he said, with dignity, "I did in tend to request something to eat, but all I ask now is that, in the interests of humanity, 'you'll feed that dog."</p>
        <p>In l- &amp; 2/6 Tins; also 4d. Packets.</p>
