      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>ZAGLE AND THE FOX.</head>
        <p>THE ZAGLE AND THE FOX.</p>
        <p>' An eagle and a fox had long lived together. One day, while the fox was 'abroad, the eagle made a swoop at the fox's cub, and carried it off to her nest, thinking that the tree was so 'high it would secure her from the fox's revenge. The fox, on her return home, begged to have her young one again; but, finding that her entreaties were of no avail, she snatched a torch from an altar, and, setting the whole tree in flames, soon made the eagle restore, through fear for herself and her own young ones, the cub which she had just now denied to her most earnest prayers. NORA L. MOORING. SETTLING THE SIZE.</p>
        <p>She walked into a fashionable shoe-shop and said to the polite assistant:— "You may show me a pak of walking bootq, No. 4. I used to wear 3'e, bill I go in for solid comfort now." _ _</p>
        <p>The man tried the boots, but they would not goon. . . .</p>
        <p>"Strange," she murmured, " it must be rheumatism. Try s's—l know I can swim in them, but my feet are so tender." While the shopman was getting theer-on sha said:—</p>
        <p>" I used to have a beautiful foot, not small, but such a good shape. I never had a small foot, but I wore 24 sizes for years, until I walked so much and grew heavier." " Your foot is a peculiar shaps, the instep is so high—that is why you require a large size," said the man, who had no fear of Ananias before his eyes. " I've heard," she said, " that the Venus dee Medeechy wears No. 5, and she is a mcdel cf true proportion." " Exactly," said the obliging young fellow, growing red in the face as he pulled and tugged to get them on. He had never heard of " dee Medeechy," but he was up to a trick or two himself. "After all," he said, "these are to large. You'll And the 4's just right." He was onlyotone a moment, but in that time he had erased 6 from the inside of a pair of shoes and substituted 4.</p>
        <p>_ diought it was strange," the said, when they were on and paid for; "why: thew, are quite as easy ag my old ones. I believe I could just as well have had,3's after all." . .</p>
        <p>And the young man without a conscience went back to his duties with the air of one ,well satiiiied with himself.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>---4- • THE MAN OF THg HOUSE.</head>
        <p>---4- • THE MAN OF THg HOUSE.</p>
        <p>Vagabonds who undertake to find out whether the "man of the house" is in or not will get little advantage if a " strong-minded " woman happens to live there. :One .who tried it at "Mrs. Brown's " the other day, will probably put up a ,danger-signal there for the benefit of tho rest of his fraternity. The tramp ascended the front steps,, and tter taking the precau- tion to read the floor-plate, rang the bell with rree and easy confidence born of previous sue- .2ess. The door was opened by a woman, as the tramp had anticipated. "Is Mr. Browu in?" he _diced. The woman took an inventory of his face and clothing, and replied, "No, Mr. Brown isn't in, but Mrs. Brown is. I've just sent Brown for ozne- yeast ; when he comes back he'll have to out out the line and hand out the wash; then seri% him out with the baby, and after that have his sweeping and dusting to do. No, you can't tee Brown to-day; and it wouldn't :hake any difference if you could: I attend to liusiness here." The tramp said he guessed he'd mistaken the house, and Mrs. Brown, as she slammed the door, said she.rockoned he had.</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>1 t</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>IIICIIAEL FARADAY.</head>
        <p>IIICIIAEL FARADAY.</p>
        <p>Among modern kicntists, Michael. Faraday, who died in 1337, stood in some respects almost none. _He ,wasi an eminently religious man. Usually, in this generation", science and religion have not gone hand-in-hand. Religion often opposes science. Science almost a'.ways has A ialf-imperceptible 'sneer for religion. But Aichael Faraday was a devout and humble he- tiever. He came into the world the son of a alacksmith' whose highest ambition Was to flake his boy a boOkbitider. At flip age of thir- ' teen Michael Faraday entered upon lifs appren- qceship. But he was not satisfied to deal with :he outside of books alone. At odd moments 'ae possessed himself of their contents. In this way he dipped into some scientific treatiAes, ind so his future career • was determined. Through a customer of his master's lie obtained in opportunity to hear some of Sir Humphrey Davy's lectures; and he afterwards wrote to Sir Humphrey, asking him to help him by some rhesus to lemie trade and enter the service of science. Through Sir Humphrey he obtainTl the post of assistant in the laboratory in 9.lbe- marle-street, and thus commenced his scientific career. How eminently successful he was, what honourable distinction he won, the world knows well. It should know, also, that he never lost his simplicity and sincerity of char- meter. The main characteristic of his life"' was his utter self-forgetfulness—his -devotion to truth for truth's sake. The pursuit of science was to him its own reward; and he saw such infinite and unexplored vistas of knowledge stretching out before him as filled him with profound humility. There was no affectation in the regrets he so frequently expressed in his last days that he had done so little for science.</p>
        <p>"Ladies and gentlemen," said the profes- sional hypnotist, as he introduced Wayside Rhodes to the assemblage, "I will now lend the subject here before you five pounds." "That's no _trick," said a_ voice.</p>
        <p>"And then," said the professor, in firm tones of assured triumph, "I will make him return the five pounds to me."</p>
