      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>POSTAL AND SAVNGS BANK REGULATIONS</head>
        <p>POSTAL AND SAVNGS BANK REGULATIONS</p>
        <p>For further particulars see " Post (Moe Guide," at any Post 03ce. • POSTAGE STAMPS._ _ _</p>
        <p>Books containing 12 penny and 23 halfpenny stamps are.ntw on sale at each post offi3e. Price 2s.</p>
        <p>INLAND CORRESPONDENCE.</p>
        <p>The term " Inland Correspondence" means nor - respondence pasted within the United Kingdom, including the Isle of Man and the Channel Islands. " Inland Correll:Lot:deuce " is divided into the following classes : (a) Letters - (d) Newspapers (b) Postcards (e) Parcels' (c) Halfpenny packets Not only letters, but any document or article (with a few trifling exceptions) whicli can be trans- mitted by post at all, may be sent by letter post, the only limitation. being that of size.</p>
        <p>LETTER POST. The prepaid rate of postage is as follows Not exceeding 4 ozs. in weight ... Id. Fur every additional 2 0z5.... fd. A letter posted unpaid is chargeable on delivery with double postage; a letter lasuLliciently paid, with double the deflciency. No letter may exceed two feet ia lensth, one foot LS width, or one foot in depth.</p>
        <p>POST-CADS. The prepaid rate of postage on every post-card is one half-penny, and on every reply post-card one penny. A post-card posted unpaid is chargeable on delivery with one penny. Cards used as post-cards must be made of ordinary card-board, not thicker than the material used for the thickest official post-card. They may not exceed 51 inches in length by 31 inches in width, or be less than 3± inches in length by 21 inches in width. A gummed label (not to exceed 2 inches long and 2-inch wide) bearing the address may now be affixed- to the address side of card. If any of the rules relating to a post-card be infringed, the card will he treated as a letter. HALFPENNY PACKET POST. _</p>
        <p>The Ifalfp-e-n—n-iNCket -P;;;C-c-an-1;e-Used only for packets not exceeding 2oz. in weight. Printed matter, &amp;c., not in the nature of a letter, may be transmitted by this method. NEWSPAPER POST.</p>
        <p>The prepaid 'tate of postage on a registered news- paper. including any supplement thereto, whether posted singly or with others is J. Newspapers not registered at the General Post (Moo are treated in the pcst like any other printed matter. , _</p>
        <p>PARCEL POST. The rates of postage on parcels areas follows: For a parcel ... not exceeding I lb. 0 3 ft exceeding 1 lb. If 2 lb. 0 4 If 9 lb PI 3.1 3 lb. 0 5 ft t 3 lb. ft 5 lb. 0 6 ), „ 5 lb. tf 7 lb. 0 7 ft ft 7 lb. ff 8 lb. 0 8 •• „ 8 lb. ft 9 lb. 0 9 11 f 1 9 lb. 'll 10 lb. 0 10 „ 10 lb. „ 11 lb. 011 No parcel may exceed 11 lb. in weight. The size allowed for an Inland parcel is:— Greatest length... ...• ... 3 ft. 6 in. Greatest length and girth,combined 6 ft. 0 in. For exa nple—A parcel measuring 3ft. Gin. jn its longest dimensions as much as 21 t. Gin. roiind its thickest part ; a short parcel may be thicker; thus, if it measure no more than 3ft. in length, it may measure as much as 31t. round its thickest part. The postage on a parcel must bs prepaicl. Mark each parcel Parcel Post." A parcel must not be posted in a letter box, but presented at the counter of a post office, and the sender should see that the weight, size and postage are in order before leaving. PARCELS (FOREIGN AND COLONIAL). Parcels are accepted for parcel post to most foreign countries under conditions similar to the inland regulations, except that for customs require- ments the contents must be declared. The customs charges can be prepaid or collected on delivery. The postage .varies to different countries and must be prepaid. MONEY ORDMIS (INLAND.) The postal authorities cannqt issues single money order for more than £4O, and will only cash them at the money order office at which they are made payable, unless they are crossed, when they can be paid through a bank. No fractional part of a Id. can be contnined In s money order. The rates of poundage are :—Not exceeding El, 2d.: £3, 3d.; £lO, 4d.; £2O, 63.; £33, Sd. ; £4O, 10d. By paying an additional 2d. aud cost of the telegram of advice, a money order can be telegraphed under certain restrictions. MONEY ORDERS (FOREIGN AND COLONIAL.) The maximum Amount fey which a money order may be issued for places abroad varies in each particular place. The poundage rates are :—Not exceeding £1,33. ; £2, Gd. ; and above that, 3d. for each additional £2 or portion of it. A fee of 6d. in addition to the ordinary poundage, together with cost of telegram of advice, enables money orders to be telegraphed to certain places abroad. POSTAL ORDERS. - -</p>
        <p>These are obtainable at any money order office in the United Kingdom, and possess the advantage over money orders that they can be oashe at any money ordor office. If crossed, they can D paid into a bank. They are issued for fixed am bunts, rising by Rd., from Gd. to .£1 Is. The poundage fees are :--id. up to 2s. GI ; Id. up to 155.; l id. up to 21s. Before parting with a postal order, always fill in the name of the person to whom the amount is to be paid. Each order is numbered, as is also the counterfoil attached, which latter should be retained and name of payee filled in. The Post- maater-General will entertain no further claim after an order is once paid. Stamps to the amotint of sd. can be affixed to makeup odd sums.</p>
        <p>TELEGRAMS (lAAND). The Postmaster-General possesses,almost entirely, the privilege of transmitting,collecting and deliver- ing telegrams in the United Kingdom. The charge for a telegram is a minimum amount of Gd. which covers 12 words, and an additional id. for each extra word. This charge includes delivery within the postal district if sent out for delivery from a head telegraph office, but if from a sub-office then within three miles only, beyond which a charge of 3d. per mile is made from the office door. All charges are, as far as possible, prepared by affixing stamps to telegram form. Address of person receiv- ing a telegram has to be paid for, but sender's name if written on back of form need not be. Five figures count as one word. The cost of a reply may be prepaid. Telegrams can be repeated on payment of half the original charge, which extra payment is refunded if error is detected. Telegrams may be posted in wall or pillar boxes, or handed to rural postmen, and are wired as soon as received at the nearest telegraph office. Late fees secure attention, in certain cases, to telegrams handed in after the usual hours. The cost of registering an abbrevi- ated address, which must consist of two words, is £1</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>TELEGRAMS (FOREIGN AND COLONIAL)</head>
        <p>TELEGRAMS (FOREIGN AND COLONIAL)</p>
        <p>Must be written onspecial forms and are accepted In the linguage of any of the principal European countries. The rates vary, and are too numerous to detail here. POST OFFICE SAVINGS BAsx.</p>
        <p>These are established at all money order offices. rn ordinary cases the annual deposit must not exceed £5O, and the total amount £2OO. No person can have more than one account. Amounts placed in the bank on behalf of children under seven years of age cannot be withdrawn until the child reaches that age, and then only under his or her own signa- ture. The smallest amount received at a time is Is., and this may take the form of 12 penny stamps affixed to a special form. Each depositor receives a bank book in which the amount of each deposit should be entered at the time of paying in. If the deposit is £1 or over, a special acknowledgment is sent from the bead London °lice. Interest is allowed at the rate of 2i per cent. Any amount up to and including £1 may be withdrawn without previous notice, on presenting a bank book. The bank book should be. sent each year, postage free, on the anniversary c f the date when the account was opened, to the Controller of the Savings Bank Department for purposes of checking and adding interest. The withdrawal of money can be expedited by telegraphing, the depositor paying cost of teleararas. P.O. TELEPITONN AND TRUNK LINA: CHARGES.</p>
        <p>The fees for the use of the Trunk lines are as fol!orcs _ _ .</p>
        <p>For 25 miles or under 3d. )9 50 Gd. P. 75 II „ 100 , „ every additional 40 miles or fraction _ thereof... ... 6d.</p>
        <p>Between the hours of 7 p.m. and 7 a.m., six minutes conversstlon is allowed for the same price as three minutes between 7 a.m. and 7 p.m., and a reduced charge is made for three minute calls.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>MOTOR CAR AND CYCLE REG u AT TONS .</head>
        <p>MOTOR CAR AND CYCLE REG u AT TONS .</p>
        <p>... Each one must be registered with the council of a county or county borough, the fee being £1 for a motor car, and ss. for a motor cycle. A registered number is assigned to each, which it, is an offence not to keep affixed when driving. Each driver must possess a license, costing 55., to be renewed every 12 months, and obtainable from the county authorities, which may be endorsed or suspended for reckless driving or certain other offentes. The highest speed at which a car may be driven on the public highways is 20 miles an hour, which is reduced to 10 miles under special local regula- tions. It is an offence under the Motor Car Act (1003), to drive a car in such a way on a pnblic highway as to be dargerous to the public, having regard to all the circumstances of the case. .</p>
        <p>In the event of an accident to any person due to tbn presence of the motor car on the road, the driver must supply his name and address, and the owner and number of the car, under heavy penal- ties. The driver must produce his licence at the request of a police constable.</p>
      </div>
