      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>WARRINGTON AGRICULTURAL SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON AGRICULTURAL SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>The Coming Show.</p>
        <p>I The nineteenth annual show of the Warrington and District Agricultural Society will be held in Walton Park, on Thursday, 13th August. Prizes, cups, &amp;c.; are offered to the value of about £7OO. Sir Gilbert Greenall Bart, is again the President. There are classes for shires, hunters, hackneys, harness horses, jumpers, tradesmen's or tenant farmers' turnouts, cattle' pigs, cheese and butter. Entry forms and all particulars may be obtained from Mr. H. Beresford Melville, Bold-street, Warrington. Entries close this Saturday, August Ist. Sir Gilbert Greenall offers three cups for competition, Mr. F. Stapleton Bretherton a special prize value-- £5, tte Executors of the late Col. ft. Pilkington, Mr. Joseph Palmer, Lymm, Mr. John Coxon and Mr. A. B. Boumphrey, Lymm, silver cups, Mr. J. Whittaker Kenworthy, Mr. James Gould, the Warrington Shire Horse Society, the Cheshire Hunt Covert Fund, Messrs. Henry Milling and Co., Messrs. Day and Sons, and Messrs. Barmak' Limited, special prizes. Medals are also offered by the Shire Horse Society, the Hunter* Improvement Society, and the Hackney Horse Society. Sir Gilbert Greenall gives premiums of £3 in some of the tenant farmer classes. It should be noted that the Society's district on the Cheshire side of Warrington now extends to 16 ranee from Market Gate, Warrington, and in the various directions goes as far as the Budworths, Kelsall, Wharton and Over, Ashton, Dunham, Thornton, Stoke, Barrow, Plinstone, Tarvin, ll iddlevrich, Peover, Wilmslow, Cranage, Eaton, Mobberley, 8:0., and, of course, includes the whole of the\district nearer to Warrington. That part of Cheshire lying east of the railway from Broadhurst to Manchester, and north •of the railway from Broadheath to Stockport is excluded. The Society's radius on the Lancashire side is 12 miles from Market Gate, Warrington. In connection with the show will be held the third Walton Flower Show and cottage garden prize com- petition. This is a most attractive feature of the show, in which Lady Greenall is particularly interested. For two or three years the Band of the Yorkshire Hussars has playgirl a selection of music at the Show. I There will be a change this year, the famous Karoly Klay Blue Hungarian Band having been engaged. Visitors to the show may look forward with confidence to a rich musical treat even if not keenly interested in , the show itself.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>AUGUST BANK BOLIDAY.</head>
        <p>AUGUST BANK BOLIDAY.</p>
        <p>The redcoatle Line Advertisements show that their fast S.S. " Blackrock "leaves South Side Nelson Dock on Friday next at 11 p.m. returning from Dublin 5 p.m. on Monday, 3rd August, which affords a capital oppor- tunity, for visiting this interesting city, and also for running out to Howth Hill, also to Bray to see the beautiful scenery on the river Dargle. The Return Saloon fare is exceedingly moderate, being only 105. The same company announce their S.S. " Cumbria" as leaving Colling!vood Dock, midnight, Saturday, Ist August, for their special round trip to Whitehaven, Maryport, and back to Whitehaven, and thence to Dublin on the Wednesday, giving nearly two days there and returning 6 p.m. on Friday. The Round Trip costs' 205., and as it includes sleeping on board for six nights is exceedingly moderate. Good meals can be purchased on board at reasonable prices.</p>
      </div>
