      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR.</head>
        <p>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR.</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS. Authors of letters intended for publication must in all cases send their names and addresses to the Editor as a guarantee of good faith. Tariff Reform. Sir,—A complaint which I think can be justly made against Tariff Reformers is that they persist in only seeing and presentir g one aspect of the question. They individualize a particular trade, point to the competi- tion from abroad, propose to remove it by a tariff to enable the home industry to develope, employ more labour, and make more profit. They argue from these , narrow premises that a protective policy would be a national gain. Surely never was so vital a conclusion based on such insufficient reasoning. Many important considerations are ignored. The fact is overlooked that whatever increased price is got for the protected home made article is at the expense of other home trades and individuals, a mere transference from one section to another, but the loss is by no means made up by the gain. The advantage gained by the one protected trade would have to be balanced by an equivalent pro- tection for all other occupations, but inasmuch as a great majority of employments from their character do not admit of protection it is clear that these will at once be placed at a disadvantsee with workets in pro- tected trades. Some Tariff Reformers admit that I economically tariffs are an evil and involve national loss, but look upon them as a form of taxation that is not readily recognized by those who pay, and a conveni- ent method of meeting the calls upon the Excheqner. I Let us examine what the proposed taxes would bring I in It is somewhat difficult for us to get definite data , upon which to base calculations because the plans suggested by the various groups of Tariff Reformers I are so varied and often contradictory. Possibly I the newly-formed Warrington branch will in; time tell us what they stand for. Perhaps it is best for the time being to look to the official leader of the Tariff Party for guidance as to what form the party desire Tariff Reform to assume. Mr. Balfour's speech in Birmingham is frequently referred to as the policy of the Conservative Party. -"I am a Tariff Reformer in the sense • Mr. Balfour explained at Birmingham" is a usual formula of candidates when steked to define their fiscal faith. In this speech four principles were laid down which should govern any practicable measure of fiscal reform. The first was that the duties should be widespread ; second, they should be small; third, they should not touch raw material; fourth, they should not alter the proportion in which the working classes con- tribute to the cost of government. Are these the principles or conditions adopted by the Warrington Tariff Reformers ? There is a significant omission, no mention is made of food taxes, and every effort to get Mr. Balfenr to say whether his plan includes food tax has so far been in vain. Upon this question there seems to be a some- what unworthy reticence. Assuming that food taxes are barred as well as those on raw material, we have left only manufactured and semi-manufsctured goods, these amount together to one hundred and fifty millions, but this amount includes many articles which we look upon as raw material such as manures, copper, led, pig iron, peddled bars, blooms, billets, raw chemicals, tanning barks, rough marble, itc. Assuming that there was an average tax of five per cent., which we suppose I would conform with Mr. Balfour's conception of a small duty, this would yield gross seven and a half millions. But the iatention of the imposition of the duty is to exclude a considerable portion of these' manufactured goods, if it does not do this then the one great object of its imposition from Tariff Reformers' point of view is useless. If as much comes in as before where is the supposed extra labour to come in? If we assume that the tax excludes one third of the goods we are now getting from abroad, the gross revenue from the balance would bring in a revenue of five millions. From this we would have to deduct the cost of collection. We have in these Islands one hundred and ninety-six ports, but only a few of these have a fully equipped custom staff, offices and warehouses. We should therefole have to erect custom house offices with a sufficient staff at every port, unless you are prepared to harass trade by , closing all the smaller ports as far as the import of dutiable articles is concerned. There is the further I question of the £91,000,000 of re-exported goods largely of a manufactured or semi-manufactured char- acter. How is this trade to be dealt with ? Are we to adopt the ridiculous plan of selling to foreigners l cheaper than to our own people as they do in Germany? Will all our ports have to be divided as in the case of I Hamburg and Antwerp, and goods for re-export landed , free at one section and dutiable goods for home con- ; sumption at another? Think for a moment what addi- tional cost this involves ? This re-export trade is largely the result of, and is dependent upon, the low costs, the result of tree trade. Burden this trade with additional costs and you at once endanger its continu- ance and growth. If in conformity with Mr. Balfour's conditions we have small duties widespread we should arrive at a condition similar to that an Germany and tax over two thousand articles. The chief difference between Germany and ourselves would he that our pro- portionate cost of collection would be greater because our duties would be small against their large ones. The cost for collecting a small duty being as great as for a large one. When Peel and Gladstone freed British trade it was clearly shown that very many of the taxes cost more to collect than they brought into the exchequer. Under Mr. Balfour's plan the result would be the same just as it is in the United States ancleGermany at the present time. To collect the five per cent revenue of floe millions the cost would be between three and four millions. It would take too much space to give full proof of this, bat if anyone is in doubt as to its correctness I would refer him to the cost of collecting similar taxes in Germany and America. The cost of collection in Germany costs per cent. about four times as much as ours does because we zestrict our taxes to few articles. The total netl amount received by the Exchequer as a result of this , reversal of national policy and dislocation of British commerce would not exceed two millions net, but this ' figure would ho further reduced if we allow Colonial manufactures to come in free. This then is the total result of the fiscal proposals as laid down by the leader of the party that favours what is called Tariff -Reform. To accomplish this we purpose to spend three millions in collection, three millions in non-productive labour, all the energy and capital spent in the erection of custom houses, bonded warehouses, free ports for re-export, all the labour of the permanent staff does not add one halfpenny to the national wealth. it is as much waste as if we threw goods to the value of three millions into the sea or burnt them by fire. We should upset our commercial treaties, surrender favoured nation clauses, harass, worry, and involve every importer in the country in additional expense, raise the price of all articles directly and indirectly affected by these tariffs, and thus dimir ish the spending power of every individual in the country. Never surely was a more absurd proposal put</p>
        <p>iorvvar4d. If in any case I have misinterpreted the proposals of Warrington Tar;El Reformers will Alderman Bennett or some other member put me right. CHARLES E. PARKER.</p>
        <p>Fenketh, July 29, MS. National Service. Germans and Admiral Fitzgerald. Sir,—A friend of mine sends me a small local German paper, " Hagener Zeitung " of ;..S3rd July, containing the report of Admiral Fitzgerald's speech at the National Service League meeting at Warrington. So his inciting words of German hatred ring throughout the country and his high position gives them weight and at least cannot help to make the feeling more peaceable. We can understand that soldiers look out for war and revel in the idea of an impending war ; war is their business and enhances their prospects of promo- tion. We can understand that the big land proprietors' cherish the idea of a future war, bece.use any war will do one thing certain : Enslave 'the masses more yet and increase the rental value of" their" land; but I cannot I understand, how thousands of people can listen to or read about such utter rubbish as that millions of Germans are longing for war with England ! A few hundred , narrow-minded officers in the German army may be I doing that for above-mentioned reasons, because the German officer is not a bit better or worse than his English companion ; but the German Emperor, even if he got the majority in the Reichstag (utterly impossible) would not start any aggressive war, for the simple reason that the overwhelming majority of the army consists of Socialists who are utterly against any aggressive war, nacl would refuse to march ! Admiral Fitzgerald says English trade and commerce are not sufficiently protected by the fleet. Well, tbe phenomen- ally quick increase in German trade and commerce has left the co,nparatirely slow increasing navy so behind that it is still far worse protected than the English trade and commerce ! But there is still another aspect to the question. " The Democratic Monthly " in an article on "Com- pulsory Military Service. The case against Conscrip- tion," 5a35 ‘" Mr. Drumont, the editor of the More parole 'and one of the leaders of the Nationalist party, in the course of a very remarkable speech, declared that" some of the very worst evils of which we com- plain in these days are directly traceable to the barracks. Compulsory military service, far from being a school of morals, is a school of drunkenness, of idleness and debauchery. It is depopulating our fields, and is over- crowding our cities. It has gone a long way towards ruining our peasantry, and to a large extent it has already debased them. No young man, however clean he may be on entering the barracks, can leave them without being more or less tainted. I deem the universal military service one of the greatest and it is sometimes one of the saddest, sacrifices that our country calls upon us to bear." In one of his newspaper articles Drumont wrote:— ' I know of few spectacles more saddening than that of the young peasant taken from his fields or village and thrown for three years into the barracks. Taken away from all that he has grown up amongst, from all that he most values, and condemned to live with Ilnen out of sympathy perhaps with him, and understanding nothing of his ideals and sentiments. All these sons of the soil, who would in time have married some honest woman and given to France a new race of strong and wholesome men, return to their homes all more or less diseased</p>
        <p>.</p>
