        <p>LOCAL INEDDINGS. PICTURESQUE CEREMONY AT NEWTON. Burrows—Airy. The handsome church of St. Peter, Newton-le- Willows, was the scene cf an interesting wedding on Tuesday, when Miss Constance Maria Airey, daughter of Mr. George Airey,, Sand&amp;ld Hall, Newton, was married to Mr. Francis Seacome Burrows, son of Mr. John S. Burrows, of Preston. The floral decorations in the church were arranged with artistic effect, alid the brilliant sunshine streaming through the windows of stained glass lent touches of vivid colmring that enhanced the exceptional attractiveness of the gathering. The walls of the chancel were outlined with red rambler roses and their foliage, and the choir stalls were banked with huge bunches of white hydrangea against a background of palms and green and white foliage. Beautiful white lilies were massed on the altar, with an occasional blossom of glowing crimson that intensified the pure whiteness of their</p>
        <p>petals. The service was fully choral, the musical portion being under the direction of M. Edwin Leyland, L.L.O M., who gave an organ recital during the arrival of the guests, which included " Serenade " (Lemaire), " Priere et Bernet's° " (Getilmont), " Cantilene Nuptiale " (Dubois), and " Adorenans " (Ravine), and after the service " The Bridal March " from " Lohengrin " (Wagner) and Mendelssohn's " Wedding March." The clergy and choir met the bridal party at the church door, and led the way to the chencel, singing "The Voice that breathed o'er Eden," and during the service a special anthem, composed by the bridegroom, was sung by Atherton Parish Church Choir. The ceremony concluded with the hymn "0, Perfect Love." The bells of St. Peter's rang out a merry peal at intArvals during the day. IThe officiating clergy were the Rev. F. 11. Burrows, 1 vicar of Christ Church, Ashton-under•Lyue, uncle of 1 the bridegroom, the Rev. A. W. Thomson, vicar of Salford, and the Rev. J. Ryder, vicar of the parish. I The bridegroom was accompanied by Mr. Walter I Brown as best man, and the groomsmen, who also ' acted as stewards, were Mr. Tom Airey, Mr. Eric I Burrows, Mr. Robin Burrows, Mr. Addison, and Mr. G askell. The bride, who was given away by her fathbr, was charmingly dressed in a princess° gown of ivory duchesse satin ; the front panel of the skirt bore a handsome design in seed pearl embroidery, and the corsage and bolero sleeves were also trimmed with , pearls. A long court train of silver tissue was caught , lat the shoulders with sprays of orange blossom, and I • was lined with thickly-ruffled frills of chiffon. A wide ' flounce of exquisite point do glaze lace was used to drape the train, which was also adorned with a huge 1 true-lovers' knot of satin ribbon, secured by a horse- shoe of myrtle and orange blossoms. The bridal of point de glaze lace was worn over a coronet of orange blossom, and the bride's bouquet was composed of whikalilies and roses. The veil and the lace drapery on the bride's dress were the gift of her mother. The bridesmaids in attendance were Miss Lettie Airey (sister of the bride), the Misses Kitty, Winnie and Bessie Burrows (sisters of the bridegroom), Miss Elena Kennard-Davis, and Miss Botsford. They wore delightful Empire frocks of rose-petal pink ninon, over a foundation of soft satin. The skirts were inserted with tinted blonde lace, and scarf-like drapery was arranged on the shoulders below the yokes of tucked cream net. Their becoming hats of pink satin were wreathed with hyacinths in the same shade, and were lined under the brim with black, and finished with loops and long streamers of pink satin ribbon. The bridegroom gave the amethyst and reridot brooches and bouquets of sweet peas. THE RECEPTION.</p>
        <p>Miss and Messrs. B. and F. Brown, silver tea caddy Mr. and Mrs. Dewharst, silver and ivory fish slicers Mr. Joseph Rarnsden, silver dish Mr. and Mrs. W. Harrison, case of silver salts,mu,stard pets, &amp;c. Mrs. Morris and Mrs. Thomas Morris, silver drsh Mr. and Mrs. Stephen and family,case of silver salts, mustard pot, &amp;c. Mr. and Mrs. Harvey, glass cucumber dish and spoon Ellen and Charles Harvey, pair of glass,vases Mrs. Charlton, cushion H Mr. and Mrs. artley, brass 'writing set Miss Ruth Ellis, china morning tea set Mr. and Mrs. Budworth, table cloth Mr., Mrs., and Misses Yates, pair of silver vases Mr. and Mrs. Charles Eckersley, oil painting by Robert Gallon Mr. J. D. Carr, large leather photograph frame Mr. C. H. and Miss Clark, silver cream jug Mr. H. D. Lowe, Royal Worcester and silver coffee set Mr.. Mrs., and the Misses Bubier, hammered silver howl</p>
