 and Mrs. Nuttall and family, afternoon tea cloth Messrs. Frank and Charlton Topp, ivory and silver crumb scoop Mr. and Mrs. Borron, sot of silver skewers Misses Andrew, silver inkstand Mr. and Mrs. Seger Owen, China rose bowl Mr, and Mrs. G. S. Brown, table centre Dr. Mrs. and the Misses Martin, nest of rosewood tables Mr. and Mrs. Arthur Smith, Crown Derby vase Mr. J. S. Gaskell, large bronze clock Messrs. Bullough, 400-day clock Mr. and Mrs. J. Nichols, three silver vases Employees of Messrs. Burrows, Ltd., silver tray Sterna then Conservatoire, Berlin, wreath of laurels Mr. and Mrs. Collingwood, afternoon tea cloth Mr. Kirkpatrick, revolving dish Rev. J. Ryder, carved figure</p>
        <p>After the ceremony a reception was held at Sand- field Hag, where the bride and bridegroom, standing under a huge bell of white and crimson flowers, in the drawing-room, received the hearty congratulations of their friends. Mrs. George Airey wore a handsome gown of black silk ninon over peacock green, trimmed with black silk hand embroidery. The bodice was arranged with cream lace and iridescent passamenterie, and her bonnet was brightened with white feathers land a touch gold. Mrs. John S. Burrows, mother of the bridegroom, was wearing an Empire gown of hand- painted cream chiffon, and a large white hat with long ostrich plumes; and Miss Gertie Burrows was in white satin and a big lace hat, with bunches of pink ribbon. Mrs. Cecil Watkins (sister of the bride, wearing silver grey chiffon embroidered with black and white beads, and a big blue hat) was accom- paniehy Miss Doris Watkins, a dainty little maid in white lace. Mrs. Willie Hughes (another sister) looked particularly attractive in palest blue and a big hat swathed with lace and clusters of roses; and her sister, Mrs. Douglas Smith, was in turquoise, finished with an Empire sash in tones of mauve, and a Leghorn hat with roses. Mrs. Whitelaw wore pastel cloth edged with black, and Mrs. William Eckersley was in peacock blue and a black hat. Miss Clegg wore wine-coloured poplin, and Mrs. Lund's white cloth gown was worn with a purple bat. Miss Ifesketh had a handsome lace scarf to brighten her gown of dark blue, and Miss Bower wore a charming frock of rose pink Oriental satin. Mrs. Cleg,g had a delicate dress of striped heliotrope and white, and Mrs. Miles Burrows wore black lace over ecru. Mrs. Sephton (Atherton), wearing black, was accompanied by two daughters, one in pink, with a white ruffle, and one in pale blue and a black hat, with white roses ; and Mrs. Sephton (Culcheth), in cream crepe de chine, with coloured embroidery, brought Miss Sephton, in pale blue and white taffeta, and Miss C. Sephton, in shrimp pink and a white hat. Mrs. Pendlebury wore a pale shade of primrose and a hat with brown feathers, and Miss Parry was very pretty in a white dress and a black bat with white osprey. Mrs. Collingwood's fawn gown was trimmed with silk braid, and her daughter was in white and a hat with pink roses. Mrs. Walter Eckersley was in white chiffon trimmed with white satin and a bat wreathed with pink roses. Mrs. Hartley was in a becoming dress of mole-coloured chiffon, and her daughter was in white net with touches of blue. Mrs. Davis wore a golden brown Directoire gown and a hat en suite, and Mrs. Lloyd was wearing a delicate shade of grey and a hat to match with sweet peas. Mrs. Thomson wore a costume.of -blue-grey and black hat with white feathers. An artistic figure was Miss rickersgill in painted net and a large hat with roses,and Miss Burn wore pale helitrope and a hat in a deeper shade.</p>
