      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, August 1, 1908.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, August 1, 1908.</p>
        <p>Mr. and Mrs. H. Marsh, carved ivory dagger Mr. Stott, pewter sugar basin Misses F. and D. Clegg, case of silver spoons Mr. W. H. Gaskell, Doulton vase Mr. and Mrs. Robson, volume of Shakespeare Mr. Addison, cut-class silver claret jug Mr. and Mrs. E. L. Vicars, pair of silver vases Mr and Mrs. Harrison Yates, silver-mounted flask</p>
        <p>Pleton —Grime. On Monday last at the pretty village church at Helsby a marriage was celebrated between Engineer-Lieut. Arthur L. Picton, R.N. of H.M.S. New Zealand (Channel Fleet) younger son of Mr. and Mrs. John Picton, of Westleigh,- Walton, near Warrington and Amy Maude, youngest daughter of Mr. Thomas Grime, of Clifton, Helsby and of Warrington. The Rev. E. Evans, vicar of the parish, officiated at the very quiet' ceremony and Mr. Yould the organist, presided at the organ. The bride, who lqoked most charming, was given away by her father. She was attired in A hand- some white cloth costume, richly trimmed with white silk braid and wore a white crinoline hat. She also carried an ivory backed Prayer Book, the gift of the bridegroom who appeared in fall dress uniform. The bride's travelling costume was of reseda green cloth, trimmed with black braid and a large black hat. The immediate relations were entertained after the service at Mr. Grime's residence, Clifton, Helsby, and the happy couple afterwards proceeded to Scarborough for the honeymoon. The following is a list of presents : Bridegroom to bride, ivory backed Prayar Book Bride to bridegroom, suit case • Mr. and Mrs. Picton and family, cheque . Mr. Grime, cheque Mr. and Mrs. Greening, Mr. jaak and Miss Elsie Greening, cheque Mr. and Mrs. James Dutton, cheque Ruth and Ella Dutton, silver tea caddy Mr. and Mrs. Bate, flower stand Mr. and Mrs. T. B. Garter, cake basket Mr, and Mrs. Meadows, jewel case Mr. and Mrs. Collinson, Crown Derby tea set Misses Bate, tea tray Mr. and Mrs. J. C. Bate, silver candlesticks Mr. and Mrs. Stanley Carter, silver salts and mustard Mr. and Mrs. James Carter, silver tea caddy Mr. and Mrs. Percy Carter, silver muffin dish Miss Carter, fancy work Mr. and Mrs. Walter °alma, sauce boat - Mr. Leonard Collinson, serviette rings Miss Mabel Collinson, fancy work Miss Any Meadows, bag Mr. Leonard Meadows, Thermos flask Mrs. Gordon Fraser, cake knife Mr. Lee, cheque Miss Bankes, cheque Mrs. Dutton, cheque Miss Ann Lancaster, gold wrist watch Engineer Officers H.M.S. New Zealand, !silver and Crown Derby Coffee Cups Mr: and Mrs. Rymer Young, tea basket Mr. and Mrs. Percy Silcock, cake knife Mrs. Elgood, Worcester coffee cups Mr. Walton, fish slice • Mr. Wood, fish knives and forks Miss Robinson, dessert knives and forks Mr. Clarke whist case Sarah (Milian), pearl and silver pin tray Martha (Westleigh), silver sugar basin Mr. and Mrs. Lin Greening, silver card case Miss Mary Greening, silver photo frame Mr. Lister, silver cigarette case Captain Cox, cigar lighter Mr. Buckley, salt and knife boxes Mr. and Mrs. Birks, crumb scoop Mr. and Mrs. Griffiths, silver clock Mrs. Harry Smith, bag Miss Sibyl Smith, fancy work Mr. and Mrs. Gabriel Davies, tea knives The Rev. E. Evans, silver flower vases The Rev: F. Clifton Smith, silver flower vases The Misses Tomlinson, fancy work Mr. and Mrs. C. E. lliggin, clock Mrs. Bradley, lace square Miss Barnes, hat pin stand Mr. Harold Kirkconnel, afternoon teaspoons Mr. and Mrs. Dimmelow' silver cream jug Miss Munro, fancy work Misses Isaac, fancy work Mrs. Guest, silver scent bottle Miss Guest, handkerchief Mr. Bagnall, bronze ornaments Mr. and Mrs. H. Milling, silver photo frame Mr. and Mrs. James Taylor, lace brooches Mr. and Miss Mary Thornely„ silver salad servers</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>Thomson--Banks.</head>
        <p>Thomson--Banks.</p>
        <p>The people of Latchford and district evinced much interest in the wedding on Thursday of Miss Alice Banks, younger daughter of Mr. D. Banks, EversleY, Grappenhall, to Mr. John Thomson, chief assistant master at Immanuel Boys' School, Birmingham, and son of Mr. James Thomson of Lancaster. The ceremony took place at Christ Church,with which Mr. Banks and his family have long been closely associated: The Rev. J. Banks, vicar of Whitbecik, Cumberland (uncle of bride), performed the ceremony, assistiset by the Rev. G. C. Briggs, vicar of Christ Church. There was a large gathering of friends inside the church, where Mr. John Moss of St. Michael's-on-Wyre, uncle of the bride, presided at the organ and played "The Voice that breathed o'er Eden," " 0 Perfect Love," Mendelssolan's " Wedding March," and other appropri- ate music. Miss Banks was given away by her father, and was tastefully attired in cream silk alpaca. She wore a gold bangle, and carried a shower bouquet of white carnations and white sweet peas, the gifts of the bridegroom. The bridesmaids were Miss Annie Banks, sister of the bride, and Miss Caroline Thomson, sister of the bridegroom. They wore fancy voile gowns, and carried bouquets of pale pink roses. Their ornaments included dress rings set with rubies and garnets, the gifts of the bridegroom. Mr. C. A. Fisher, of Lancaster,acted as best manovith Mr.W. G. Limb, Latchford, as groomsman. After the ceremony, a reception was held at the residence of the bride's father. Mrs. Thomson later left for London find Worthing, travelling in a blue costume with hat to match. .</p>
        <p>------- . The fcllowing is a list of the presents : Father and mother of bride, piano and household linen Teachers and scholars3mmanuel school, Birmingham, pair of pictures Rev. and Mrs. J. Banks, cutlery Mr. Walter H. Banks, marble clock Miss Annie Banks, eider down quilt Miss Atkinson and Girls' Society, sideboard cloth and silver cake basket Mrs. Deacon and girls' Bible class, silver epergne Mr. Herbert Banks, Chesterfield couch Mr. Ernest Banks, wicker chair Master Thomas Banks, crumb tray and brush Master Rowland Banks, kerb Mr. and Mrs. George, oxidised plant pot Mr. and Mrs. Hardy, carver rests Mr. and Mrs. W. Starkey, sot of fruit dishes Mr. Arthur Starkey;picture Misses Clark (Glasgow), two rose bowls Mr. and Mrs. Watson, cheque Mr. W. R. Robinson. dinner service Miss Lily Blackburn, plant pot Mr. and Mrs. IL Taylor, copper kettle Miss Chappell, breakfast cruet Miss Ronald, plant pot Miss J. Atkinson, brass photo frame Miss Carrie Thomson, embroidered nightdress Mr. and Mrs. F. Clifre,silver-mounted photo frame Miss Mason, table centre Mr. and Mrs. R. T. Bridge, picturo Miss B,nirdruore, picture Miss E Holland, embroidered cloth Mr. and Mrs. Bates, marmalade jar Mr. and Mrs. Turton, silver fruit dish Mrs. Brailey, brass stand Miss Coxon, fruit dish Mr. and Mrs. W. Longbottom, tea service Misses Parkinson, tea pot and stand Mr. W. G. Limb, barometer Mrs. and Miss Valiant, bed spread Miss Fountain' aluminium photo frame Rev, and Mrs. F. A. Screeton, Worcester vase Miss West and Mr. Clapham, table cloth Miss Alice Hardman, clock Miss Hannah Hardman, tray cloth Mr. and Mrs. J. Moss, table Miss Place, water colour Master H. E. Hankey, jug and glasses Mr. F. G. Ellison, silver-mounted biscuit jar Mr. and Mrs. G. Hardman, ornaments Mr. W. A. Hardman, Devonshire China jelly dish Mr. James Valiant, silver-mounted tea pot stand Mr. and Mrs. Warburton and family, carved oak cabinet Mr. and Mrs. J. Thompson, silver-mounted inkstand Mrs. Kerfoot. pincushion Mr. Thomas Moss, pair of water colours Rev. and Mrs. G. C. Briggs, Whittier's Poems Mr. and Mr. Holt, teaspoons, pickle fork, &amp;c. Miss Pollie Thomson, jam dish and silver stand Mrs. R. Hallows, cushion cover Miss Mary Moss and Mr. J. Stuart, case of teaspoons ad tongs Miss Ethel Starkey, lace and embroidered duches.se set Mr. R. Fisher, china teapot and sugar basin Mr. C. A. Fisher, damask table cloth Miss Emily Knight, hand painted plaque Robson—Miller. -</p>
        <p>The wedding took place on Wednesday at St. John's Church, Menston in Wharfedale, of Mr. E. S. A. Robson, M.Sc. (master of the Warrington Technical Institute) and son of Mr. John Robson of Sale, to Katheline Leonora, younger daughter of the late Henry Miller, of Sale, and Mrs. Miller of Menston in Wharfedale. The ceremony was performed by the I Rev. T. Nicholas Dunscombe (Vicar).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>GRAMMAR SCHOOL SPEECH DAY.</head>
        <p>GRAMMAR SCHOOL SPEECH DAY.</p>
        <p>HEADMHTER'S REPORT. The Work of the School. Speech Day at the Warrington Grammar School is always an important event in the history of the school, but this year additional interest was lent to the occa- sion by the fact that it was the first occasion at which the new headmaster, the Rev. Horace Gray, presented his report of the work of the institution. The event fell a little later this year owing to the new arrange- ments for the holidays, and it was noticeable that several of the pupils were away, evidently on holiday with their parents ; therefore they were unable to receive their prizes or take part in the ceremony. The Parr Hall was this year requisitioned for the ceremony,it being thought that this large and commodious building was more adaptable for the occasion, and permitted of a wider circle 9! friends being invited. The proceed- ings were presided over by an old boy in the person of Mr. C. S. Burgess, who was accompanied on the platform (which had been tastefully decorated with plants and improvised as a stage) by the Rev. Horace Gray, head- master, the Rev. Dr. B. Pollock (master of Wellington College and chaplain-in-ordinary to th 3 King), Dr. Sedgefleld, Nfanchester University), Rev. E. C. E. Carleton), Messrs. W. Peter Rylands, Vera B. Davies, Henry Roberts and Alderman Bennett. There was an excellent attendance of parents and friends,the scholars being assembled in the centre gallery. CHAIRMAN'S ADDRESS. •</p>
        <p>The CHAIRM AN in opening the proceedings regretted the absence of the senior co-opted governors of the school. Letters of regret for non-attendance had been received from Lord Lilford, Mr. Roger Parr, and Alderman Forshatv. Ile pointed out that the governors bad re-elected senior Boteler scholars. Messrs,</p>
        <p>... _ Brown, Burke, Bunch, Hands, Lawton and Stone, whilst on the recommendation of the examiner, senior Boteler scholarsirips had been awarded to Messrs. Stansfield and Weaver. He extended to his hearers a cordial .welcome.</p>
        <p>- It marked an epoch in their history. It had been the invariable custom to hold the Speech Day in the sdhool and as an old boy," Speech Day "had been robbed of much of the sentiment by the change which had been made, but as a governor, he saw it was inevitable. The year just closed had been one of considerable anxiety to tbe governing body. fly the death of their late headmaster, who devoted his whole lifetime to the school and to whose memory he had previously paid a fitting tribute,they were faced with serious respon- sibility in having to provide for the carrying on of the school until a new appointment could be made. In the selection of the Rev. Horace Gray they had succeeded in obtaining for the school a headmaster win) would ably' and fitly maintain its best traditions. He came with an excellent record of attain- ments and work and he confidently believed he would bring honour to himself and increased success and prosperity to tbe school. (Applause.) They had had other problems of a different character to solve; the regulations for secondary schools issued by the Board of Education,both last and this year,had made important changes. It had been most difficult to decide on the best course to adopt, having regard to the trust under which their foundation was governed. The chief point they had bad to consider was the point with regard to denominational teaching, the constitution of the governing body, the revision of the tuition fees, and the number of free places to be assigned to boys coming the elementary schools. To all of those things they had given careful attention and he believed they had arrived at a conclusion satisfactory and honourable to themselves, and which would not in any way lower the prestige of that school. It was a matter of regret that a number of boys were absent from to-day's ceremony owing to the parents having gone on holidays. He believed there were boys absent that day, who should have been there to receive their prizes. He hoped that the mere mention of that fact would have the effect of inducing parents to consider what was right in the interest of the school. It was an absolute necessity _ . .</p>
        <p>that their sons should take the full curriculum. The specialization of boys below 6th form was not advis- able, and parents should not encourage their sons in doing what was evidently a shirking of work. He con- gratulated the Headmaster and the stall on the year's work. (Applause.) THE HEADMASTER'S SPEECH.</p>
        <p>The HEADMASTER, who had a very cordial reception on rising to make his first report said, it is with much pleasuee I rise on this occasion, the first speech day of my headmastership to present my report on the life and progress of the school during the past year. You will I confidently expect, desire to learn from ma that so ancient an institute is doing good and useful work, and that this is so I can now assure you, as also those parents who have entrusted the education of their sons to our charge. The school, so prudently and carefully guided by the late Headmaster, whose name and work must of necessity live long in the minds of old Botelarians, gained a prominent place amongst the more important Grammar Schools in the country. If curriculum was based, and very wisely so on sound classical lines, and it is mysincero hope that the wisdom of that policy may be remarked and accepted by parents generally. The successes of last year in the Oxford Local Examinations point clearly to the high standard attained on the classical side. Never before had the school merited so many distinctions in the various subjects. But when we consider the modern tendency and utility of education in this country very consider- able stress and importance is attached to sciences. Its must be the duty of the school to raise to a similar high standard in mathematics and scientific subjects taught. In order to accomplish this adequate provision must be made. As our -examiner recently remarked to me, the school has not made these her own subjects, and again in his report refers to the restricted range and deficiency of this branch of study. I, personally, see no reason why a very much higher standard and wider range should not be aimed at without impairing the excellence of the classics. This should be one of our aims during the coming year. It will be readily recognised that as the school year was well advanced when I assumed charge of her, no, what I might term structural altera- tions were politic or could be contemplated. The most important fact then was to alleviate the congestion in various departments, and this I think we satisfactorily accomplished. It was possible with certain restrictions to place the specialist in charge of his own subject or subjects and very beneficial result should in the near future accrue from this policy. I have submitted to the Board of Educa- tion a scheme of work for this next year which is based on these lines. Thus each master e ill be fully engaged in raising the standard of those subjects which he has made his own. The regulations for Secondary Schools juive recently laid more stress upon the importance of English subjects and these in consequence receive fuller recognition at our hands. The text books which L we are to adopt this next term will permit of these subjects being treated in a more scholarly and efficient manner. The teaching of modern languages is founded on the right lines, oral French and German finding their place in our time table. The Examiner refers to the excellence of these subjects, which are of so great importance in our commercial life. It may be well for me to mention what will be a new feature of our cur- riculum this next year. The school having fully accepted the regulations for secondary schools will now provide for special religious instruction in accordance with the teaching of the Church of England. The trust under which the school is administered demands this, and thus, those boys whose parents signify in writing their desire that such 'instruction shall be given, will receive this opecial teaching in the ordinary school hours. Parents will remember the circular letter of the Governors which they received in May last, referring to this distinctive teaching. The replies, received showed that a very large percentage of parents were desirous that this teaching should be provided. The intentions of our pious founder Will in this way still then be respected, Of course the conscience clause will still be recognised and other provision for religious instruction such as the study of one of the historical books of the Old Testament will be made 'for those boys who do not receive the special instruction just referred to. There Is another point which bears directly upon the scheme of government of the school. The i Board of Education, n order to secure uniformity in Secondary schools ask that we make certain alterations in the matter of schools, fees and that we afford a proportionate number of places for free scholars who may be received cn examination from elementary schools. The tuition fees are to be arranged on a different basis, viz., that of age, and must cover the provision of all educational apparatus with the excep- tion of printed books and mathematical instruments. This important change was yesterday under the careful consideration of the Governors, and a new scale was formulated to be submitted to the Board for thelr sanc- tion. Parents will be notified in due course of the scale which come into operation next term. This will not affect boys who are at present in the school. It may at first sight appear as though this were a revolu- tionary move, but I shall have made my point, if parents grasp the fact that the motive power comes from White- hall. Turning to the statistics of the bast year, there are pleasing features which must substantiate our con- tention that valuable work has been and is still being done. In the Oxford Local Examination the Senior Honours List contained the names of Sterling (religious knowledge and Greek), Wiloax (Religious knowledge), and Greaves (Latin) in the First Class, these names representing all our candidates in that division. In the Junior Browne (French, Latin and Greek), Isherwood (Latin, Greek), Bunch (min, Greek) and Thomas (religious knowledge and Greek) secured first-class honours. Lawton (Latin), Weaver, Rigby (Greek) and Stansfield were placed in the second class. Hee satisfied the , examiners: Bunch maj, Collins, Crampton, Henshall and Rose. Four others, Boulton., Locker, rurner and Young, were placed in the Pass Division. These could not oompete for Honours on account of the age limit. No boy has sat for any University Scholarship, an omission which I think the near future will see rectified. There are, I am glad to say—and the Examiner confirms my view—several members of the 6th Form who are showing promise and we are hopeful that ther names will ere long figure in the University Honours list. Dawson, who goes to Oxford this next term, has, I</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <p>BM pleased to say, been awarded a grant of £3O from WRITING.—Forra 1., L. F. Unsworth. the Bishop of Liverpool's Fund and our own Exhibition GREEK TESTAMENT.—(Presented by Rev. Canon Fund whmh is so generously supported by our friends is Morley Stevenson), F. Browne. able to assist that grant. Amongst Old Batelarians who I LATIN VERSE.—(Presented by Rev. Canon Willis), have done honour to the school we make mention of . Wildox. 0. H. Cooke, who was placed on the 3rd Class B.C.L. NATURAL lIISTORY.—G. 0. Venn. Oxford, and called to the Bar in January of this year. Asfiros MEMORCAL.—Lawton. L. G. Corney obtained a 2nd Class in the Classical OXFORD LOCAL EXAMINATION, 1907. tripos at Cambridge. SENIORS HONOURS DIVISION. S. Ilenshall was awarded the Holt Scholarship of £5O FIRST CLASS.—J. A. G. Sterling, distinction for architecture at Liverpool University. R. Lee Religious Knowledge and Greek; R. Wilcox, distinc-4. secured the Gee Scholarship £25 at the same University tion in Religious Knowledge ; G. F. Greaves, distinc-11 and passed the Ist M.B. and C.H.B. titan in Latin. %V. B. Forshaw passed the final examination of the I SECOND CLASS.—J. H. Dawson. • Law Society and also obtained a 2nd class at the THIRD CLASS.—W. M. Watkins. Honours Examinations. F. G. Dunn passed the final JUNIORS HONOURS DIVISION. of the Institute of chartered accountants. E. Gandy FIRST CL ' ASS :—B. Browne distinction in French, has passed thte final M.B. at Manchester University. Latin, Greek; It. Isherwood, distinction in Latiori H. Dutton who is in residence at Cambridge his had Greek; J. Bunch, distinction in Greek; E. A. Thomasi his exhibition augmented. Religious Knowledge and Greek. In accordance with our custom the school has, clarity, SECOND CLASS :—B. E. 11. Lawton, distinciion ini Latin; W. H. Rigby, distinction in Greek; R W-4 the past ten dills been examined by the Osforn University Delegacy. The rep3rt which I have read Weaver and F. 0. Stansfield. deals fully with the working of the school and should c .3ATISFIED THE ExaAt MRS :—F. Bunch, S. , very materially assist us during the next session. It Collins, P. Crampton, L. Henshall A. Rose. :. . , . . , emphasises our strong paints and also indicates what.° PASS DIVISION —E VP Boulton T C Locker Eh we may hope to improve ourselves. Turner, W. Young. To give a few extracts from the report :—" The strength of the school at the present time is—as it has been traditionally in past years—in the classical and literary work, which in the sixth form is thoroughly sound and satisfactory." "The mathematical work offered in the examination has been sorne‘vhat restricted in range. It will probably be desirable and for practical reasons inevitable to extend this range at the expense of some of the other subjects." .` The fourth and third forms are strong and promisieg. They will speedily provide recruits fully worthy of the best traditions of the higher forms. I saw no signs of weakness in any of the papers submitted to me." "The French and German work appears to one excellent in every way." I am sure the examiner will forgive me, for supplementing the above extracts, by quoting a phraes contained in one of his letters to me, viz. :—" That so much classical work should have flourished with wire mills grinding ail around you, is all bat miraculous." These lengthy and perhaps not too interesting remarks, must, I venture to think, clearly represent to you how largely I an indebted to the staff, which laboured so long under my predecessor. I I would take this opportunity of thanking my colleagues for the exceedingly kind way in which they received me and for the support that they have extended to me. I feel I may confidently look forward to a practically united staff ell working for the best interests of the school. It now remains for me to tender my sincere thanks to the governing body for their generous and whole-hearted aid in promoting such reforms as I have considered it my duty to suggest. A headmaster—and the master of Wellington College, who was so I largely responsible for my introduction to %Varrington I will bear me out in this—enjoys no sinecure, but sup- ported as I am, both by my governing body and staff, the duties attached to my position are rendered far I less exacting than they would otherwise be. It would I not be fitting for me to close my report withoutacknow- iledging the extreme kindness of Dr. Pollock for having I at no small inconvenience to himself come to distribute the prizes. It was my pleasure to serve Dr. Pollock in a subordinate capacity and the experience I gained during my time at Wellington College, has proved of , very great value to me. There will, I am sure, be no need for me to ask my present pupils to extend him a very hearty welcome. (Applause). After distributing the prizes the Rev. B. Pomona.' addressed the gathering and gave some sound advice to the boys as to the choosing of their vocations. He congratulated the prize winners on the beautiful books they had received and the distinction they had wan in receiving those prizes. He had seen several s .hool prize days, but he could say to the winners that he i doubted whether any day n their future life would r come up to the joy which they had experienced that day in receiving those volumes in the presence of their fathers and mothers and their friends. He hoped that I the winners would hereafter receive greater distinction in larger fields, and he ventured to prophesy if those Idistinctions should come to them elsewhere they would I then miss the winning look of gladness in the faoe of their father or mother which really constituted their greatest joy.He wished to impress upon the unsuccessful students that the knowledge acquired by the prize winners was also shared by them. He dare say that only a few marks distinguished one from the other, and those few marks came to very little in the matter of knowledge I they had achieved. In another respect they had shared the greater and valaable gift of discipline, the discipline of learning, the disciplining of their minds, and learning to teach themselves that anything to be done success- fully must cost an effort. There was no short out to the things which really mattered for the most in this world, *elf sacrificing industry, and self control, and that devotion to effort must go hand in hand with prize seeking and prize getting. That was a precious gift which no examiner could give them, or any bad examiner could rob them of. It was a pleasure to him to see Mr. Gray at home working happily, comfortably and successfully in Warrington. Mr. Gray had been kind enough to allow him to see a back number of the school magazine, and he (the speaker) was inter- ested in reading there, that it was required that the Headmaster should be an honest man and learned in grammar. He hoped that the months which had elapsed since Mr. Gray's appointment had fairly well convinced them that be was an honest man and learned in grammar; certainly they detected no slips in his speech. If he I might prophesy he ventured to say that they-would soon learn that Idr. Gray was much more than an honest man and learned in grammar; but that he would be one to whom his pupils could go to, knowing they would receive the best highest and wisest advice, and also one to whom the parents would find they could turn to with increased confidence. He was once in conversation with a Bishop whe remarked that it was to be lamented I that the boys and young men of the present day entered upon their professions without any sense of vocation. They either adopted them on selfish grounds or they drifted into them by mere accident. He was of the opinion that a boy was rather a prig who selected his future work merely by looking round and seeing whom he could help and thinking that position was- marked ont-Tor him. It must be clearly understood that the meaning of the true greatness of a profession was derived not from the importance or advancement of one worker or another but from the work itself. However humble their task might be,how- ever small their part might be in the sphere before them, if they kept their minds fisted upon the greater work of which they were really a part then their work became great. (Applause.) Mr. HENRY ROBERTS, in proposing a vote of thanks to the Rev. B. Pollock, said he would like to take the opportunity of asking for the sympathy and friendly consideration of the parents and students towards their Headmaster, who had only just come amongst them. He had taken on a very responsible and onerous task. (Applause.) Everybody who had experienced the diffi- culty which surrounded a man who came to take charge of any department, whether commercial cr otherwise, who found a number of well fixed ideas as to the con- duct of that department, had many difficulties to over- come. The school was .now entering upon what he thought was the most important epoch an its career. As they knew the Corporation had had representatives uaon the governing body now for a number of years. The association between the municipality and the school was now infinitely closer than it had over pre- viously been. The school had to take its proper position in the: scheme of edacation, and proportionately to the i interest of the people of Warrington n that school, and the support the Corporation gave to the school, so would be the usefulness of that school to the town generally, and its influence be felt upon generations to come. (Applause.) Mr. W. PETER RYLANDS seconded, and said he hoped there were many boys amongst the prize winners who would rise to positions which would do credit to the school in which they were taught. (Applause). The vote of thanks was carried with unanimity. At the conclusion of the prize giving the scholars proceeded to entertain the guests, plays in French and Greek being presented. The boys acquitted themselves very creditably. The following was the programme and dramatis personae DANS LE PIEGE. (Trapped.) Comedy in One Act. Joseph Pommeau (dragoon... ... Madame Labouret •.. Caroline (her housemaid) ... Chiffonnett (burglar)... Police-Constable</p>
